{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01XhNmx9GDNoMAbANbM7xp2y","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"RMS shear strain maximum shear strain 1D seismic site response analysis equivalent uniform strain DOI\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"An improved notion for the computation of strain ratio in equivalent linear site response | Bulletin of Earthquake Engineering\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s10518-024-02061-1\"},{\"title\":\"Numerical Modelling of Seismic Site Response at Large Strains: A Parametric Study, American Journal of Civil Engineering, Science Publishing Group\",\"url\":\"https://sciencepublishinggroup.com/article/10.11648/10051269\"},{\"title\":\"Simplified Model for Small-Strain Nonlinearity and Strength in 1D Seismic Site Response Analysis | Journal of Geotechnical and Geoenvironmental Engineering | Vol 142, No 9\",\"url\":\"https://ascelibrary.org/doi/10.1061/%28ASCE%29GT.1943-5606.0001496\"},{\"title\":\"(PDF) Comparison of Equivalent Linear and Nonlinear Site Response Analysis Results and Model to Estimate Maximum Shear Strain\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/285619629_Comparison_of_Equivalent_Linear_and_Nonlinear_Site_Response_Analysis_Results_and_Model_to_Estimate_Maximum_Shear_Strain\"},{\"title\":\"Strain-threshold- and frequency-dependent seismic simulation of nonlinear soils | Earthquake Science | Springer Nature Link\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s11589-014-0102-z\"},{\"title\":\"(PDF) Ground Motion Parameter Sensitivity to Shear Strain Ratio in Equivalent Linear Analyses\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/284766296_Ground_Motion_Parameter_Sensitivity_to_Shear_Strain_Ratio_in_Equivalent_Linear_Analyses\"},{\"title\":\"RSSeismic | 1D Seismic Site Response Analysis Software - Rocscience\",\"url\":\"https://www.rocscience.com/software/rsseismic\"},{\"title\":\"Comparison of Seismic Site Factor Models Based on Equivalent Linear and Nonlinear Analyses and Correction Factors for Updating Equivalent Linear Results for Charleston, South Carolina\",\"url\":\"https://www.mdpi.com/2076-3263/15/4/115\"},{\"title\":\"(PDF) Evaluation of One-Dimensional Site Response Techniques Using Borehole Arrays\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/281214341_Evaluation_of_One-Dimensional_Site_Response_Techniques_Using_Borehole_Arrays\"},{\"title\":\"Site-Specific Dynamic Ground Response Analysis - March 2022\",\"url\":\"https://dot.ca.gov/-/media/dot-media/programs/engineering/documents/geotechnical-services/202203-gm-sitespecificdgra-a11y.pdf\"}]\n\nBased on the web search results, here's what I found related to your query about RMS shear strain, maximum shear strain, equivalent linear analysis, and equivalent uniform strain in 1D seismic site response analysis:\n\n## Key Findings:\n\n**Equivalent Linear Site Response Analysis (EQLSRA):**\nThe geotechnical engineering community widely uses one-dimensional (1D) Equivalent Linear Site Response Analysis (EQLSRA).\n\n**Strain Ratio and Equivalent Shear Strain:**\nFor a given strain time history, the corresponding Equivalent Shear Strain (ESS) is taken as Strain Ratio (STR) times the absolute peak strain, with a constant value usually ranging from 0.40 to 0.75 and typically 0.65 manually set as STR.\n\n**Equivalent Uniform Strain Procedure:**\nThe equivalent linear procedure is an iterative procedure to obtain values for the modulus and damping ratio that are compatible with equivalent uniform strain induced in each sublayer.\n\n**Maximum Shear Strain Computation:**\nThe maximum observed shear strain is used to compute the effective shear strain, and the G and ξ corresponding to the effective shear strain values are estimated based on the input G/Gmax-γ and D-γ curves.\n\n**Applicability Range:**\nEquivalent linear seismic site response analyses are easier to use than nonlinear methods and accurate up to shear strains of about 0.4-1%.\n\nThe search results contain several relevant research papers, including a recent 2024 article in the *Bulletin of Earthquake Engineering* that specifically addresses improved methods for computing strain ratios in equivalent linear analyses.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_011wm6d9hWxbGBCfH6BoLdbY","session_id":"2e84c092-3450-428f-a602-fb47a8d85d56","uuid":"82da946e-4785-468d-833e-48f42245a582","timestamp":"2026-03-29T20:01:43.418Z"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01PXcmtCmjhRHJnbCDqPkcmY","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"Dobry 1982 pore pressure increment PGA earthquake magnitude empirical method liquefaction DOI\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"BSSA 106:3, Electronic Supplement to Bastin et al.\",\"url\":\"https://www.seismosoc.org/Publications/BSSA_html/bssa_106-3/2015166-esupp/\"},{\"title\":\"Energy-Based Methods for Seismic Soil Liquefaction: Past, Present, and Future | Indian Geotechnical Journal | Springer Nature Link\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s40098-026-01480-y\"},{\"title\":\"Estimating Earthquake-Induced Pore Water Pressure: Hierarchy of Existing Methods\",\"url\":\"https://www.mdpi.com/2076-3263/16/2/61\"},{\"title\":\"7 Empirical and Semiempirical Methods for Evaluating Liquefaction Consequences | State of the Art and Practice in the Assessment of Earthquake-Induced Soil Liquefaction and Its Consequences | The National Academies Press\",\"url\":\"https://nap.nationalacademies.org/read/23474/chapter/9\"},{\"title\":\"Adjustment of a numerical model for pore pressure generation during an earthquake - PMC\",\"url\":\"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6762144/\"},{\"title\":\"What is the Smallest Earthquake Magnitude that Needs to be Considered in Assessing Liquefaction Hazard? - Russell A. Green, Julian J. Bommer, 2019\",\"url\":\"https://journals.sagepub.com/doi/10.1193/032218EQS064M\"},{\"title\":\"Nationalacademies\",\"url\":\"https://www.nationalacademies.org/read/23474/chapter/6\"},{\"title\":\"RECOMMENDED GUIDELINES FOR LIQUEFACTION EVALUATIONS USING GROUND MOTIONS FROM\",\"url\":\"https://www.oregon.gov/ODOT/GeoEnvironmental/Docs_GeologyGeotech/OSU_PSHA-Liquefaction-Eval.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Read \\\"State of the Art and Practice in the Assessment of Earthquake-Induced Soil Liquefaction and Its Consequences\\\" at NAP.edu\",\"url\":\"https://www.nationalacademies.org/read/23474/chapter/4\"},{\"title\":\"Estimation of Excess Pore Pressure Generation and Nonlinear Site Response of Liquefied Areas | IntechOpen\",\"url\":\"https://www.intechopen.com/chapters/68777\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, I found relevant information about the Dobry 1982 study on pore pressure prediction and liquefaction. Here are the key findings:\n\n## Publication Details\n\nDobry et al. (1982) published \"Prediction of pore water pressure buildup and liquefaction of sands during earthquakes by the cyclic strain method\" in NBS Building Science Series 138, published by the National Bureau of Standards in Gaithersburg, Maryland in 1982.\n\n## Key Concepts from the Method\n\nThe Dobry et al. 1982 work established that there exists a threshold shear strain below which excess pore pressures are not expected to develop, irrespective of the number of loading cycles. Data compiled by Dobry and Ladd (1980) suggest that the relationship between pore pressure and cyclic shear strain is relatively insensitive to initial variations in the soil fabric.\n\nThis empirical method based on cyclic strain represents an important foundational approach for predicting pore pressure buildup during earthquakes, though stress-based empirical models have become the standard of practice, despite remaining limited in capturing the cumulative and nonlinear effects of cyclic loading that govern pore pressure buildup.\n\nUnfortunately, the search results do not contain the specific DOI for this publication. You may need to consult the National Bureau of Standards archives or academic databases directly for the precise DOI.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_011wm6d9hWxbGBCfH6BoLdbY","session_id":"2e84c092-3450-428f-a602-fb47a8d85d56","uuid":"4e5f92d2-e348-43ba-b8c9-8868250953bc","timestamp":"2026-03-29T20:01:43.887Z"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01FHpGuxfGSDum53PYAfhFzL","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"PySeismoSoil pysra OpenSeesPy PM4Sand Python site response pore pressure analysis library\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"GitHub - Caltech-geoquake/PySeismoSoil: A Python library for performing 1D seismic site response analysis\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/Caltech-geoquake/PySeismoSoil\"},{\"title\":\"14.4.3. PM4Sand model undrained cyclic simple shear element — OpenSeesPy 3.5.1.3 documentation\",\"url\":\"https://openseespydoc.readthedocs.io/en/latest/src/pm4sand_cyc_cal.html\"},{\"title\":\"pySRA · PyPI\",\"url\":\"https://pypi.org/project/pySRA/\"},{\"title\":\"4.15.1.12. PM4Sand — OpenSeesPy 3.5.1.3 documentation\",\"url\":\"https://openseespydoc.readthedocs.io/en/latest/src/PM4Sand.html\"},{\"title\":\"Releases · Caltech-geoquake/PySeismoSoil\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/Caltech-geoquake/PySeismoSoil/releases\"},{\"title\":\"PySeismoSoil/PySeismoSoil/class_simulation.py at main · Caltech-geoquake/PySeismoSoil\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/Caltech-geoquake/PySeismoSoil/blob/main/PySeismoSoil/class_simulation.py\"},{\"title\":\"Readthedocs\",\"url\":\"https://openseespydoc.readthedocs.io/en/latest/_downloads/35553d5f6357928dad9defb1ed836789/PM4Sand_Cyc_Cal.py\"},{\"title\":\"14.4.2. Effective Stress Site Response Analysis of a Layered Soil Column — OpenSeesPy 3.5.1.3 documentation\",\"url\":\"https://openseespydoc.readthedocs.io/en/latest/src/freeFieldEffective.html\"},{\"title\":\"5. Examples — Quantified Uncertainty with Optimization for the FEM documentation\",\"url\":\"https://nheri-simcenter.github.io/quoFEM-Documentation/common/user_manual/examples/desktop/examples.html\"},{\"title\":\"Performing site response analysis in Jupyter - DesignSafe User Guide\",\"url\":\"https://designsafe-ci.org/user-guide/usecases/arduino/usecase_siteResponse/\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, here's what I found about these seismic site response analysis tools:\n\n## PySeismoSoil\n\nPySeismoSoil is a Python library for performing 1D seismic site response analysis. It is developed by the California Institute of Technology, based on research supported by the National Science Foundation (NSF) Cooperative Agreement EAR-1033462 and the U.S. Geological Survey (USGS) Cooperative Agreement G12AC20038. The authors of this library are the current and past members of the Geoquake Research Group of the California Institute of Technology: Jian Shi, Domniki Asimaki, Wei Li, and Flora Xia.\n\n## pySRA (pyStrata)\n\npySRA is a Python library for site response analysis.\n\n## PM4Sand and OpenSeesPy\n\nPM4Sand is a 2-dimensional sand plasticity material that can be constructed in OpenSeesPy. The PM4Sand constitutive model is available in OpenSees for modeling liquefiable soils. The search results show OpenSeesPy examples implementing PM4Sand for site response analysis with pore pressure analysis capabilities, including stress-strain and pore pressure plots for points in soil profiles.\n\nAll of these tools are designed for seismic site response analysis in Python, with PM4Sand available through OpenSeesPy for advanced geotechnical earthquake engineering applications including liquefaction and pore pressure analysis.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_011wm6d9hWxbGBCfH6BoLdbY","session_id":"2e84c092-3450-428f-a602-fb47a8d85d56","uuid":"42858e40-eb53-4559-9e56-394eb6283b3c","timestamp":"2026-03-29T20:01:44.534Z"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_018h1MfxFhfuxM91FLe39jZs","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"Iwasaki 1978 1982 Liquefaction Potential Index LPI depth weighting liquefaction severity DOI\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Real-time LPI-based Assessment of the Liquefaction\",\"url\":\"https://www.iitk.ac.in/nicee/wcee/article/WCEE2012_3356.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"LIQUEFACTION POTENTIAL INDEX: A CRITICAL ...\",\"url\":\"http://yo-1.ct.ntust.edu.tw/jge/files/articlefiles/v1i1200707031332604692.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"INTERNATIONAL SOCIETY FOR SOIL MECHANICS AND GEOTECHNICAL ENGINEERING\",\"url\":\"https://www.issmge.org/uploads/publications/1/45/06-technical-committee-09-tc203-26.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"A GLPI framework for gravelly soil liquefaction hazard assessment based on fuzzy mathematics - ScienceDirect\",\"url\":\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0013795225002303\"},{\"title\":\"Liquefaction potential index: Field assessment | U.S. Geological Survey\",\"url\":\"https://www.usgs.gov/publications/liquefaction-potential-index-field-assessment\"},{\"title\":\"Theoretical notes > Simplifid methods > Liquefaction potential index LPI\",\"url\":\"https://help.geostru.eu/liquiter/en/indice_potenziale_liquefazione.htm\"},{\"title\":\"Liquefaction potential index: Field assessment\",\"url\":\"https://pubs.usgs.gov/publication/70025796\"},{\"title\":\"1 2 3 4 Modification of the Liquefaction Potential Index to ...\",\"url\":\"https://repository.library.noaa.gov/view/noaa/51703/noaa_51703_DS1.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Liquefaction Hazard Assessment and Mapping Across the Korean Peninsula Using Amplified Liquefaction Potential Index\",\"url\":\"https://www.mdpi.com/2076-3417/16/2/612\"},{\"title\":\"Moving towards an improved index for assessing liquefaction hazard: Lessons from historical data - ScienceDirect\",\"url\":\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0038080615000827\"}]\n\nI found comprehensive information about the Iwasaki Liquefaction Potential Index (LPI). Here's a summary of the key findings:\n\n## Iwasaki Liquefaction Potential Index (LPI)\n\nLiquefaction potential index (IL) was developed by Iwasaki et al. in 1978 to predict the potential of liquefaction to cause foundation damage at a site.\n\n### Depth Weighting Function\n\nThe depth weighting factor, w(z) = 10 – 0.5z where z = depth (m). The weighting factor is 10 at z = 0, and linearly decreased to 0 at z = 20 m. A weighting function gives higher values to the layers closest to the ground surface, and decreases linearly to zero, at a depth of 20 m.\n\n### Liquefaction Severity Classification\n\nIwasaki et al. (1978, 1982) found that severe liquefaction and minor liquefaction are likely to occur whenever the LPI is greater than 15 and the LPI is less than 5, respectively. Liquefaction risk is very low if IL = 0; low if 0 < IL ≤ 5; high if 5 < IL ≤ 15; and very high if IL > 15.\n\n### Calibration and Development\n\nIwasaki et al. (1978, 1982) proposed the use of an index which is proportional to the thickness of the liquefiable layer and the value of the factor of safety against liquefaction of each layer. Iwasaki et al. (1982) calibrated the values of LPI with the severity of liquefaction induced damage using data mostly for sandy soils, as provided by 87 SPT borings in liquefied and non-liquefied sites in Japan.\n\nUnfortunately, the search results do not contain specific DOI citations for these seminal papers.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_011wm6d9hWxbGBCfH6BoLdbY","session_id":"2e84c092-3450-428f-a602-fb47a8d85d56","uuid":"698f554f-0d78-422b-82d8-1c5dc4152e2e","timestamp":"2026-03-29T20:01:47.985Z"}
