{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01XtNy3R2dD49L79JAieyVBF","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"Kottke Rathje 2008 \"pySRA\" equivalent linear site response strain ratio journal DOI\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"An improved notion for the computation of strain ratio in equivalent linear site response | Bulletin of Earthquake Engineering\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s10518-024-02061-1\"},{\"title\":\"PACIFIC EARTHQUAKE ENGINEERING RESEARCH CENTER Technical Manual for Strata\",\"url\":\"https://peer.berkeley.edu/sites/default/files/web_peer810_albert_r._kottke_ellen_m._rathje.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"(PDF) Influence of the effective strain ratio on equivalent linear ground response simulation\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/381475867_Influence_of_the_effective_strain_ratio_on_equivalent_linear_ground_response_simulation\"},{\"title\":\"(PDF) Comparison of Equivalent Linear and Nonlinear Site Response Analysis Results and Model to Estimate Maximum Shear Strain\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/285619629_Comparison_of_Equivalent_Linear_and_Nonlinear_Site_Response_Analysis_Results_and_Model_to_Estimate_Maximum_Shear_Strain\"},{\"title\":\"Relative Differences between Nonlinear and Equivalent-Linear 1-D Site Response Analyses - Byungmin Kim, Youssef M. A. Hashash, Jonathan P. Stewart, Ellen M. Rathje, Joseph A. Harmon, Michael I. Musgrove, Kenneth W. Campbell, Walter J. Silva, 2016\",\"url\":\"https://journals.sagepub.com/doi/10.1193/051215EQS068M\"},{\"title\":\"(PDF) Relative Differences between Nonlinear and Equivalent-Linear 1D Site Response Analyses\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/296472559_Relative_Differences_between_Nonlinear_and_Equivalent-Linear_1D_Site_Response_Analyses\"},{\"title\":\"Strata Manual Web Peer810 Kottke Rathje\",\"url\":\"https://www.scribd.com/doc/276057485/Strata-Manual-Web-Peer810-Kottke-Rathje\"},{\"title\":\"GitHub - arkottke/pystrata: Site Response Analysis Toolkit for Python · GitHub\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/arkottke/pystrata\"},{\"title\":\"pySRA · PyPI\",\"url\":\"https://pypi.org/project/pySRA/\"},{\"title\":\"CENA Special Issue Earthquake Spectra 2021, Vol. 37(S1) 1420–1439\",\"url\":\"https://www.daveboore.com/pubs_online/kottke-et-al-2021-ES.pdf\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, I found relevant information about the Kottke and Rathje 2008 work on equivalent linear site response analysis and strain ratio. Here's what I found:\n\nKottke AR and Rathje EM (2008) published a \"Technical manual for strata\" as Report No. 2008/10 from the Pacific Earthquake Engineering Research Center. This appears to be the reference you're looking for.\n\nRegarding the strain ratio in equivalent linear site response analysis: For a given strain time history, the equivalent shear strain is typically taken as the Strain Ratio (STR) times the absolute peak strain, with conventional values usually ranging from 0.40 to 0.75 and typically 0.65.\n\nThe search results show that Kottke and Rathje (2009) published the Technical Manual for Strata in PEER Report 2008/10 from the University of California, Berkeley. (Note: There appears to be a publication date discrepancy in the references between 2008 and 2009.)\n\nI also found that pySRA was later renamed to pyStrata, which is the Python-based equivalent linear site response analysis toolkit.\n\nUnfortunately, the search results do not contain the specific DOI for the 2008 Kottke and Rathje technical manual. For the complete DOI, you may want to check the PEER Center website directly or search DOI databases.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_011wm6d9hWxbGBCfH6BoLdbY","session_id":"2e84c092-3450-428f-a602-fb47a8d85d56","uuid":"ef4b59f0-3314-45e1-9f48-d00c1c6e1182","timestamp":"2026-03-29T20:01:57.966Z"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01JdWUH95VUXBpCViT6QVLCe","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"Seed Tokimatsu 1987 pore pressure ratio PGA cyclic stress ratio liquefaction earthquake DOI journal\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Volumetric strain values by using cyclic stress ratio and SPT on... | Download Scientific Diagram\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/figure/olumetric-strain-values-by-using-cyclic-stress-ratio-and-SPT-on-saturated-sands_fig1_330022961\"},{\"title\":\"REPORT NO. UCB/EERC-84/16 OCTOBER 1984 PB85-197837\",\"url\":\"https://nehrpsearch.nist.gov/static/files/NSF/PB85197887.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"NUREG/CR-5741 Technical Bases for Regulatory Guide for Soil Liquefaction\",\"url\":\"https://www.nrc.gov/docs/ml0037/ml003701612.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Settle3D Liquefaction Theory Manual © Rocscience Inc., 2023\",\"url\":\"https://static.rocscience.cloud/assets/verification-and-theory/Settle3D-Liquefaction-Theory-Manual.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"4 The Simplified Stress-Based Approach to Triggering Assessment | State of the Art and Practice in the Assessment of Earthquake-Induced Soil Liquefaction and Its Consequences | The National Academies Press\",\"url\":\"https://nap.nationalacademies.org/read/23474/chapter/6\"},{\"title\":\"Simplified procedures for the evaluation of settlements in sands due to earthquake shaking | Semantic Scholar\",\"url\":\"https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/Simplified-procedures-for-the-evaluation-of-in-due-Tokimatsu-Seed/66ecf27413ff5b2d8fa2467497dff6bed14d61b9\"},{\"title\":\"(PDF) Liquefaction and post-liquefaction settlement assessment — A probabilistic approach\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/233780555_Liquefaction_and_post-liquefaction_settlement_assessment_-_A_probabilistic_approach\"},{\"title\":\"Estimation of Excess Pore Pressure Generation and Nonlinear Site Response of Liquefied Areas | IntechOpen\",\"url\":\"https://www.intechopen.com/chapters/68777\"},{\"title\":\"A New Equation to Evaluate Liquefaction Triggering Using the Response Surface Method and Parametric Sensitivity Analysis | MDPI\",\"url\":\"https://www.mdpi.com/2071-1050/11/1/112\"},{\"title\":\"LIQUEFACTION POTENTIAL STUDY OF TAIWAN\",\"url\":\"https://www.iitk.ac.in/nicee/wcee/article/1310.pdf\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, I found information about the Tokimatsu and Seed 1987 research work. Here's what I found:\n\n## Tokimatsu and Seed 1987 Study\n\nTokimatsu and Seed (1987) proposed simplified procedures for estimating probable settlements in either saturated or unsaturated sand deposits subjected to earthquake shaking.\n\nThe primary factors controlling induced settlement are the cyclic stress ratio and maximum shear strain induced in saturated sands by the earthquake shaking and the cyclic strains induced in dry or partially saturated sands, together with the SPT N-value of the sand and the magnitude of the earthquake.\n\n## Journal Publication Details\n\nThe 1987 work by Tokimatsu and Seed was published in the Journal of Geotechnical Engineering, volume 113, pages 861–878.\n\n## Key Research Focus\n\nThe study presents a practical method for prediction of volumetric strains by use of cyclic stress ratio and SPT-N, when saturated sands are exposed to an earthquake of magnitude M=7.5.\n\nThe research dealt with relationships between pore pressure ratios, cyclic stress ratios (CSR), and liquefaction potential in earthquake-induced soil behavior. However, I was unable to locate the specific DOI from the search results. You may need to consult academic databases like Google Scholar, ResearchGate, or the Journal of Geotechnical Engineering directly for the DOI information.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_011wm6d9hWxbGBCfH6BoLdbY","session_id":"2e84c092-3450-428f-a602-fb47a8d85d56","uuid":"e34a5816-971f-4009-8244-fb98451afc52","timestamp":"2026-03-29T20:02:00.069Z"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01CjKvmGVHtHUydr2P6k214W","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"Iwasaki 1978 \"evaluation of liquefaction potential\" Technical Memorandum PWRI Japan original paper\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"LIQUEFACTION POTENTIAL INDEX: A CRITICAL ...\",\"url\":\"http://yo-1.ct.ntust.edu.tw/jge/files/articlefiles/v1i1200707031332604692.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"SIMPLIFIED METHOD IN EVALUATING LIQUEFACTION ...\",\"url\":\"https://www.iitk.ac.in/nicee/wcee/article/14_04-02-0011.PDF\"},{\"title\":\"Google Scholar\",\"url\":\"https://scholar.google.com/scholar_lookup?title=A+practical+method+for+assessing+soil+liquefaction+potential+based+on+case+studies+at+various+sites+in+Japan&amp=&author=T.+Iwasaki&amp=&author=F.+Tatsuoka&amp=&author=K.+Tokida&amp=&author=S+Yasuda&amp=&publication_year=1978&amp=&pages=885-896\"},{\"title\":\"Evaluation of Liquefaction Susceptibility of Soils in Kerala, India, Based on Equivalent N Value and Equivalent Acceleration | SpringerLink\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/chapter/10.1007/978-981-16-3383-6_14\"},{\"title\":\"Real-time LPI-based Assessment of the Liquefaction\",\"url\":\"https://www.iitk.ac.in/nicee/wcee/article/WCEE2012_3356.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Liquefaction Potential Assessment Using Different Criteria | SpringerLink\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/chapter/10.1007/978-981-96-1606-0_23\"},{\"title\":\"INTERNATIONAL SOCIETY FOR SOIL MECHANICS AND GEOTECHNICAL ENGINEERING\",\"url\":\"https://www.issmge.org/uploads/publications/1/45/06-technical-committee-09-tc203-26.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"(PDF) Calibration of liquefaction potential index: A re-visit focusing on a new CPTU model\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/248508632_Calibration_of_liquefaction_potential_index_A_re-visit_focusing_on_a_new_CPTU_model\"},{\"title\":\"Selecting the Optimal Factor of Safety or Probability of Liquefaction Triggering for Engineering Projects Based on Misprediction Costs | Journal of Geotechnical and Geoenvironmental Engineering | Vol 147, No 6\",\"url\":\"https://ascelibrary.org/doi/abs/10.1061/(ASCE)GT.1943-5606.0002511\"},{\"title\":\"Re-calibrating the thresholds for the classification of liquefaction potential index based on the 2004 Niigata-ken Chuetsu earthquake - ScienceDirect\",\"url\":\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S001379521300330X\"}]\n\nBased on my search results, I found information about the Iwasaki 1978 paper on liquefaction evaluation:\n\nThe paper is by Iwasaki, T., Tatsuoka, F., Tokida, K., and Yasuda, S. (1978), titled \"A practical method for assessing soil liquefaction potential based on case studies at various sites in Japan,\" presented at the Proceedings of the 2nd International Conference on Microzonation.\n\nAccording to another source, it was presented at the 5th Japan Earthquake Engineering Symposium, Vol II: 641-648.\n\nHowever, I should note that the search results do not specifically confirm this as a \"Technical Memorandum PWRI\" (Public Works Research Institute) publication. The Liquefaction Potential Index (IL) was developed by Iwasaki et al. in 1978 to predict the potential of liquefaction to cause foundation damage at a site. The paper became a seminal work in the field and has been widely cited in subsequent liquefaction evaluation research.\n\nIf you're looking for the original PWRI Technical Memorandum specifically, you may need to access Japanese geotechnical databases or archives directly, as my search did not locate that specific document designation.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_011wm6d9hWxbGBCfH6BoLdbY","session_id":"2e84c092-3450-428f-a602-fb47a8d85d56","uuid":"73140b8a-bf15-408e-8ad7-a90e4a9f1f1a","timestamp":"2026-03-29T20:02:00.557Z"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01NatKY6opf8crEESPgqra7V","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"Boulanger Ziotopoulou PM4Sand constitutive model sand liquefaction OpenSees DOI report\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"PM4SAND (VERSION 3): A SAND PLASTICITY MODEL ...\",\"url\":\"https://faculty.engineering.ucdavis.edu/boulanger/wp-content/uploads/sites/71/2014/09/Boulanger_Ziotopoulou_PM4Sand_Model_CGM-15-01_2015.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Implementation, Verification, and Validation of the PM4Sand Model in OpenSees, PEER Report 2021-02 | Pacific Earthquake Engineering Research Center\",\"url\":\"https://peer.berkeley.edu/publications/2021-02\"},{\"title\":\"New PEER Report 2021/02: \\\"Implementation, Verification, and Validation of the PM4Sand Model in OpenSees\\\" | Pacific Earthquake Engineering Research Center\",\"url\":\"https://peer.berkeley.edu/news/new-peer-report-202102-implementation-verification-and-validation-pm4sand-model-opensees\"},{\"title\":\"PM4Sand (Boulanger & Ziotopoulou) - SoilModels\",\"url\":\"https://soilmodels.com/pm4sand/\"},{\"title\":\"INTERNATIONAL SOCIETY FOR SOIL MECHANICS AND GEOTECHNICAL ENGINEERING\",\"url\":\"https://www.issmge.org/uploads/publications/1/120/20ICSMGE_SoA_7_Boulanger.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Comparison of Liquefaction Constitutive Models for a Hypothetical Sand\",\"url\":\"https://par.nsf.gov/servlets/purl/10041305\"},{\"title\":\"Home | PM4Sand\",\"url\":\"https://pm4sand.engr.ucdavis.edu/\"},{\"title\":\"Performance evaluation of PM4Sand model for simulation of the liquefaction remedial measures for embankment - ScienceDirect\",\"url\":\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0267726121004644\"},{\"title\":\"(PDF) PM4Sand Version 3: A Sand Plasticity Model for Earthquake Engineering Applications\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/273694951_PM4Sand_Version_3_A_Sand_Plasticity_Model_for_Earthquake_Engineering_Applications\"},{\"title\":\"Comparison of Liquefaction Constitutive Models for a Hypothetical Sand | NSF Public Access Repository\",\"url\":\"https://par.nsf.gov/biblio/10041305-comparison-liquefaction-constitutive-models-hypothetical-sand\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, I found relevant information about the PM4Sand constitutive model developed by Boulanger and Ziotopoulou:\n\n## Key Findings:\n\nThe sand plasticity model PM4Sand was developed for geotechnical earthquake engineering applications by Professors Ross W. Boulanger and Katerina Ziotopoulou at the University of California at Davis.\n\nPM4Sand is a stress-ratio controlled, critical state compatible, bounding surface plasticity model for sand (and other purely nonplastic granular soils).\n\n### OpenSees Implementation:\n\nA constitutive model, PM4Sand (Boulanger and Ziotopoulou, 2017), was implemented using the finite-element platform OpenSees. Chen and Arduino published \"Implementation, Verification, and Validation of the PM4Sand Model in OpenSees, PEER Report No. 2021/02\" from the Pacific Earthquake Engineering Research Center, University of California, Berkeley, CA.\n\n### Sand Liquefaction Applications:\n\nThe model was developed and implemented to approximate stress-strain responses of specific importance to geotechnical earthquake engineering and liquefaction problems. Developments are described in manuals (version 1 in Boulanger 2010, version 2 in Boulanger and Ziotopoulou 2012, and version 3) and associated publications.\n\nThe search results indicate that the PM4Sand model represents a comprehensive constitutive framework for simulating sand behavior under earthquake loading and liquefaction conditions, with availability in multiple numerical platforms including OpenSees, FLAC, and PLAXIS.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_011wm6d9hWxbGBCfH6BoLdbY","session_id":"2e84c092-3450-428f-a602-fb47a8d85d56","uuid":"d5d49293-31de-4543-afca-324e442b770b","timestamp":"2026-03-29T20:02:00.606Z"}
