{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01SYMJ7QHMkPQdDeyDy7KFUX","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"\"max-max\" \"RMS\" shear strain 1D site response equivalent linear DEEPSOIL SHAKE comparison approach\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Comparison of 1D linear, equivalent-linear, and nonlinear site response models at six KiK-net validation sites - ScienceDirect\",\"url\":\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0267726114002188\"},{\"title\":\"(PDF) Comparison of Equivalent Linear and Nonlinear Site Response Analysis Results and Model to Estimate Maximum Shear Strain\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/285619629_Comparison_of_Equivalent_Linear_and_Nonlinear_Site_Response_Analysis_Results_and_Model_to_Estimate_Maximum_Shear_Strain\"},{\"title\":\"Site-Specific Dynamic Ground Response Analysis - March 2022\",\"url\":\"https://dot.ca.gov/-/media/dot-media/programs/engineering/documents/geotechnical-services/202203-gm-sitespecificdgra-a11y.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Comparison of Seismic Site Factor Models Based on Equivalent Linear and Nonlinear Analyses and Correction Factors for Updating Equivalent Linear Results for Charleston, South Carolina\",\"url\":\"https://www.mdpi.com/2076-3263/15/4/115\"},{\"title\":\"Version 7.1 - DEEPSOIL\",\"url\":\"https://deepsoil.cee.illinois.edu/Files/DEEPSOIL_User_Manual_v7.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"A holistic equivalent linear method for site response analysis - ScienceDirect\",\"url\":\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0267726120311027\"},{\"title\":\"2D equivalent linear ground response analysis for randomized site profiles in: Pollack Periodica Volume 19 Issue 2 (2024)\",\"url\":\"https://akjournals.com/view/journals/606/19/2/article-p87.xml\"},{\"title\":\"(PDF) Comparison of equivalent-linear site response analysis software\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/272492218_Comparison_of_equivalent-linear_site_response_analysis_software\"},{\"title\":\"Benchmarking of Nonlinear Geotechnical Ground Response ...\",\"url\":\"https://peer.berkeley.edu/sites/default/files/web_peer804_onathan_p._stewart_annie_on-lei_kwok.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"1 Practical Use of Geotechnical Site Response Models\",\"url\":\"https://apps.peer.berkeley.edu/lifelines/Workshop304/pdf/p_Kramer.pdf\"}]\n\nBased on the web search results, I found relevant research comparing 1D site response analyses using equivalent-linear and nonlinear methods with DEEPSOIL and SHAKE. Here are the key findings:\n\n## Comparison of DEEPSOIL and SHAKE\n\nThe equivalent-linear site response program SHAKE and the nonlinear site response program DEEPSOIL have been employed in comparative studies of 1D site response.\n\n## Strain Levels and Model Performance\n\nAt peak shear strains from 0.01% to 0.1%, linear site response models fail to accurately predict short-period ground motions; equivalent-linear and nonlinear models offer a significant improvement at strains beyond this level, with nonlinear models exhibiting a slight improvement over equivalent-linear models at strains greater than approximately 0.05%.\n\nAdditionally, the accuracies of equivalent-linear and nonlinear models were generally similar, but the predictions deviate when maximum shear strains exceed ~0.3%.\n\n## Equivalent Shear Strain Approach\n\nIn SHAKE2000, the equivalent shear strain is generally 0.65 times of the maximum shear strain. This represents a key difference in how the equivalent-linear approach handles strain calculations compared to the direct maximum shear strain used in nonlinear analyses.\n\n## Complex Shear Modulus Formulation\n\nThe frequency-independent complex shear modulus formulation should be used in DEEPSOIL when performing equivalent-linear analysis, which is the same complex shear modulus formulation as used in SHAKE.\n\nThe research indicates that the choice between maximum shear strain and RMS (or equivalent) strain representations is critical for understanding differences between equivalent-linear and nonlinear approaches in 1D site response analysis.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_011wm6d9hWxbGBCfH6BoLdbY","session_id":"2e84c092-3450-428f-a602-fb47a8d85d56","uuid":"69e56a8f-e8b3-4317-abfb-2e64213cf685","timestamp":"2026-03-29T20:02:11.993Z"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01ALVAgVprPBBvJYdnAtWju4","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"Idriss Boulanger 2008 pore pressure ratio CSR cyclic resistance liquefaction empirical model DOI\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"SPT-BASED LIQUEFACTION TRIGGERING PROCEDURES\",\"url\":\"https://faculty.engineering.ucdavis.edu/boulanger/wp-content/uploads/sites/71/2014/09/Idriss_Boulanger_SPT_Liquefaction_CGM-10-02.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"CPT AND SPT BASED LIQUEFACTION TRIGGERING ...\",\"url\":\"https://www.ce.memphis.edu/7137/PDFs/Notes/i3Boulanger_Idriss_CPT_and_SPT_Liq_triggering_CGM-14-01_20141.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"INTERNATIONAL SOCIETY FOR SOIL MECHANICS AND GEOTECHNICAL ENGINEERING\",\"url\":\"https://www.issmge.org/uploads/publications/59/57/pbdiiiPaper282.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"1 Cyclic Shear Strain Needed for Liquefaction Triggering and\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/profile/Ricardo-Dobry/publication/279239866_Cyclic_Shear_Strain_Needed_for_Liquefaction_Triggering_and_Assessment_of_Overburden_Pressure_Factor_Ks/links/559a8f5d08ae5d8f3937dbc3/Cyclic-Shear-Strain-Needed-for-Liquefaction-Triggering-and-Assessment-of-Overburden-Pressure-Factor-Ks.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"CENTER FOR GEOTECHNICAL MODELING REPORT NO. UCD/CGM-14/01 CPT AND SPT BASED\",\"url\":\"https://faculty.engineering.ucdavis.edu/boulanger/wp-content/uploads/sites/71/2014/09/Boulanger_Idriss_CPT_and_SPT_Liq_triggering_CGM-14-01_20141.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"CPT-BASED LIQUEFACTION TRIGGERING PROCEDURE by\",\"url\":\"https://escholarship.org/content/qt6mh5q86w/qt6mh5q86w_noSplash_d58ad6abe6045f5518a5f19eba757445.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Settle3D Liquefaction Theory Manual © Rocscience Inc., 2023\",\"url\":\"https://static.rocscience.cloud/assets/verification-and-theory/Settle3D-Liquefaction-Theory-Manual.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"EVALUATING THE POTENTIAL FOR LIQUEFACTION OR ...\",\"url\":\"https://faculty.engineering.ucdavis.edu/boulanger/wp-content/uploads/sites/71/2014/09/Boulanger_Idriss_CGM04-01_2004.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"© 2025 Rocscience Inc. Settle3 Liquefaction Theory Manual\",\"url\":\"https://static.rocscience.cloud/assets/verification-and-theory/Settle3/Settle3-Liquefaction-Theory-Manual.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Soil liquefaction during earthquakes | Request PDF\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/288624104_Soil_liquefaction_during_earthquakes\"}]\n\nBased on the web search results, I found relevant information about the Idriss and Boulanger 2008 liquefaction model. Here are the key findings:\n\n## Document Reference\n\nThe primary reference is: Idriss, I. M., and Boulanger, R. W. (2008). Soil liquefaction during earthquakes. Monograph MNO-12, Earthquake Engineering Research Institute, Oakland, CA, 261 pp.\n\n## Model Overview\n\nIdriss and Boulanger published liquefaction triggering correlations in 2004 and 2008. These liquefaction triggering correlations compare the cyclic resistance ratio (CRR) adjusted for M = 7.5 and σ'v = 1 atm versus (N1)60cs.\n\n## Related Methodologies\n\nRecent updates to SPT-based procedures include those by Idriss and Boulanger (2008, 2010). The analysis framework is described in Idriss and Boulanger (2008), except for the revised magnitude scaling factor and triggering charts for use with the CPT.\n\n**Note:** The search results provide detailed information about the 2008 Idriss and Boulanger model, but I did not locate a specific DOI number in the available results. The monograph reference provided (MNO-12) is the standard citation for this work.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_011wm6d9hWxbGBCfH6BoLdbY","session_id":"2e84c092-3450-428f-a602-fb47a8d85d56","uuid":"768c24e1-902c-44ee-8845-c5b56be6e77e","timestamp":"2026-03-29T20:02:12.358Z"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01NoiAucd3tUrVLxmPDC1YWy","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"Zhu Boulanger Idriss 2006 \"liquefaction severity number\" LSN definition DOI\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Comparison of CPT-based simplified liquefaction ...\",\"url\":\"https://www.issmge.org/uploads/publications/89/100/12ANZ_082.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"(PDF) Calibrating the Liquefaction Severity Number (LSN) for Varying Misprediction Economies: A Case Study in Christchurch, New Zealand\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/283835735_Calibrating_the_Liquefaction_Severity_Number_LSN_for_Varying_Misprediction_Economies_A_Case_Study_in_Christchurch_New_Zealand\"},{\"title\":\"Assessing Liquefaction Susceptibility Using the CPT Soil Behavior Type Index\",\"url\":\"https://par.nsf.gov/servlets/purl/10051372\"},{\"title\":\"CPT AND SPT BASED LIQUEFACTION TRIGGERING ...\",\"url\":\"https://www.ce.memphis.edu/7137/PDFs/Notes/i3Boulanger_Idriss_CPT_and_SPT_Liq_triggering_CGM-14-01_20141.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"SPT-BASED LIQUEFACTION TRIGGERING PROCEDURES\",\"url\":\"https://faculty.engineering.ucdavis.edu/boulanger/wp-content/uploads/sites/71/2014/09/Idriss_Boulanger_SPT_Liquefaction_CGM-10-02.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Calibrating the Liquefaction Severity Number (LSN) for ...\",\"url\":\"https://secure.tcc.co.nz/ei/images/ICEGE15%20Papers/Maurer_491.00.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Selecting factor of safety against liquefaction for design ...\",\"url\":\"https://par.nsf.gov/servlets/purl/10134690\"},{\"title\":\"(PDF) Pipeline Damage Predictions in Liquefaction Zones Using LSN\",\"url\":\"https://www.academia.edu/75373004/Pipeline_Damage_Predictions_in_Liquefaction_Zones_Using_LSN\"},{\"title\":\"Appendix A : Assessment of Liquefaction Vulnerability in Christchurch\",\"url\":\"https://www.eqc.govt.nz/assets/Canterbury-earthquake-page-documents/ILV/Appendix-A.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"CENTER FOR GEOTECHNICAL MODELING REPORT NO. UCD/CGM-14/01 CPT AND SPT BASED\",\"url\":\"https://faculty.engineering.ucdavis.edu/boulanger/wp-content/uploads/sites/71/2014/09/Boulanger_Idriss_CPT_and_SPT_Liq_triggering_CGM-14-01_20141.pdf\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, I found information about the Liquefaction Severity Number (LSN) and its relationship to Boulanger and Idriss's work, though I should note that the specific 2006 publication you mentioned wasn't directly located. Here's what I found:\n\nThe Liquefaction Severity Number (LSN) is a metric used for evaluating liquefaction-induced land damage observations, and it's commonly applied in liquefaction assessment frameworks.\n\nThe search results show that the Idriss and Boulanger (2008) liquefaction evaluation procedure is used to compute the factor of safety against liquefaction (FSliq), which feeds into LSN calculations. While the LSN approach is now widely used in New Zealand, it is applicable to any hazard framework.\n\nAdditionally, LSN is a recently developed parameter that expresses liquefaction severity as repairs per kilometer, and areas with high LSN values spatially correlate well with areas of more severe land damage observations, while areas of low LSN values correlate well with areas of less severe land damage observations.\n\nHowever, the search results did not provide a specific DOI or the exact definition from a 2006 Zhu, Boulanger, and Idriss publication. The results primarily reference the 2008 Idriss and Boulanger monograph and later work. If you need the specific 2006 reference or DOI, you may want to search academic databases directly or provide additional context about that publication.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_011wm6d9hWxbGBCfH6BoLdbY","session_id":"2e84c092-3450-428f-a602-fb47a8d85d56","uuid":"53a3400b-9b59-4356-9b3d-8172162c7a5e","timestamp":"2026-03-29T20:02:13.229Z"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01T7EQpdzSpDgdaYwT8hd3Lm","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"Shi Asimaki PySeismoSoil 2018 2019 seismic site response California journal DOI publication\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"GitHub - Caltech-geoquake/PySeismoSoil: A Python library for performing 1D seismic site response analysis\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/Caltech-geoquake/PySeismoSoil\"},{\"title\":\"A Site Response Module Toolbox for the Broadband Platform: Implementation and Verification | Statewide California Earthquake Center\",\"url\":\"https://central.scec.org/publication/9906\"},{\"title\":\"SeismoSoil User Manual, v1.3\",\"url\":\"https://www.asimaki.caltech.edu/resources/software/SeismoSoil_manual_v1p3.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"A Site Response Module Toolbox for the Broadband Platform: Implementation and Verification | Southern California Earthquake Center\",\"url\":\"https://www.scec.org/publication/9906\"},{\"title\":\"Development and Verification of a Neural Operator Based Nonlinear Site Response Module for the San Francisco Bay Area | Statewide California Earthquake Center\",\"url\":\"https://central.scec.org/publication/14059\"},{\"title\":\"PySeismoSoil\",\"url\":\"https://pypi.org/project/PySeismoSoil/\"},{\"title\":\"Caltech-geoquake · GitHub\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/Caltech-geoquake\"},{\"title\":\"GitHub - Caltech-geoquake/SeismoSoil: Software with GUI for 1D seismic site response analysis · GitHub\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/Caltech-geoquake/SeismoSoil\"},{\"title\":\"A Generic Velocity Profile for Basin Sediments in California Conditioned on VS30 | Request PDF\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/325328674_A_Generic_Velocity_Profile_for_Basin_Sediments_in_California_Conditioned_on_VS30\"},{\"title\":\"Domniki Asimaki\",\"url\":\"https://scholar.google.com/citations?user=o5_Mbh8AAAAJ&hl=en\"}]\n\nBased on the web search results, I found relevant information about Shi and Asimaki's work on PySeismoSoil and seismic site response in California:\n\n## Key Publications\n\nJ. Shi and D. Asimaki published \"A Generic Velocity Profile for Basin Sediments in California Conditioned on Vs30\" in Seismological Research Letters, 89 (4), 1397-1409 in 2018.\n\nJian Shi and Domniki Asimaki published \"A Site Response Module Toolbox for the Broadband Platform: Implementation and Verification\" in August 2019 (SCEC Contribution #9906).\n\n## PySeismoSoil Software\n\nPySeismoSoil is a Python library for performing 1D seismic site response analysis. The open-source Python library PySeismoSoil can be used to compute: (1) synthetic empirical adjustments for nonlinear site response on BBP ground motion synthetics in the Western United States, (2) site-specific (wave-propagation based) nonlinear site response, and (3) randomized 1D shear wave velocity profiles.\n\n## Related Research\n\nJ. Shi and D. Asimaki also published \"From stiffness to strength: Formulation and validation of a hybrid hyperbolic nonlinear soil model for site-response analyses\" in the Bulletin of the Seismological Society of America, 107 (3), 1336-1355 in 2017.\n\nThe search results confirm work by Shi and Asimaki in the 2018-2019 timeframe focused on California seismic site response analysis and the development of PySeismoSoil.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_011wm6d9hWxbGBCfH6BoLdbY","session_id":"2e84c092-3450-428f-a602-fb47a8d85d56","uuid":"ecd59476-3511-4fc7-8c5d-e5cc5de93887","timestamp":"2026-03-29T20:02:14.348Z"}
