{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01R9QZSiW2WU5ppzbHQZPp4n","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"Seed De Alba 1986 \"excess pore pressure\" earthquake cyclic stress ratio simplified procedure journal ASCE DOI\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Estimating Earthquake-Induced Pore Water Pressure: Hierarchy of Existing Methods\",\"url\":\"https://www.mdpi.com/2076-3263/16/2/61\"},{\"title\":\"Excess pore water pressure generation in saturated sandy soils subjected to various cyclic stress paths - ScienceDirect\",\"url\":\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0267726123000490\"},{\"title\":\"Influences of particle size distribution and fines content on the excess pore water pressure generation of sand-silt mixtures under cyclic loading - ScienceDirect\",\"url\":\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0267726123004463\"},{\"title\":\"Effect of Excess Pore Pressure on Earthquake‐Induced Displacement of Partially Saturated Sandy Soil Slopes: Flexible Sliding Block Analysis - Zhang - 2025 - International Journal for Numerical and Analytical Methods in Geomechanics - Wiley Online Library\",\"url\":\"https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1002/nag.3855\"},{\"title\":\"Estimation of Excess Pore Pressure Generation and Nonlinear Site Response of Liquefied Areas | IntechOpen\",\"url\":\"https://www.intechopen.com/chapters/68777\"},{\"title\":\"Effects of Excess Pore Pressure Redistribution in Liquefiable Layers | Journal of Geotechnical and Geoenvironmental Engineering | Vol 150, No 4\",\"url\":\"https://ascelibrary.org/doi/abs/10.1061/JGGEFK.GTENG-11857\"},{\"title\":\"Earth Science Research; Vol. 1, No. 2; 2012 ISSN 1927-0542 E-ISSN 1927-0550\",\"url\":\"https://ccsenet.org/journal/index.php/esr/article/download/14298/11550\"},{\"title\":\"Energy-Based Pore Pressure Generation Models in Silty Sands under Earthquake Loading\",\"url\":\"https://www.mdpi.com/2076-3263/14/6/166\"},{\"title\":\"Insight into excess pore pressure generation leading to liquefaction of sand with stress history under saturated and unsaturated conditions - ScienceDirect\",\"url\":\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0038080622000956\"},{\"title\":\"Pore pressure model based on accumulated stress | Bulletin of Earthquake Engineering | Springer Nature Link\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s10518-014-9702-1\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, I found information related to your query about Seed and De Alba's work on excess pore pressure, cyclic stress ratio, and simplified procedures for earthquakes. The search results contain multiple references to the Seed model and related simplified procedures in ASCE journals.\n\nFrom the search results, the Seed model is used to characterize the growth of excess pore water pressure (EPWP) for clean sands, and the relationship between the EPWP ratio and the cycle ratio for sand-silt mixtures can be well expressed by the Seed model, which is significantly affected by cyclic stress ratio (CSR).\n\nThe search results show that existing simplified procedures for evaluating soil liquefaction potential or for estimating excess pore pressures during earthquakes are typically based on undrained cyclic tests performed on saturated soil samples under controlled loading and boundary conditions.\n\nHowever, the search results do not provide the specific 1986 Seed and De Alba publication with the exact DOI you requested. To find the precise journal article with its DOI, you may want to search directly in:\n- ASCE's digital library (ascelibrary.org)\n- Google Scholar\n- Your institution's library database\n\nThe article appears to be a foundational work on pore pressure generation models that is widely cited in the liquefaction engineering literature.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_011wm6d9hWxbGBCfH6BoLdbY","session_id":"2e84c092-3450-428f-a602-fb47a8d85d56","uuid":"9a773d4a-7cd9-4b4b-bc73-4b2d881d3502","timestamp":"2026-03-29T20:02:23.356Z"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01EthScausXwc8qG5cGM9fJp","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"LSN \"liquefaction severity number\" Tonkin Taylor New Zealand definition formula DOI paper\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"(PDF) Calibrating the Liquefaction Severity Number (LSN) for Varying Misprediction Economies: A Case Study in Christchurch, New Zealand\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/283835735_Calibrating_the_Liquefaction_Severity_Number_LSN_for_Varying_Misprediction_Economies_A_Case_Study_in_Christchurch_New_Zealand\"},{\"title\":\"Appendix A : Assessment of Liquefaction Vulnerability in Christchurch\",\"url\":\"https://www.eqc.govt.nz/assets/Canterbury-earthquake-page-documents/ILV/Appendix-A.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Calibrating the Liquefaction Severity Number (LSN) for ...\",\"url\":\"https://secure.tcc.co.nz/ei/images/ICEGE15%20Papers/Maurer_491.00.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"LSN – a new methodology for characterising the effects of liquefaction in terms of relative land damage severity - New Zealand Geotechnical Society\",\"url\":\"https://www.nzgs.org/libraries/nzgs19_163/\"},{\"title\":\"LSN – a new methodology for characterising the effects of ...\",\"url\":\"https://fl-nzgs-media.s3.amazonaws.com/uploads/2022/06/NZGS_Symposium_19_163-3.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Calibrating the Liquefaction Severity Number (LSN) for ...\",\"url\":\"https://www.issmge.org/uploads/publications/59/60/491.00_Maurer.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Publications | Tonkin + Taylor\",\"url\":\"http://tonkin-staging.azurewebsites.net/publications?sec=1203\"},{\"title\":\"A liquefaction potential integral index based on pore pressure build-up - ScienceDirect\",\"url\":\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0013795219312670\"},{\"title\":\"Tonkin & Taylor Ltd Christchurch Liquefaction Vulnerability Study\",\"url\":\"https://ccc.govt.nz/assets/Documents/Environment/Land/CCC-Liquefaction-ReportBody.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Development of LSN-based pipe repair rate models utilising data from the 2011 Christchurch earthquakes | Bulletin of the New Zealand Society for Earthquake Engineering\",\"url\":\"https://bulletin.nzsee.org.nz/index.php/bnzsee/article/view/1556\"}]\n\nBased on the web search results, I found information about the Liquefaction Severity Number (LSN) developed by Tonkin & Taylor. Here are the key details:\n\n## Definition\n\nThe Liquefaction Severity Number (LSN) is a dimensionless number produced by a depth-weighted assessment methodology to assess vulnerability of land to liquefaction-induced damage.\n\n## Formula\n\nLSN is defined as the integral of the post-liquefaction 1D volumetric reconsolidation strains (εv) calculated for each soil layer divided by the depth of its midpoint. LSN takes into account the contribution of non-liquefied soil strata and uses a hyperbolic depth weighing function (1/z) that gives high relevance to shallower liquefiable soil layers.\n\n## Development and Application\n\nThe methodology was developed by comparing measured attributes from properties damaged by the Canterbury Earthquake sequence with parameters calculated from approximately 7,500 Cone Penetration Tests (CPT). The results show that the depth to the liquefaction layers (controlled by the groundwater table) is the most important parameter when determining whether liquefaction is likely to cause damage at the ground surface.\n\n## Reference\n\nThe primary reference appears to be Tonkin & Taylor Ltd. Liquefaction vulnerability study. T&T Ref: 52020.0200/v.1.0, Christchurch, NZ, 2013. Additional peer-reviewed work on LSN calibration can be found in the 2015 Maurer et al. paper with DOI https://doi.org/10.1016/j.sandf.2015.06.010.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_011wm6d9hWxbGBCfH6BoLdbY","session_id":"2e84c092-3450-428f-a602-fb47a8d85d56","uuid":"233c6a8e-a8b1-4ede-aed1-d7306b58cf38","timestamp":"2026-03-29T20:02:25.517Z"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01TwGEr399sbZGJQzZQz4xiw","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"Hashash Groholski DEEPSOIL \"nonlinear site response\" pore pressure generation model DOI documentation\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Version 7.1 - DEEPSOIL\",\"url\":\"https://deepsoil.cee.illinois.edu/Files/DEEPSOIL_User_Manual_v7.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Estimation of Excess Pore Pressure Generation and Nonlinear Site Response of Liquefied Areas | IntechOpen\",\"url\":\"https://www.intechopen.com/chapters/68777\"},{\"title\":\"Development of an inverse analysis framework for extracting dynamic soil behavior and pore pressure response from downhole array measurements - Groholski - 2013 - International Journal for Numerical and Analytical Methods in Geomechanics - Wiley Online Library\",\"url\":\"https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/abs/10.1002/nag.2172\"},{\"title\":\"Nonlinear Site Response Analysis with Pore-Water Pressure Generation for Liquefaction Triggering Evaluation | Journal of Geotechnical and Geoenvironmental Engineering | Vol 146, No 2\",\"url\":\"https://ascelibrary.org/doi/abs/10.1061/(ASCE)GT.1943-5606.0002191\"},{\"title\":\"Evaluation of 1-D Non-linear Site Response Analysis using ...\",\"url\":\"https://www.issmge.org/uploads/publications/59/60/269.00_Hashash.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"recent advances in non-linear site response analysis\",\"url\":\"https://deepsoil.cee.illinois.edu/Files/5th%20Int%20Conference%20Paper%20State%20of%20the%20art%20-%20Hashash.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Deepsoil User Manual v6 | PDF | Spectral Density | Amplitude\",\"url\":\"https://www.scribd.com/document/399037896/Deepsoil-User-Manual-v6\"},{\"title\":\"Comparative nonlinear soil-structure interaction analyses using macro-element and soil-block modelling approaches | Bulletin of Earthquake Engineering | Springer Nature Link\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s10518-022-01379-y\"},{\"title\":\"[PDF] Evaluation of 1-D Non-linear Site Response Analysis using a General Quadratic / Hyperbolic Strength-Controlled Constitutive Model | Semantic Scholar\",\"url\":\"https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/Evaluation-of-1-D-Non-linear-Site-Response-Analysis-Groholski-Hashash/3910c17079c2dcbf91516d86d2b71fd1869df7cd\"},{\"title\":\"Nonlinear Solid–Fluid Coupled Seismic Response Analysis of Layered Liquefiable Deposit\",\"url\":\"https://www.mdpi.com/2076-3417/12/11/5628\"}]\n\nBased on the web search results, I found relevant information about Hashash, Groholski, and DEEPSOIL's nonlinear site response and pore pressure generation models:\n\n## Key Findings:\n\n**Software and Authors:**\nDEEPSOIL is a one-dimensional site response analysis program developed at the University of Illinois at Urbana-Champaign. Hashash, Y.M.A.; Musgrove, M.I.; Harmon, J.A.; Okan, I.; Groholski, D.R.; Phillips, C.A.; Park, D. authored DEEPSOIL 7.0, User Manual; Department of Civil and Environmental Engineering, University of Illinois at Urbana-Champaign: Champaign, IL, USA, 2017.\n\n**Nonlinear Model (GQ/H):**\nThe derivation and theoretical background of the GQ/H model are discussed in Groholski et. al (in preparation). The GQ/H model is implemented in DEEPSOIL. Users have the option to select the General Quadratic/Hyperbolic model (GQ/H) (Groholski et al. 2016).\n\n**Pore Pressure Generation Models:**\nDEEPSOIL includes models for: a) Equivalent Linear, b) Hyperbolic (MR, MRD, DC), c) a Non-Masing Hyperbolic model (MRDF), and d) Porewater Pressure Generation and Dissipation.\n\n**DOI Information:**\nThe search results reference user manuals and conference papers, but specific DOI documentation was not explicitly listed in the results. The DEEPSOIL User Manual v7.1 PDF is available at deepsoil.cee.illinois.edu.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_011wm6d9hWxbGBCfH6BoLdbY","session_id":"2e84c092-3450-428f-a602-fb47a8d85d56","uuid":"029d86dc-63ad-470d-86ae-1fc7e566cb10","timestamp":"2026-03-29T20:02:26.482Z"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Cd4wocz45PbewYPFhqD5GU","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"pysra \"pyStrata\" Kottke site response Python package pypi GitHub DOI journal citation\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"pyStrata · PyPI\",\"url\":\"https://pypi.org/project/pyStrata/\"},{\"title\":\"GitHub - arkottke/pystrata: Site Response Analysis Toolkit for Python · GitHub\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/arkottke/pystrata\"},{\"title\":\"pySRA · PyPI\",\"url\":\"https://pypi.org/project/pySRA/\"},{\"title\":\"pySRA 0.5.0 on PyPI - Libraries.io - security & maintenance data for open source software\",\"url\":\"https://libraries.io/pypi/pySRA\"},{\"title\":\"pyStrata - Python Package Health Analysis | Snyk\",\"url\":\"https://snyk.io/advisor/python/pystrata\"},{\"title\":\"pySRA: Docs, Community, Tutorials, Reviews | Openbase\",\"url\":\"https://openbase.com/python/pySRA\"},{\"title\":\"Releases · arkottke/pystrata\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/arkottke/pystrata/releases\"},{\"title\":\"arkottke/pysra v0.4.5\",\"url\":\"https://zenodo.org/records/3522104\"},{\"title\":\"NC92Soil: A computer code for deterministic and stochastic 1D equivalent linear seismic site response analyses - ScienceDirect\",\"url\":\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0266352X23006146\"},{\"title\":\"GitHub - arkottke/strata: Site response program featuring simulated profiles, random vibration theory, and a graphical user interface. · GitHub\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/arkottke/strata\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, here's what I found about pyStrata/pysra:\n\n## Overview\npyStrata is a site response analysis toolkit implemented in Python. The package was previously named pysra, but renamed to better associate with Strata.\n\n## Functionality\npyStrata provides tools for modeling wave propagation through layered soil profiles and computing surface ground motion from bedrock input motions, supporting time series and random vibration theory (RVT) approaches, equivalent-linear and frequency-domain methods, and logic trees for uncertainty quantification.\n\n## Package Availability\n- **PyPI**: pystrata is available via pip\n- **GitHub**: The package is available at https://github.com/arkottke/pystrata\n- **Repository**: The package is developed by Albert Kottke & Maxim Millen\n\n## Citation and DOI\nAlbert Kottke & Maxim Millen released v0.5.2 through Zenodo with DOI 10.5281/zenodo.7551992.\n\nThe search results show that pyStrata is a well-established open-source project for site response analysis with comprehensive documentation and examples available through the GitHub repository.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_011wm6d9hWxbGBCfH6BoLdbY","session_id":"2e84c092-3450-428f-a602-fb47a8d85d56","uuid":"86b90df9-1bd7-4c85-94b8-a8c40def12c3","timestamp":"2026-03-29T20:02:26.624Z"}
