{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01HMgNmHJMt8uSAPWube2m4Q","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"Estimating Earthquake-Induced Pore Water Pressure Hierarchy Methods MDPI Geosciences 2025 DOI 10.3390\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Estimating Earthquake-Induced Pore Water Pressure: Hierarchy of Existing Methods\",\"url\":\"https://www.mdpi.com/2076-3263/16/2/61\"},{\"title\":\"A Machine Learning Approach to Predicting Pore Pressure Response in Liquefiable Sands under Cyclic Loading | Proceedings | Vol , No\",\"url\":\"https://ascelibrary.org/doi/abs/10.1061/9780784484692.021\"},{\"title\":\"A New Method for Evaluating Liquefaction by Energy-Based Pore Water Pressure Models\",\"url\":\"https://www.mdpi.com/2079-6412/15/1/7\"},{\"title\":\"Water Level Response to Earthquakes in an Open Well and in a Closed Well—Analysis of Field Observations\",\"url\":\"https://www.mdpi.com/2073-4441/17/16/2453\"},{\"title\":\"Energy-Based Pore Pressure Generation Models in Silty Sands under Earthquake Loading\",\"url\":\"https://www.mdpi.com/2076-3263/14/6/166\"},{\"title\":\"Effects of Excess Pore Pressure Redistribution in Liquefiable Layers | Journal of Geotechnical and Geoenvironmental Engineering | Vol 150, No 4\",\"url\":\"https://ascelibrary.org/doi/abs/10.1061/JGGEFK.GTENG-11857\"},{\"title\":\"Deterministic numerical analysis of excess pore water pressure induced by 2012 earthquake in Pieve di Cento (Italy) - ScienceDirect\",\"url\":\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0267726125004178\"},{\"title\":\"Pore Water Pressure Prediction Based on Machine Learning Methods—Application to an Earth Dam Case | MDPI\",\"url\":\"https://www.mdpi.com/2076-3417/14/11/4749\"},{\"title\":\"Stability Analysis of Coastal Sheet Pile Wall Considering Soil Weakening Induced by Earthquake Loading\",\"url\":\"https://www.mdpi.com/2077-1312/13/9/1752\"},{\"title\":\"Estimation of Equivalent Pore Aspect Ratio in Rock Physics Models and Validation Using Digital Rocks\",\"url\":\"https://www.mdpi.com/2076-3263/15/2/67\"}]\n\nPerfect! I found the article you were searching for. The article \"Estimating Earthquake-Induced Pore Water Pressure: Hierarchy of Existing Methods\" was published in Geosciences 2026, 16(2), 61, with DOI 10.3390/geosciences16020061, and was published on February 1, 2026.\n\nThe article addresses the prediction of earthquake-induced excess pore water pressure, which is becoming a crucial step for liquefaction-related problems, such as the prediction of settlements, assessment of bearing capacity of shallow foundations, and efficiency of liquefaction mitigation measurements. The goal of this review is to provide a state of the art of the available methods for estimating excess pore water pressure from the most simplified approaches, based on simple hand-made calculations, to the most advanced models implemented in computer codes for seismic site response analysis.\n\nThe article is available on the MDPI website at: https://www.mdpi.com/2076-3263/16/2/61\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_011wm6d9hWxbGBCfH6BoLdbY","session_id":"2e84c092-3450-428f-a602-fb47a8d85d56","uuid":"cae0f893-0049-4cc2-885a-949370844f4d","timestamp":"2026-03-29T20:02:34.825Z"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01V4poihZw5SkV5tuaCsFKAx","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"\"nonlinear site response\" pore pressure generation liquefaction 2020 ASCE journal geotechnical DOI 10.1061\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Nonlinear Site Response Analysis with Pore-Water Pressure Generation for Liquefaction Triggering Evaluation - Illinois Experts\",\"url\":\"https://experts.illinois.edu/en/publications/nonlinear-site-response-analysis-with-pore-water-pressure-generat/\"},{\"title\":\"Nonlinear Site Response Analysis with Pore-Water Pressure Generation for Liquefaction Triggering Evaluation | Journal of Geotechnical and Geoenvironmental Engineering | Vol 146, No 2\",\"url\":\"https://ascelibrary.org/doi/abs/10.1061/(ASCE)GT.1943-5606.0002191\"},{\"title\":\"Estimation of Excess Pore Pressure Generation and Nonlinear Site Response of Liquefied Areas | IntechOpen\",\"url\":\"https://www.intechopen.com/chapters/68777\"},{\"title\":\"Nonlinear Site Response Analysis With Pore-Water Pressure Generation For Liquefaction Triggering Evaluation | PDF | Moment Magnitude Scale | Stress (Mechanics)\",\"url\":\"https://www.scribd.com/document/549517730/ASCE-GT-1943-5606-0002191\"},{\"title\":\"Youssef M. A. Hashash | Civil & Environmental Engineering | Illinois\",\"url\":\"https://cee.illinois.edu/directory/profile/hashash\"},{\"title\":\"Pore pressure generation and liquefaction analysis using nonlinear, effective stress-based site response analysis | IDEALS\",\"url\":\"https://www.ideals.illinois.edu/items/109954\"},{\"title\":\"Effects of Excess Pore Pressure Redistribution in Liquefiable Layers | Journal of Geotechnical and Geoenvironmental Engineering | Vol 150, No 4\",\"url\":\"https://ascelibrary.org/doi/abs/10.1061/JGGEFK.GTENG-11857\"},{\"title\":\"A Machine Learning Approach to Predicting Pore Pressure Response in Liquefiable Sands under Cyclic Loading | Proceedings | Vol , No\",\"url\":\"https://ascelibrary.org/doi/abs/10.1061/9780784484692.021\"},{\"title\":\"References | Seismic Site Response Analysis with Pore Water Pressure Generation: Guidelines | The National Academies Press\",\"url\":\"https://nap.nationalacademies.org/read/27536/chapter/11\"},{\"title\":\"Nonlinear Site Response Analysis with Pore-Water Pressure Generation for Liquefaction Triggering Evaluation | Request PDF\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/338972983_Nonlinear_Site_Response_Analysis_with_Pore-Water_Pressure_Generation_for_Liquefaction_Triggering_Evaluation\"}]\n\nI found a highly relevant match for your search query. The paper \"Nonlinear Site Response Analysis with Pore-Water Pressure Generation for Liquefaction Triggering Evaluation\" was authored by Olson, Scott M., Mei, Xuan, and Hashash, Youssef M.A. and published in 2020 in the Journal of Geotechnical and Geoenvironmental Engineering, Volume 146, Issue 2, Article 04019128, with DOI 10.1061/(ASCE)GT.1943-5606.0002191.\n\nThe study performed a broad parametric study to assess the viability of using nonlinear site response analysis with validated constitutive and pore-water pressure generation models to evaluate level-ground liquefaction. For a wide range of conditions, the parametric results agreed with published empirical liquefaction-triggering relations, and the nonlinear site response analysis with pore-water pressure generation also correctly predicted liquefaction for dynamic centrifuge tests and field cases.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_011wm6d9hWxbGBCfH6BoLdbY","session_id":"2e84c092-3450-428f-a602-fb47a8d85d56","uuid":"634164b9-4c87-4bca-87b0-5ed2d5785ed4","timestamp":"2026-03-29T20:02:36.065Z"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_019uyBvZ3A29taMYxts38f1Y","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"Iwasaki 1982 \"liquefaction potential\" \"soil deposits\" \"damage\" Japan journal second paper DOI citation\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Real-time LPI-based Assessment of the Liquefaction\",\"url\":\"https://www.iitk.ac.in/nicee/wcee/article/WCEE2012_3356.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"INTERNATIONAL SOCIETY FOR SOIL MECHANICS AND GEOTECHNICAL ENGINEERING\",\"url\":\"https://www.issmge.org/uploads/publications/1/45/06-technical-committee-09-tc203-26.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"A study of the liquefaction risk potential at Yuanlin, Taiwan - ScienceDirect\",\"url\":\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0013795203001285\"},{\"title\":\"LIQUEFACTION POTENTIAL INDEX: A CRITICAL ...\",\"url\":\"http://yo-1.ct.ntust.edu.tw/jge/files/articlefiles/v1i1200707031332604692.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Technical Note 2 – Surface manifestation of liquefaction | GeoAdvanced\",\"url\":\"https://geoadvanced.com/support/tech-notes/surface-manifestation/\"},{\"title\":\"Susceptibility to liquefaction of geomorphological units (Iwasaki 1982) | Download Table\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/figure/Susceptibility-to-liquefaction-of-geomorphological-units-Iwasaki-1982_tbl1_257632762\"},{\"title\":\"Liquefaction (GH0307) | UNDRR\",\"url\":\"https://www.undrr.org/understanding-disaster-risk/terminology/hips/gh0307\"},{\"title\":\"Theoretical notes > Simplifid methods > Iwasaki et al.\",\"url\":\"https://help.geostru.eu/liquiter/en/iwasaki_alii.htm\"},{\"title\":\"Liquefaction Potential Index (LPI) -Iwasaki et al (1982) & Sonmez (2003) | Download Scientific Diagram\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/figure/Liquefaction-Potential-Index-LPI-Iwasaki-et-al-1982-Sonmez-2003_tbl1_335207175\"},{\"title\":\"Scholars' Mine - International Conferences on Recent Advances in Geotechnical Earthquake Engineering and Soil Dynamics: Soil Liquefaction Potential Evaluation with Use of the Simplified Procedure\",\"url\":\"https://scholarsmine.mst.edu/icrageesd/01icrageesd/session02/12/\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, I found multiple references to Iwasaki's 1982 work on liquefaction potential. Here are the key citations:\n\n## Primary 1982 Reference\n\nIwasaki, T., Tokida, K., Tatsuoka, F., Watanabe, S., Yasuda, S., and Sato, H. (1982). \"Microzonation for soil liquefaction potential using simplified methods.\" Proceedings 3rd International Conference on Microzonation, Seattle, USA. 1319-1330.\n\n## Alternative 1982 Reference\n\nIwasaki, T., Arakawa, T. and Tokida, K. (1982). \"Simplified Procedures for Assessing Soil Liquefaction During Earthquakes\" Proc. Conference on Soil Dynamics and Earthquake Engineering. Southampton, 925-939.\n\n## Related Earlier Work\n\nIwasaki, T., Tatsuoka, F., Tokida, K. and Yasuda, S. (1978). \"A Practical Method for Assessing Soil Liquefaction Potential Based on Case Studies at Various site in Japan.\" 5th Japan Earthquake Engineering Symposium. Vol II: 641-648.\n\nThe search results show there are two different Iwasaki et al. 1982 papers - one presented at the International Conference on Microzonation in Seattle and another at the Conference on Soil Dynamics and Earthquake Engineering in Southampton. Both deal with simplified procedures for assessing soil liquefaction potential and associated damage to soil deposits. However, I was unable to locate specific DOI numbers in the search results for these conference proceedings.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_011wm6d9hWxbGBCfH6BoLdbY","session_id":"2e84c092-3450-428f-a602-fb47a8d85d56","uuid":"25af413f-1e7d-4340-8061-6112c6ddf05c","timestamp":"2026-03-29T20:02:38.465Z"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Y7MKLMDRFXHGUZrN7MzmdT","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"\"30 percent\" OR \"30%\" liquefiable layer thickness failure criterion liquefaction 1D analysis reference\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Liquefiable and non-liquefiable layer thickness within the 15 first m... | Download Scientific Diagram\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/figure/Liquefiable-and-non-liquefiable-layer-thickness-within-the-15-first-m-of-Vs-profile_tbl2_341090361\"},{\"title\":\"Examining the Role of Liquefiable Layer Thickness and Depth on the Seismic Lateral Response of Piles through Numerical Analyses | International Journal of Geomechanics | Vol 23, No 5\",\"url\":\"https://ascelibrary.org/doi/abs/10.1061/IJGNAI.GMENG-8143\"},{\"title\":\"LPI-based approach for calibrating the severity of liquefaction-induced failures and for assessing the probability of liquefaction surface evidence - ScienceDirect\",\"url\":\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0013795207002116\"},{\"title\":\"Probabilistic evaluation of liquefaction analysis in performance based design framework | Bulletin of Earthquake Engineering | Springer Nature Link\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s10518-025-02230-w\"},{\"title\":\"Effect of layered liquefiable deposits on the seismic response of soil-foundations-structure systems - ScienceDirect\",\"url\":\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0267726118313654\"},{\"title\":\"Moving towards an improved index for assessing liquefaction hazard: Lessons from historical data - ScienceDirect\",\"url\":\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0038080615000827\"},{\"title\":\"The Effect of Failure Criteria on Liquefaction and Pore Pressure Prediction in Non-Plastic Soils | MDPI\",\"url\":\"https://www.mdpi.com/2673-7094/5/2/27\"},{\"title\":\"Recent Advances in Seismic Soil Liquefaction Engineering | SpringerLink\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/chapter/10.1007/978-3-319-07118-3_19\"},{\"title\":\"Impact of Liquefiable Layers on Site Response Analysis\",\"url\":\"https://www.hrpub.org/download/20241030/CEA28-14838685.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Effects of Excess Pore Pressure Redistribution in Liquefiable Layers | Journal of Geotechnical and Geoenvironmental Engineering | Vol 150, No 4\",\"url\":\"https://ascelibrary.org/doi/abs/10.1061/JGGEFK.GTENG-11857\"}]\n\nI've completed the web search for your query on the \"30 percent\" or \"30%\" liquefiable layer thickness failure criterion related to liquefaction and 1D analysis. \n\nThe search results returned several relevant academic papers and studies on liquefaction analysis, though the specific \"30 percent\" criterion you mentioned doesn't appear prominently in the initial results. Here's what the search found:\n\n**Key findings from the results:**\n\n1. Liquefiable layer thickness plays a critical role in controlling seismic pore pressure response.\n\n2. A criterion has been developed to evaluate the thicknesses of a layer below which redistribution could prevent liquefaction even if the layer is deemed liquefied according to existing liquefaction-triggering procedures.\n\n3. Parametric analyses have been conducted for a wide range of liquefiable layer thicknesses and depths.\n\n4. Several studies use 1D effective stress analysis for site response analysis involving liquefiable deposits, but the search results don't explicitly reference a universal \"30 percent thickness\" failure criterion.\n\nThe results suggest that layer thickness is an important parameter in liquefaction analysis, but to find the specific reference you're looking for regarding the \"30 percent\" criterion, you may need to search for more specific author names or check foundational papers by researchers like Ishihara, Iwasaki, or Idriss who have developed criteria for liquefaction assessment.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your respo