{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_014xve8WKDXUiV1XimXcRJwU","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"Kim Hashash Stewart Rathje 2016 \"relative differences\" nonlinear equivalent linear 1D site response DOI Earthquake Spectra\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Relative Differences between Nonlinear and Equivalent-Linear 1-D Site Response Analyses - Byungmin Kim, Youssef M. A. Hashash, Jonathan P. Stewart, Ellen M. Rathje, Joseph A. Harmon, Michael I. Musgrove, Kenneth W. Campbell, Walter J. Silva, 2016\",\"url\":\"https://journals.sagepub.com/doi/10.1193/051215EQS068M\"},{\"title\":\"(PDF) Relative Differences between Nonlinear and Equivalent-Linear 1D Site Response Analyses\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/296472559_Relative_Differences_between_Nonlinear_and_Equivalent-Linear_1D_Site_Response_Analyses\"},{\"title\":\"Relative Differences between Nonlinear and Equivalent‐Linear 1‐D Site Response Analyses\",\"url\":\"https://chooser.crossref.org/?doi=10.1193/051215EQS068M\"},{\"title\":\"Simulated site amplification for Central and Eastern North America: Data set development and amplification models - Okan Ilhan, Youssef MA Hashash, Jonathan P Stewart, Ellen M Rathje, Sissy Nikolaou, Kenneth W Campbell, 2024\",\"url\":\"https://journals.sagepub.com/doi/full/10.1177/87552930231215723\"},{\"title\":\"Comparison of Equivalent Linear and Nonlinear Site Response Analysis Results and Model to Estimate Maximum Shear Strain - Brian Carlton, Kohji Tokimatsu, 2016\",\"url\":\"https://journals.sagepub.com/doi/abs/10.1193/021215EQS029MR1\"},{\"title\":\"A Site-Specific Response Analysis: A Case Study in Hanoi, Vietnam\",\"url\":\"https://mdpi.com/2076-3417/10/11/3972/htm\"},{\"title\":\"Divergence Between Nonlinear and Equivalent-Linear 1D Site Response Analyses for Different V S Realizations of Typical Clay Sites - Pure and Applied Geophysics\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s00024-017-1586-y?error=cookies_not_supported&code=0994c48a-1043-466b-b417-597d6cb737cd\"},{\"title\":\"A simulation-based nonlinear site amplification model for ground-motion prediction equations in Japan - Earthquake Engineering and Engineering Vibration\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s11803-021-2058-z?error=cookies_not_supported&code=7d5cd314-c4cc-4b2d-9ebb-369a615428d0\"},{\"title\":\"(PDF) Comparison of Equivalent Linear and Nonlinear Site Response Analysis Results and Model to Estimate Maximum Shear Strain\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/285619629_Comparison_of_Equivalent_Linear_and_Nonlinear_Site_Response_Analysis_Results_and_Model_to_Estimate_Maximum_Shear_Strain\"},{\"title\":\"Equivalent Linear and Nonlinear Site-Specific Ground Response Analysis of Pashto Cultural Museum Peshawar, Pakistan | Iranian Journal of Science and Technology, Transactions of Civil Engineering\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s40996-020-00346-4\"}]\n\nI found the paper you're looking for! Here are the key details:\n\n**Publication Information:**\nThe paper \"Relative Differences between Nonlinear and Equivalent-Linear 1-D Site Response Analyses\" was published in Earthquake Spectra in August 2016 by authors Byungmin Kim, Youssef M. A. Hashash, Jonathan P. Stewart, Ellen M. Rathje, Joseph A. Harmon, Michael I. Musgrove, Kenneth W. Campbell, and Walter J. Silva.\n\n**DOI:** 10.1193/051215eqs068m\n\n**Paper Focus:**\nThe study investigates the conditions for which one-dimensional (1-D) nonlinear (NL) site response analysis results are distinct from equivalent-linear (EL) results and provides guidance for predicting when differences are large enough to be of practical significance.\n\n**Key Findings:**\nRelative differences in spectral accelerations and Fourier amplitudes computed from NL and EL analyses were assessed for a range of site conditions and for suites of input motions appropriate for active crustal and stable continental regions, with EL/NL differences most clearly dependent on shear strain index (Iγ), defined as the ratio of input motion peak velocity to time-averaged shear-wave velocity in the top 30 m of the soil profile.\n\nThe paper is available through Wiley and GeoScienceWorld platforms.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_011wm6d9hWxbGBCfH6BoLdbY","session_id":"2e84c092-3450-428f-a602-fb47a8d85d56","uuid":"a2927cc9-3a9b-42b6-88ba-3b9477f350be","timestamp":"2026-03-29T20:03:00.901Z"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01VS6MYE4rJvS2sn41WPQv1j","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"Tonkin Taylor 2013 \"liquefaction vulnerability study\" Christchurch LSN report full citation URL\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Tonkin & Taylor Ltd Christchurch Liquefaction Vulnerability Study\",\"url\":\"https://ccc.govt.nz/assets/Documents/Environment/Land/CCC-Liquefaction-ReportBody.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Appendix A : Assessment of Liquefaction Vulnerability in Christchurch\",\"url\":\"https://www.eqc.govt.nz/assets/Canterbury-earthquake-page-documents/ILV/Appendix-A.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Implications of Sea Level Rise on Liquefaction ...\",\"url\":\"https://secure.tcc.co.nz/ei/images/ICEGE15%20Papers/Fraser%20109.00_.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Assessment of Liquefaction-Induced Land Damage for Residential Christchurch - S. van Ballegooy, P. Malan, V. Lacrosse, M. E. Jacka, M. Cubrinovski, J. D. Bray, T. D. O'Rourke, S. A. Crawford, H. Cowan, 2014\",\"url\":\"https://journals.sagepub.com/doi/10.1193/031813EQS070M\"},{\"title\":\"Comparison of CPT-based simplified liquefaction ...\",\"url\":\"https://www.issmge.org/uploads/publications/89/100/12ANZ_082.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Publications | Tonkin + Taylor\",\"url\":\"http://tonkin-staging.azurewebsites.net/publications?sec=1203\"},{\"title\":\"(PDF) Calibrating the Liquefaction Severity Number (LSN) for Varying Misprediction Economies: A Case Study in Christchurch, New Zealand\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/283835735_Calibrating_the_Liquefaction_Severity_Number_LSN_for_Varying_Misprediction_Economies_A_Case_Study_in_Christchurch_New_Zealand\"},{\"title\":\"(PDF) Pipeline Damage Predictions in Liquefaction Zones Using LSN\",\"url\":\"https://www.academia.edu/75373004/Pipeline_Damage_Predictions_in_Liquefaction_Zones_Using_LSN\"},{\"title\":\"Mapping liquefaction vulnerability for Christchurch City\",\"url\":\"https://abley.com/our-insights/mapping-liquefaction-vulnerability\"},{\"title\":\"Observed liquefaction maps from Tonkin and Taylor for a) the February... | Download Scientific Diagram\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/figure/Observed-liquefaction-maps-from-Tonkin-and-Taylor-for-a-the-February-event-and-b-the_fig1_283712058\"}]\n\nBased on the web search results, here is the information for the Tonkin Taylor 2013 Liquefaction Vulnerability Study:\n\n**Full Citation:**\nTonkin & Taylor, Ltd., 2013. Liquefaction Vulnerability Study, Report to Earthquake Commission, T&T ref. 52020.0200/v1.0, prepared by S. van Ballegooy and P. Malan, available at https://canterburygeotechnicaldatabase.projectorbit.com.\n\n**Primary Document URL:**\nhttps://ccc.govt.nz/assets/Documents/Environment/Land/CCC-Liquefaction-ReportBody.pdf\n\n**Additional Details:**\nTonkin & Taylor Ltd (T+T) was engaged by Christchurch City Council (CCC) to provide a technical review of information relevant to the liquefaction hazard in Christchurch, and from this information develop a broad-scale liquefaction vulnerability model to help inform various future activities.\n\nThe report developed a liquefaction vulnerability model using the Liquefaction Severity Number (LSN) parameter and is based on extensive geotechnical data from the Canterbury earthquakes.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_011wm6d9hWxbGBCfH6BoLdbY","session_id":"2e84c092-3450-428f-a602-fb47a8d85d56","uuid":"1ee6a2c8-fa44-4bda-a5c7-99546198d2f3","timestamp":"2026-03-29T20:03:01.467Z"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Fy9EVCDf9Z36gAy8Akxzp9","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"Seed Martin Lysmer 1976 pore pressure under dynamic loads cohesionless soils ASCE journal geotechnical DOI\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"(PDF) An Energy-Based Excess Pore Pressure Generation Model for Cohesionless Soils\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/265564025_An_Energy-Based_Excess_Pore_Pressure_Generation_Model_for_Cohesionless_Soils\"},{\"title\":\"(PDF) Pore Pressure Generation Models for Sands and Silty Soils Subjected to Cyclic Loading\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/245294607_Pore_Pressure_Generation_Models_for_Sands_and_Silty_Soils_Subjected_to_Cyclic_Loading\"},{\"title\":\"Development of Excess Pore Water Pressure in Sand during K0-Controlled Cyclic Loading | KSCE Journal of Civil Engineering\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s12205-024-2185-y\"},{\"title\":\"Cohesionless Soil - an overview | ScienceDirect Topics\",\"url\":\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/engineering/cohesionless-soil\"},{\"title\":\"Effects of Excess Pore Pressure Redistribution in Liquefiable Layers | Journal of Geotechnical and Geoenvironmental Engineering | Vol 150, No 4\",\"url\":\"https://ascelibrary.org/doi/abs/10.1061/JGGEFK.GTENG-11857\"},{\"title\":\"Analysis of gravel drains in liquefiable soils\",\"url\":\"https://www.issmge.org/uploads/publications/1/31/1997_03_0055.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"The laboratory evidence of phase transformation from landslide to debris flow | SpringerLink\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s12303-013-0049-4?error=cookies_not_supported&code=b3646da7-9a7c-4db6-bcc9-4f89673e33c7\"},{\"title\":\"Pore Pressure Generation of Gravelly Soils in Constant Volume Cyclic Simple Shear | Journal of Geotechnical and Geoenvironmental Engineering | Vol 149, No 2\",\"url\":\"https://ascelibrary.org/doi/abs/10.1061/(ASCE)GT.1943-5606.0002928\"},{\"title\":\"Pore pressure model based on accumulated stress | Bulletin of Earthquake Engineering | Springer Nature Link\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s10518-014-9702-1\"},{\"title\":\"Cyclic Large Strain and Induced Pore Pressure Models for Saturated Clean Sands | Journal of Geotechnical and Geoenvironmental Engineering | Vol 138, No 3\",\"url\":\"https://ascelibrary.org/doi/abs/10.1061/(ASCE)GT.1943-5606.0000631\"}]\n\nBased on the web search results, I found the specific publication you're looking for:\n\nSeed, H. B., P. P. Martin, and J. Lysmer. 1976. \"Pore-water pressure changes during soil liquefaction.\" J. Geotech. Eng. Div. 102 (4): 323–346. https://doi.org/10.1061/AJGEB6.0000258.\n\nThis is the seminal 1976 publication by Seed, Martin, and Lysmer on pore pressure changes under dynamic loads in cohesionless soils, published in the ASCE Journal of Geotechnical Engineering Division. The DOI is **10.1061/AJGEB6.0000258** and it appears in volume 102, issue 4, pages 323-346.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_011wm6d9hWxbGBCfH6BoLdbY","session_id":"2e84c092-3450-428f-a602-fb47a8d85d56","uuid":"5f9dfa98-7405-4842-bbab-b92d4599391d","timestamp":"2026-03-29T20:03:01.995Z"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01AManYjz3abbNsQ5hc3XGtV","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"Chen Arduino 2021 \"PM4Sand OpenSees\" PEER report implementation verification DOI URL\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"New PEER Report 2021/02: \\\"Implementation, Verification, and Validation of the PM4Sand Model in OpenSees\\\" | Pacific Earthquake Engineering Research Center\",\"url\":\"https://peer.berkeley.edu/news/new-peer-report-202102-implementation-verification-and-validation-pm4sand-model-opensees\"},{\"title\":\"Implementation, Verification, and Validation of the PM4Sand Model in OpenSees, PEER Report 2021-02 | Pacific Earthquake Engineering Research Center\",\"url\":\"https://peer.berkeley.edu/publications/2021-02\"},{\"title\":\"News | Pacific Earthquake Engineering Research Center\",\"url\":\"https://peer.berkeley.edu/news-archive?page=4\"},{\"title\":\"PEER Reports | Pacific Earthquake Engineering Research Center\",\"url\":\"https://peer.berkeley.edu/topics/peer-reports?sort_order=DESC&sort_by=changed&page=36\"},{\"title\":\"3.1.6.7. PM4Sand Material — OpenSees Documentation documentation\",\"url\":\"https://opensees.github.io/OpenSeesDocumentation/user/manual/material/ndMaterials/PM4Sand.html\"},{\"title\":\"PEER Research Project Highlight: “Implementation and Validation of PM4Sand in OpenSees” | Pacific Earthquake Engineering Research Center\",\"url\":\"https://peer.berkeley.edu/news/peer-research-project-highlight-%E2%80%9Cimplementation-and-validation-pm4sand-opensees%E2%80%9D\"},{\"title\":\"PM4Sand-Example 1 - OpenSeesWiki\",\"url\":\"https://opensees.berkeley.edu/wiki/index.php/PM4Sand-Example_1\"},{\"title\":\"14.4.3. PM4Sand model undrained cyclic simple shear element — OpenSeesPy 3.5.1.3 documentation\",\"url\":\"https://openseespydoc.readthedocs.io/en/latest/src/pm4sand_cyc_cal.html\"},{\"title\":\"PM4Sand-Example 2 - OpenSeesWiki\",\"url\":\"https://opensees.berkeley.edu/wiki/index.php/PM4Sand-Example_2\"},{\"title\":\"PM4Sand Material - OpenSeesWiki\",\"url\":\"https://opensees.berkeley.edu/wiki/index.php/PM4Sand_Material\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, here is the information for the Chen and Arduino 2021 PM4Sand OpenSees PEER report:\n\n**Report Information:**\n- Title: \"Implementation, Verification, and Validation of the PM4Sand Model in OpenSees\"\n- Authors: Long Chen and Pedro Arduino, Department of Civil and Environmental Engineering, University of Washington\n- Report No. 2021/02\n- Published by Pacific Earthquake Engineering Research Center, University of California, Berkeley, CA\n\n**DOI:**\nhttps://doi.org/10.55461/SJEU6160\n\n**URL:**\nhttps://peer.berkeley.edu/news/new-peer-report-202102-implementation-verification-and-validation-pm4sand-model-opensees\n\n**Publication Details:**\nThe main objective was to implement, verify, and validate the PM4Sand constitutive model using the finite-element platform OpenSees, with verification studies comparing the model's response across three different frameworks: OpenSees, FLAC, and PLAXIS.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_011wm6d9hWxbGBCfH6BoLdbY","session_id":"2e84c092-3450-428f-a602-fb47a8d85d56","uuid":"1806f7cb-3ccf-4d0a-ba3d-e247c6aa4140","timestamp":"2026-03-29T20:03:03.234Z"}
