{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_0117pagrLia3S6Fzypc8Kwb7","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"Seed 1976 \"pore pressure changes during soil liquefaction\" journal geotechnical engineering division ASCE volume 102 DOI\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Effects of Excess Pore Pressure Redistribution in Liquefiable Layers | Journal of Geotechnical and Geoenvironmental Engineering | Vol 150, No 4\",\"url\":\"https://ascelibrary.org/doi/abs/10.1061/JGGEFK.GTENG-11857\"},{\"title\":\"Pore-Water Pressure Changes during Soil Liquefaction | Journal of the Geotechnical Engineering Division | Vol 102, No 4\",\"url\":\"https://ascelibrary.org/doi/10.1061/AJGEB6.0000258\"},{\"title\":\"Pore-Water Pressure Changes during Soil Liquefaction\",\"url\":\"https://cedb.asce.org/CEDBsearch/record.jsp?dockey=0006631\"},{\"title\":\"PORE-WATER PRESSURE CHANGES DURING SOIL LIQUEFACTION - TRID\",\"url\":\"https://trid.trb.org/view.aspx?id=45358\"},{\"title\":\"Prediction of liquefaction potential and pore water pressure beneath machine foundations | Central European Journal of Engineering\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/article/10.2478/s13531-013-0165-y\"},{\"title\":\"Influence of Buildings on Potential Liquefaction Damage | Journal of Geotechnical Engineering | Vol 116, No 2\",\"url\":\"https://ascelibrary.org/doi/abs/10.1061/%28ASCE%290733-9410%281990%29116%3A2%28165%29\"},{\"title\":\"Calibration of Pore Pressure Model for the Liquefaction Prediction of Soils Based on Behavior of Clean Sand, Magnitude Scaling Factor, and Initial Stress Level | Journal of Geotechnical and Geoenvironmental Engineering | Vol 149, No 8\",\"url\":\"https://ascelibrary.org/doi/10.1061/JGGEFK.GTENG-11447\"},{\"title\":\"Factors Affecting Liquefaction Resistance and Assessment by Pore Pressure Model | SpringerLink\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/chapter/10.1007/978-3-031-11898-2_7\"},{\"title\":\"Recent Advances in Seismic Soil Liquefaction Engineering | SpringerLink\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/chapter/10.1007/978-3-319-07118-3_19\"},{\"title\":\"Evaluation of Liquefaction Potential Using Field Performance Data | Journal of Geotechnical Engineering | Vol 109, No 3\",\"url\":\"https://ascelibrary.org/doi/10.1061/%28ASCE%290733-9410%281983%29109%3A3%28458%29\"}]\n\nBased on the web search results, I found the paper you're looking for:\n\n**Seed, H. B., P. P. Martin, and J. Lysmer. 1976. \"Pore-water pressure changes during soil liquefaction.\" J. Geotech. Eng. Div. 102 (4): 323–346. https://doi.org/10.1061/AJGEB6.0000258.**\n\nThe paper is available through the ASCE Library. An analytical procedure is presented for evaluating the general characteristics of pore-water pressure buildup and subsequent dissipation in sand deposits both during and following a period of earthquake shaking. It is shown that in layers of fine sand, excess hydrostatic pressures may persist for an hour or more after an earthquake. However evidence of subsurface liquefaction may not appear at the ground surface until several minutes after the shaking has stopped and the critical condition at the ground surface may not develop until 10 min-30 min after the earthquake.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_011wm6d9hWxbGBCfH6BoLdbY","session_id":"2e84c092-3450-428f-a602-fb47a8d85d56","uuid":"42ff6282-d53a-42b9-80be-bdaf1c5d8701","timestamp":"2026-03-29T20:04:08.350Z"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_013BSfwFEX9C7vXmr62FnSjT","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"\"effective strain\" \"strain ratio\" 0.65 SHAKE equivalent linear history Schnabel Lysmer Seed 1972 original\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"(PDF) Influence of the effective strain ratio on equivalent linear ground response simulation\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/381475867_Influence_of_the_effective_strain_ratio_on_equivalent_linear_ground_response_simulation\"},{\"title\":\"(PDF) Ground Motion Parameter Sensitivity to Shear Strain Ratio in Equivalent Linear Analyses\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/284766296_Ground_Motion_Parameter_Sensitivity_to_Shear_Strain_Ratio_in_Equivalent_Linear_Analyses\"},{\"title\":\"EQUIVALENT LINEAR ANALYSIS CONSIDERING LARGE ...\",\"url\":\"https://www.iitk.ac.in/nicee/wcee/article/1832.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"SHAKE - A Computer Program for Earthquake Response Analysis of Horizontally Layered Sites | Resolution Copper Project and Land Exchange Environmental Impact Statement\",\"url\":\"https://www.resolutionmineeis.us/documents/schnabel-lysmer-seed-1972\"},{\"title\":\"(PDF) Shear Stress-Strain Curves Based on the G/G max Logic: A Procedure for Strength Compatibility\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/269057136_Shear_Stress-Strain_Curves_Based_on_the_GG_max_Logic_A_Procedure_for_Strength_Compatibility\"},{\"title\":\"Strain-threshold- and frequency-dependent seismic simulation of nonlinear soils | Earthquake Science | Springer Nature Link\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s11589-014-0102-z\"},{\"title\":\"Schnabel, P.B., Lysmer, J. and Seed, H.B. (1972) SHAKE —A computer program for earthquake response analysis of horizontal layered sites. Report. No. EERC 71-12, Earthquake Engineering Research Center, Berkeley. - References - Scientific Research Publishing\",\"url\":\"https://www.scirp.org/reference/referencespapers?referenceid=536307\"},{\"title\":\"1972 Shake Schnabel and Seed | PDF\",\"url\":\"https://www.scribd.com/document/364979048/1972-Shake-Schnabel-and-Seed\"},{\"title\":\"P. B. Schnabel, J. Lysmer and H. B. Seed, “SHAKE, a Computer Program for Earthquake Response Analysis of Horizontally Layered Sites,” Report No. EERC 72-12, Earthquake Engineering Research Center, University of California, Berkeley, 1972. - References - Scientific Research Publishing\",\"url\":\"https://www.scirp.org/reference/ReferencesPapers?ReferenceID=881838\"},{\"title\":\"schnabel-lysmer-seed-1972.pdf\",\"url\":\"https://www.resolutionmineeis.us/sites/default/files/references/schnabel-lysmer-seed-1972.pdf\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, I found information about the effective strain, strain ratio of 0.65, and the SHAKE program by Schnabel, Lysmer, and Seed from 1972.\n\nThe effective strain is computed as the product between the maximum shear strain and the effective strain ratio, which is a scaling factor conventionally equal to 0.65. This is a fundamental concept in equivalent linear ground response analysis.\n\nSeed and Idriss (Schnabel et al. 1972) suggested that the effective shear strain (γeff) is fixed as 65 % of the maximum strain (γmax), namely, γeff = 0.65γmax.\n\nThe original work comes from Schnabel, P.B., Lysmer, J. and Seed, H.B., 1972. SHAKE: A computer program for earthquake response analysis of horizontally layered sites. The full citation is \"SHAKE, a Computer Program for Earthquake Response Analysis of Horizontally Layered Sites,\" Report No. EERC 72-12, Earthquake Engineering Research Center, University of California, Berkeley, 1972.\n\nHowever, the proposed values are the result of recommendations without a rigorous demonstration and their reliability has been questioned.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_011wm6d9hWxbGBCfH6BoLdbY","session_id":"2e84c092-3450-428f-a602-fb47a8d85d56","uuid":"f99c46ad-d8f5-440c-a1a1-4720912b6b2c","timestamp":"2026-03-29T20:04:09.276Z"}
