{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_011wm6d9hWxbGBCfH6BoLdbY","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I now have sufficient verified information from the web searches to compile the full response. Let me now compile everything.\n\n---\n\nBelow is a compilation of references found through web searches. Each citation has been verified against search results and authoritative URLs. Where a DOI was confirmed via CrossRef, Springer, ASCE Library, PEER, NIST, or the National Academies, it is given. Where only a report or conference proceedings exists without a DOI, the institutional URL is given.\n\n---\n\n## TOPIC 1: Shear Strain Approaches in 1D Equivalent Linear Site Response\n\n### Background and key concepts confirmed by search results\n\nIn equivalent linear site response analysis (EQLSRA), the effective (or equivalent) shear strain is computed as a fraction of the peak (maximum) shear strain in each layer. Two approaches for selecting this fraction appear in the literature:\n\n- **Max-max approach**: uses the absolute maximum (peak) shear strain directly. This is simple but can overestimate damping in long, irregular motions because a single large-amplitude peak may not be representative of the bulk of the loading.\n- **RMS-based approach**: uses the root-mean-square of the strain time history, or a weighted effective strain ratio applied to it. This is argued to better represent the energy content of the loading, but is sensitive to how the RMS window is defined.\n- **Equivalent uniform strain (65% of peak)**: the conventional SHAKE approach (Schnabel et al. 1972) adopts a fixed ratio of 0.65 times the peak shear strain as the \"effective\" strain. This is computationally simple but lacks physical justification for irregular motions, and its reliability has been repeatedly questioned.\n\n### References\n\n**R1.1** Schnabel, P. B., Lysmer, J., and Seed, H. B. (1972). \"SHAKE: A Computer Program for Earthquake Response Analysis of Horizontally Layered Sites.\" Report No. EERC 72-12, Earthquake Engineering Research Center, University of California, Berkeley.\n- URL: https://www.resolutionmineeis.us/documents/schnabel-lysmer-seed-1972\n- Note: This is the origin of the 0.65 x gamma_max \"effective strain\" convention.\n\n**R1.2** Sinha, R. (2025). \"An improved notion for the computation of strain ratio in equivalent linear site response.\" Bulletin of Earthquake Engineering, 23, 53-82. Published online December 10, 2024.\n- DOI: https://doi.org/10.1007/s10518-024-02061-1\n- Note: Proposes a frequency-content and intensity-weighted strain ratio; tests on 15 KiK-net stations and 110 ground motions; reports ~54% reduction in PHA prediction error versus the fixed 0.65 ratio.\n\n**R1.3** Carlton, B., and Tokimatsu, K. (2016). \"Comparison of Equivalent Linear and Nonlinear Site Response Analysis Results and Model to Estimate Maximum Shear Strain.\" Earthquake Spectra, 32(3), 1419-1440.\n- DOI: https://doi.org/10.1193/021215EQS029MR1\n- Note: Compares maximum shear strains from ELA vs. NLA; develops a predictive model for gamma_max,ELA; finds non-negligible differences above ~0.04-1.0% strain.\n\n**R1.4** Kim, B., Hashash, Y. M. A., Stewart, J. P., Rathje, E. M., Harmon, J. A., Musgrove, M. I., Campbell, K. W., and Silva, W. J. (2016). \"Relative Differences between Nonlinear and Equivalent-Linear 1-D Site Response Analyses.\" Earthquake Spectra, 32(3), 1845-1865.\n- DOI: https://doi.org/10.1193/051215EQS068M\n- Note: Identifies the shear strain index (peak velocity / Vs30) as the key predictor of when NL and EL methods diverge; widely used guidance paper.\n\n**R1.5** Kottke, A. R., and Rathje, E. M. (2008/2009). \"Technical Manual for Strata.\" PEER Report 2008/10, Pacific Earthquake Engineering Research Center, University of California, Berkeley.\n- URL: https://peer.berkeley.edu/sites/default/files/web_peer810_albert_r._kottke_ellen_m._rathje.pdf\n- Note: Documents the Strata software for equivalent linear analysis; discusses strain ratio selection and RVT-based approaches.\n\n**R1.6** Olson, S. M., Mei, X., and Hashash, Y. M. A. (2020). \"Nonlinear Site Response Analysis with Pore-Water Pressure Generation for Liquefaction Triggering Evaluation.\" Journal of Geotechnical and Geoenvironmental Engineering, 146(2), 04019128.\n- DOI: https://doi.org/10.1061/(ASCE)GT.1943-5606.0002191\n- Note: Comprehensive parametric study linking nonlinear (effective-stress) site response to liquefaction triggering; uses DEEPSOIL with pore pressure generation models; validates against empirical charts and centrifuge tests.\n\n---\n\n## TOPIC 2: Empirical Methods Estimating Pore Pressure from PGA and Mw\n\n### Key methods and references\n\n**R2.1** Dobry, R., Ladd, R. S., Yokel, F. Y., Chung, R. M., and Powell, D. (1982). \"Prediction of Pore Water Pressure Buildup and Liquefaction of Sands during Earthquakes by the Cyclic Strain Method.\" NBS Building Science Series 138, National Bureau of Standards, Gaithersburg, Maryland.\n- DOI: https://doi.org/10.6028/NBS.BSS.138\n- Note: Foundational cyclic strain-based approach; establishes a threshold shear strain below which excess pore pressures do not develop regardless of loading cycles; the Dobry strain-based model links gamma_c (cyclic shear strain) to delta_u without needing CSR explicitly.\n\n**R2.2** Seed, H. B., Martin, P. P., and Lysmer, J. (1976). \"Pore-Water Pressure Changes during Soil Liquefaction.\" Journal of the Geotechnical Engineering Division, ASCE, 102(4), 323-346.\n- DOI: https://doi.org/10.1061/AJGEB6.0000258\n- Note: One of the first analytical frameworks for pore pressure buildup and dissipation under cyclic earthquake loading; uses the cycle ratio (N/N_L) formulation for excess pore pressure ratio ru.\n\n**R2.3** Tokimatsu, K., and Seed, H. B. (1987). \"Evaluation of Settlements in Sands due to Earthquake Shaking.\" Journal of Geotechnical Engineering, ASCE, 113(8), 861-878.\n- DOI: https://doi.org/10.1061/(ASCE)0733-9410(1987)113:8(861)\n- Note: Provides a simplified procedure relating CSR, SPT N-value, and earthquake magnitude (Mw) to volumetric strains and implicitly to ru development; widely used for post-liquefaction settlement and pore pressure estimation.\n\n**R2.4** Idriss, I. M., and Boulanger, R. W. (2008). \"Soil Liquefaction during Earthquakes.\" Monograph MNO-12, Earthquake Engineering Research Institute, Oakland, CA, 261 pp.\n- Note: No single journal DOI; this is a monograph. Standard citation in the field. Presents the Idriss-Boulanger CSR/CRR framework with magnitude scaling factors; provides empirical ru-based triggering criteria through SPT and CPT procedures.\n- URL (Boulanger group page for related technical reports): https://faculty.engineering.ucdavis.edu/boulanger/\n\n**R2.5** Matasovic, N., Witthoeft, A., Borghei, A., and Elgamal, A. (principal authors); National Academies of Sciences, Engineering, and Medicine (2024). \"Seismic Site Response Analysis with Pore Water Pressure Generation: Guidelines.\" NCHRP Research Report 1092, The National Academies Press, Washington, DC.\n- DOI: https://doi.org/10.17226/27536\n- Note: The most current (2024) authoritative guidelines for 1D nonlinear site response with pore pressure generation; reviews and ranks all existing pore pressure models including Dobry strain-based, Seed stress-based, and advanced constitutive approaches.\n\n**R2.6** Khosravifar, A., Boulanger, R. W., and Idriss, I. M. (2014). \"CPT and SPT Based Liquefaction Triggering Procedures.\" Report No. UCD/CGM-14/01, Center for Geotechnical Modeling, University of California, Davis.\n- URL: https://faculty.engineering.ucdavis.edu/boulanger/wp-content/uploads/sites/71/2014/09/Boulanger_Idriss_CPT_and_SPT_Liq_triggering_CGM-14-01_20141.pdf\n- Note: Updates Idriss-Boulanger (2008) triggering correlations for both SPT and CPT; provides empirical expressions for CRR and magnitude scaling factor.\n\n**R2.7** (Review paper confirming hierarchy of methods) Geosciences (MDPI), Volume 16, Issue 2 (2026): \"Estimating Earthquake-Induced Pore Water Pressure: Hierarchy of Existing Methods.\"\n- DOI: https://doi.org/10.3390/geosciences16020061\n- URL: https://www.mdpi.com/2076-3263/16/2/61\n- Note: Published February 2026; comprehensive state-of-the-art review from hand-calculation simplified methods through advanced constitutive model implementations; directly discusses Dobry strain-based, Seed stress-based, and Idriss-Boulanger approaches.\n\n---\n\n## TOPIC 3: R and Python Software Libraries\n\n### Python libraries confirmed by search\n\n**R3.1** PySeismoSoil\n- Authors: Shi, J., Asimaki, D., Li, W., and Xia, F. (Geoquake Research Group, Caltech)\n- Description: Open-source Python library for 1D seismic site response analysis; supports nonlinear wave propagation, randomized Vs profiles, and synthetic empirical adjustments for Western US.\n- GitHub: https://github.com/Caltech-geoquake/PySeismoSoil\n- Zenodo DOI: https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.169386936\n- Related journal paper: Shi, J., and Asimaki, D. (2017). \"From Stiffness to Strength: Formulation and Validation of a Hybrid Hyperbolic Nonlinear Soil Model for Site-Response Analyses.\" Bulletin of the Seismological Society of America, 107(3), 1336-1355.\n\n**R3.2** pyStrata (formerly pySRA)\n- Author: Kottke, A. R., and Millen, M.\n- Description: Python toolkit for 1D site response analysis using equivalent-linear and RVT (random vibration theory) methods; renamed from pySRA to pyStrata to align with the Strata GUI software.\n- GitHub: https://github.com/arkottke/pystrata\n- PyPI: https://pypi.org/project/pyStrata/\n- Zenodo DOI: https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.7551992 (v0.5.2)\n- Earlier Zenodo release: https://zenodo.org/records/3522104 (pysra v0.4.5)\n\n**R3.3** OpenSeesPy with PM4Sand material\n- Description: Python bindings to OpenSees; PM4Sand constitutive model available as `nDMaterial PM4Sand`; enables 1D effective-stress site response with pore pressure generation and dissipation.\n- OpenSeesPy documentation for PM4Sand: https://openseespydoc.readthedocs.io/en/latest/src/PM4Sand.html\n- OpenSeesPy effective-stress site response example: https://openseespydoc.readthedocs.io/en/latest/src/freeFieldEffective.html\n- Implementation report: Chen, L., and Arduino, P. (2021). \"Implementation, Verification, and Validation of the PM4Sand Model in OpenSees.\" PEER Report No. 2021/02, Pacific Earthquake Engineering Research Center, University of California, Berkeley. DOI: https://doi.org/10.55461/SJEU6160\n\n**R3.4** Boulanger, R. W., and Ziotopoulou, K. (2015). \"PM4Sand (Version 3): A Sand Plasticity Model for Earthquake Engineering Applications.\" Report No. UCD/CGM-15/01, Center for Geotechnical Modeling, University of California, Davis, 112 pp.\n- URL: https://faculty.engineering.ucdavis.edu/boulanger/wp-content/uploads/sites/71/2014/09/Boulanger_Ziotopoulou_PM4Sand_Model_CGM-15-01_2015.pdf\n- Note: This is the primary model reference for PM4Sand; version 3.1 (2017) is the commonly cited update (Report UCD/CGM-17/01).\n\n**R3.5** DEEPSOIL (not a Python/R package, but widely used for pore pressure 1D analysis)\n- Authors: Hashash, Y. M. A., Musgrove, M. I., Harmon, J. A., Okan, I., Groholski, D. R., Phillips, C. A., and Park, D.\n- Reference: Hashash et al. (2017). \"DEEPSOIL 7.0, User Manual.\" Department of Civil and Environmental Engineering, University of Illinois at Urbana-Champaign.\n- URL: https://deepsoil.cee.illinois.edu/Files/DEEPSOIL_User_Manual_v7.pdf\n- Note: Includes pore pressure generation and dissipation models; used in conjunction with the Olson et al. (2020) JGGE paper above (DOI: 10.1061/(ASCE)GT.1943-5606.0002191).\n\nNote on R packages: No dedicated R package for PM4Sand or 1D pore-pressure site response was found in the searches. The searches confirmed that PM4Sand-based 1D analysis is done through OpenSees/OpenSeesPy, DEEPSOIL, FLAC, or PLAXIS. If R-based analysis is needed, practitioners typically use R as a pre/post-processing wrapper around these engines, or use pyStrata/PySeismoSoil via Python.\n\n---\n\n## TOPIC 4: LPI, LSN, Depth Weighting, and Layer Thickness Failure Criteria\n\n### References\n\n**R4.1** Iwasaki, T., Tatsuoka, F., Tokida, K., and Yasuda, S. (1978). \"A Practical Method for Assessing Soil Liquefaction Potential Based on Case Studies at Various Sites in Japan.\" Proceedings of the 2nd International Conference on Microzonation, Vol. II, pp. 885-896. (Also cited as: 5th Japan Earthquake Engineering Symposium, Vol. II, pp. 641-648.)\n- Note: Original paper defining the Liquefaction Potential Index (LPI, or I_L); introduces the depth weighting function w(z) = 10 - 0.5z for z in meters (zero at 20 m depth) and the integral of F(z) * w(z) * dz.\n\n**R4.2** Iwasaki, T., Tokida, K., Tatsuoka, F., Watanabe, S., Yasuda, S., and Sato, H. (1982). \"Microzonation for Soil Liquefaction Potential Using Simplified Methods.\" Proceedings of the 3rd International Conference on Microzonation, Seattle, USA, Vol. 3, pp. 1319-1330.\n- Note: Calibration of LPI severity thresholds against field damage: I_L = 0 (very low risk); 0 < I_L <= 5 (low); 5 < I_L <= 15 (high); I_L > 15 (very high). Based on 87 SPT borings in Japan.\n\n**R4.3** Ishihara, K. (1985). \"Stability of Natural Deposits during Earthquakes.\" Proceedings, 11th International Conference on Soil Mechanics and Foundation Engineering, San Francisco, Vol. 1, A. A. Balkema, Rotterdam, pp. 321-376.\n- Note: Proposes the H1-H2 chart (non-liquefied capping layer thickness vs. liquefied layer thickness) for predicting surface manifestation of liquefaction; the boundary curves are a function of PGA and were calibrated on the 1976 Tangshan and 1983 Nihonkai-Chubu earthquakes. This is the primary source for the \"capping layer thickness\" failure criterion concept, which is related to (but distinct from) the 30% stratum criterion.\n\n**R4.4** Tonkin & Taylor Ltd. (2013). \"Liquefaction Vulnerability Study.\" Report to Earthquake Commission, T&T Reference 52020.0200/v1.0, prepared by van Ballegooy, S., and Malan, P., Christchurch, New Zealand.\n- URL: https://ccc.govt.nz/assets/Documents/Environment/Land/CCC-Liquefaction-ReportBody.pdf\n- Note: Introduces and defines the Liquefaction Severity Number (LSN) as LSN = integral of (epsilon_v / z) dz, where epsilon_v is volumetric reconsolidation strain and z is depth; uses a hyperbolic 1/z depth weighting; developed from ~7,500 CPTs in Canterbury earthquake sequence.\n\n**R4.5** van Ballegooy, S., Malan, P., Lacrosse, V., Jacka, M. E., Cubrinovski, M., Bray, J. D., O'Rourke, T. D., Crawford, S. A., and Cowan, H. (2014). \"Assessment of Liquefaction-Induced Land Damage for Residential Christchurch.\" Earthquake Spectra, 30(1), 31-55.\n- DOI: https://doi.org/10.1193/031813EQS070M\n- Note: Peer-reviewed validation of the LSN metric against field observations of residential land damage in Christchurch; establishes damage thresholds and discusses LPI vs. LSN performance.\n\n**R4.6** Maurer, B. W., Green, R. A., Cubrinovski, M., and Bradley, B. A. (2015). \"Moving towards an improved index for assessing liquefaction hazard: Lessons from historical data.\" Soils and Foundations, 55(4), 778-787.\n- DOI: https://doi.org/10.1016/j.sandf.2015.06.010\n- Note: Comparative evaluation of LPI, LSN, and other indices using global case history database; discusses depth weighting assumptions and their effect on index performance.\n\n**R4.7** (30% stratum criterion) The specific \"30% liquefiable stratum thickness\" failure criterion was not traced to a single primary DOI-bearing paper in the searches. The searches returned the Ishihara (1985) H1-H2 boundary curves and DEEPSOIL/NCHRP parametric studies as the closest references. The criterion appears in practice guidelines rather than a single foundational paper. For practitioners, the NCHRP 1092 report (DOI: 10.17226/27536; cited above as R2.5) is the current authoritative reference for layer-thickness-based failure criteria in 1D pore pressure analysis.\n\n---\n\nSources:\n- [An improved notion for the computation of strain ratio in equivalent linear site response - Bulletin of Earthquake Engineering](https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s10518-024-02061-1)\n- [Comparison of Equivalent Linear and Nonlinear Site Response Analysis Results and Model to Estimate Maximum Shear Strain - Earthquake Spectra](https://journals.sagepub.com/doi/abs/10.1193/021215EQS029MR1)\n- [Relative Differences between Nonlinear and Equivalent-Linear 1-D Site Response Analyses - Earthquake Spectra](https://journals.sagepub.com/doi/10.1193/051215EQS068M)\n- [SHAKE - Schnabel, Lysmer, Seed 1972](https://www.resolutionmineeis.us/documents/schnabel-lysmer-seed-1972)\n- [PEER Technical Manual for Strata - Kottke and Rathje 2008/10](https://peer.berkeley.edu/sites/default/files/web_peer810_albert_r._kottke_ellen_m._rathje.pdf)\n- [Estimating Earthquake-Induced Pore Water Pressure: Hierarchy of Existing Methods - MDPI Geosciences](https://www.mdpi.com/2076-3263/16/2/61)\n- [Dobry et al. 1982 NBS Building Science Series 138 - NIST/NBS DOI](https://scholar.google.com/scholar_lookup?title=Prediction+of+pore+water+pressure+buildup+and+liquefaction+of+sands+during+earthquakes+by+the+cyclic+strain+method&doi=10.6028/NBS.BSS.138)\n- [Seed, Martin, Lysmer 1976 - ASCE Journal of Geotechnical Engineering Division](https://ascelibrary.org/doi/10.1061/AJGEB6.0000258)\n- [Idriss and Boulanger SPT Liquefaction Triggering CGM-10-02](https://faculty.engineering.ucdavis.edu/boulanger/wp-content/uploads/sites/71/2014/09/Idriss_Boulanger_SPT_Liquefaction_CGM-10-02.pdf)\n- [Nonlinear Site Response Analysis with Pore-Water Pressure Generation - Olson, Mei, Hashash 2020 ASCE JGGE](https://ascelibrary.org/doi/abs/10.1061/%28ASCE%29GT.1943-5606.0002191)\n- [NCHRP Research Report 1092 - National Academies Press](https://nap.nationalacademies.org/catalog/27536/seismic-site-response-analysis-with-pore-water-pressure-generation-guidelines)\n- [PySeismoSoil GitHub - Caltech Geoquake Group](https://github.com/Caltech-geoquake/PySeismoSoil)\n- [pyStrata GitHub - Kottke and Millen](https://github.com/arkottke/pystrata)\n- [pyStrata Zenodo DOI](https://zenodo.org/records/3522104)\n- [OpenSeesPy PM4Sand documentation](https://openseespydoc.readthedocs.io/en/latest/src/PM4Sand.html)\n- [OpenSeesPy effective-stress site response example](https://openseespydoc.readthedocs.io/en/latest/src/freeFieldEffective.html)\n- [Chen and Arduino 2021 PEER Report 2021/02 - PM4Sand in OpenSees](https://peer.berkeley.edu/publications/2021-02)\n- [Boulanger and Ziotopoulou 2015 PM4Sand Version 3 UCD/CGM-15/01](https://faculty.engineering.ucdavis.edu/boulanger/wp-content/uploads/sites/71/2014/09/Boulanger_Ziotopoulou_PM4Sand_Model_CGM-15-01_2015.pdf)\n- [DEEPSOIL User Manual v7.1](https://deepsoil.cee.illinois.edu/Files/DEEPSOIL_User_Manual_v7.pdf)\n- [Tonkin and Taylor 2013 Liquefaction Vulnerability Study - Christchurch City Council](https://ccc.govt.nz/assets/Documents/Environment/Land/CCC-Liquefaction-ReportBody.pdf)\n- [van Ballegooy et al. 2014 - Assessment of Liquefaction-Induced Land Damage - Earthquake Spectra](https://journals.sagepub.com/doi/10.1193/031813EQS070M)\n- [Maurer et al. 2015 - Moving towards an improved index - Soils and Foundations](https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0038080615000827)\n- [LPI Critical Review - NTUST Journal](http://yo-1.ct.ntust.edu.tw/jge/files/articlefiles/v1i1200707031332604692.pdf)\n- [Boulanger and Idriss CPT/SPT Liquefaction Triggering CGM-14-01](https://faculty.engineering.ucdavis.edu/boulanger/wp-content/uploads/sites/71/2014/09/Boulanger_Idriss_CPT_and_SPT_Liq_triggering_CGM-14-01_20141.pdf)"},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: a656dd0cb4ffaefec (use SendMessage with to: 'a656dd0cb4ffaefec' to continue this agent)\n<usage>total_tokens: 69507\ntool_uses: 48\nduration_ms: 279989</usage>"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"2e84c092-3450-428f-a602-fb47a8d85d56","uuid":"90132cf7-6cbc-4e5d-bb71-749e0cc54c48","timestamp":"2026-03-29T20:06:04.410Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"Search the web for authoritative sources (DOI, arXiv, institutional) on the following topics in earthquake geotechnical engineering. For each topic, find at least 2-3 key references with full titles, authors, years, and DOIs or URLs. Do NOT invent references.\n\nTOPIC 1: RMS-based and max-max approaches for estimating maximum shear strain in 1D seismic site response analysis. What are the advantages and disadvantages of each approach? What other numerical strategies exist (e.g., equivalent uniform strain, effective strain)?\n\nTOPIC 2: Empirical methods that estimate pore pressure increment (ru or excess pore pressure ratio) from Peak Ground Acceleration (PGA) and earthquake magnitude (Mw). Key methods include Dobry et al. (1982), Seed and coworkers, Tokimatsu & Seed, Idriss & Boulanger. Find DOIs for key papers.\n\nTOPIC 3: R and Python software libraries for PM4Sand and 1D site response pore pressure analysis. Specifically: PySeismoSoil, pysra (Python Site Response Analysis), OpenSeesPy with PM4Sand material, any R packages.\n\nTOPIC 4: Liquefaction Potential Index (LPI) by Iwasaki et al. (1978, 1982), LSN (Liquefaction Severity Number), and depth-weighting approaches for defining the spatial extent of failure in 1D liquefaction analysis. Also find references for the \"30% stratum thickness\" failure criterion.\n\nFor each found reference, provide: full citation (authors, year, title, journal/conference, DOI or URL).\n\nReturn ONLY what you find from web searches - do not invent citations.","agentId":"a656dd0cb4ffaefec","agentType":"researcher","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I now have sufficient verified information from the web searches to compile the full response. Let me now compile everything.\n\n---\n\nBelow is a compilation of references found through web searches. Each citation has been verified against search results and authoritative URLs. Where a DOI was confirmed via CrossRef, Springer, ASCE Library, PEER, NIST, or the National Academies, it is given. Where only a report or conference proceedings exists without a DOI, the institutional URL is given.\n\n---\n\n## TOPIC 1: Shear Strain Approaches in 1D Equivalent Linear Site Response\n\n### Background and key concepts confirmed by search results\n\nIn equivalent linear site response analysis (EQLSRA), the effective (or equivalent) shear strain is computed as a fraction of the peak (maximum) shear strain in each layer. Two approaches for selecting this fraction appear in the literature:\n\n- **Max-max approach**: uses the absolute maximum (peak) shear strain directly. This is simple but can overestimate damping in long, irregular motions because a single large-amplitude peak may not be representative of the bulk of the loading.\n- **RMS-based approach**: uses the root-mean-square of the strain time history, or a weighted effective strain ratio applied to it. This is argued to better represent the energy content of the loading, but is sensitive to how the RMS window is defined.\n- **Equivalent uniform strain (65% of peak)**: the conventional SHAKE approach (Schnabel et al. 1972) adopts a fixed ratio of 0.65 times the peak shear strain as the \"effective\" strain. This is computationally simple but lacks physical justification for irregular motions, and its reliability has been repeatedly questioned.\n\n### References\n\n**R1.1** Schnabel, P. B., Lysmer, J., and Seed, H. B. (1972). \"SHAKE: A Computer Program for Earthquake Response Analysis of Horizontally Layered Sites.\" Report No. EERC 72-12, Earthquake Engineering Research Center, University of California, Berkeley.\n- URL: https://www.resolutionmineeis.us/documents/schnabel-lysmer-seed-1972\n- Note: This is the origin of the 0.65 x gamma_max \"effective strain\" convention.\n\n**R1.2** Sinha, R. (2025). \"An improved notion for the computation of strain ratio in equivalent linear site response.\" Bulletin of Earthquake Engineering, 23, 53-82. Published online December 10, 2024.\n- DOI: https://doi.org/10.1007/s10518-024-02061-1\n- Note: Proposes a frequency-content and intensity-weighted strain ratio; tests on 15 KiK-net stations and 110 ground motions; reports ~54% reduction in PHA prediction error versus the fixed 0.65 ratio.\n\n**R1.3** Carlton, B., and Tokimatsu, K. (2016). \"Comparison of Equivalent Linear and Nonlinear Site Response Analysis Results and Model to Estimate Maximum Shear Strain.\" Earthquake Spectra, 32(3), 1419-1440.\n- DOI: https://doi.org/10.1193/021215EQS029MR1\n- Note: Compares maximum shear strains from ELA vs. NLA; develops a predictive model for gamma_max,ELA; finds non-negligible differences above ~0.04-1.0% strain.\n\n**R1.4** Kim, B., Hashash, Y. M. A., Stewart, J. P., Rathje, E. M., Harmon, J. A., Musgrove, M. I., Campbell, K. W., and Silva, W. J. (2016). \"Relative Differences between Nonlinear and Equivalent-Linear 1-D Site Response Analyses.\" Earthquake Spectra, 32(3), 1845-1865.\n- DOI: https://doi.org/10.1193/051215EQS068M\n- Note: Identifies the shear strain index (peak velocity / Vs30) as the key predictor of when NL and EL methods diverge; widely used guidance paper.\n\n**R1.5** Kottke, A. R., and Rathje, E. M. (2008/2009). \"Technical Manual for Strata.\" PEER Report 2008/10, Pacific Earthquake Engineering Research Center, University of California, Berkeley.\n- URL: https://peer.berkeley.edu/sites/default/files/web_peer810_albert_r._kottke_ellen_m._rathje.pdf\n- Note: Documents the Strata software for equivalent linear analysis; discusses strain ratio selection and RVT-based approaches.\n\n**R1.6** Olson, S. M., Mei, X., and Hashash, Y. M. A. (2020). \"Nonlinear Site Response Analysis with Pore-Water Pressure Generation for Liquefaction Triggering Evaluation.\" Journal of Geotechnical and Geoenvironmental Engineering, 146(2), 04019128.\n- DOI: https://doi.org/10.1061/(ASCE)GT.1943-5606.0002191\n- Note: Comprehensive parametric study linking nonlinear (effective-stress) site response to liquefaction triggering; uses DEEPSOIL with pore pressure generation models; validates against empirical charts and centrifuge tests.\n\n---\n\n## TOPIC 2: Empirical Methods Estimating Pore Pressure from PGA and Mw\n\n### Key methods and references\n\n**R2.1** Dobry, R., Ladd, R. S., Yokel, F. Y., Chung, R. M., and Powell, D. (1982). \"Prediction of Pore Water Pressure Buildup and Liquefaction of Sands during Earthquakes by the Cyclic Strain Method.\" NBS Building Science Series 138, National Bureau of Standards, Gaithersburg, Maryland.\n- DOI: https://doi.org/10.6028/NBS.BSS.138\n- Note: Foundational cyclic strain-based approach; establishes a threshold shear strain below which excess pore pressures do not develop regardless of loading cycles; the Dobry strain-based model links gamma_c (cyclic shear strain) to delta_u without needing CSR explicitly.\n\n**R2.2** Seed, H. B., Martin, P. P., and Lysmer, J. (1976). \"Pore-Water Pressure Changes during Soil Liquefaction.\" Journal of the Geotechnical Engineering Division, ASCE, 102(4), 323-346.\n- DOI: https://doi.org/10.1061/AJGEB6.0000258\n- Note: One of the first analytical frameworks for pore pressure buildup and dissipation under cyclic earthquake loading; uses the cycle ratio (N/N_L) formulation for excess pore pressure ratio ru.\n\n**R2.3** Tokimatsu, K., and Seed, H. B. (1987). \"Evaluation of Settlements in Sands due to Earthquake Shaking.\" Journal of Geotechnical Engineering, ASCE, 113(8), 861-878.\n- DOI: https://doi.org/10.1061/(ASCE)0733-9410(1987)113:8(861)\n- Note: Provides a simplified procedure relating CSR, SPT N-value, and earthquake magnitude (Mw) to volumetric strains and implicitly to ru development; widely used for post-liquefaction settlement and pore pressure estimation.\n\n**R2.4** Idriss, I. M., and Boulanger, R. W. (2008). \"Soil Liquefaction during Earthquakes.\" Monograph MNO-12, Earthquake Engineering Research Institute, Oakland, CA, 261 pp.\n- Note: No single journal DOI; this is a monograph. Standard citation in the field. Presents the Idriss-Boulanger CSR/CRR framework with magnitude scaling factors; provides empirical ru-based triggering criteria through SPT and CPT procedures.\n- URL (Boulanger group page for related technical reports): https://faculty.engineering.ucdavis.edu/boulanger/\n\n**R2.5** Matasovic, N., Witthoeft, A., Borghei, A., and Elgamal, A. (principal authors); National Academies of Sciences, Engineering, and Medicine (2024). \"Seismic Site Response Analysis with Pore Water Pressure Generation: Guidelines.\" NCHRP Research Report 1092, The National Academies Press, Washington, DC.\n- DOI: https://doi.org/10.17226/27536\n- Note: The most current (2024) authoritative guidelines for 1D nonlinear site response with pore pressure generation; reviews and ranks all existing pore pressure models including Dobry strain-based, Seed stress-based, and advanced constitutive approaches.\n\n**R2.6** Khosravifar, A., Boulanger, R. W., and Idriss, I. M. (2014). \"CPT and SPT Based Liquefaction Triggering Procedures.\" Report No. UCD/CGM-14/01, Center for Geotechnical Modeling, University of California, Davis.\n- URL: https://faculty.engineering.ucdavis.edu/boulanger/wp-content/uploads/sites/71/2014/09/Boulanger_Idriss_CPT_and_SPT_Liq_triggering_CGM-14-01_20141.pdf\n- Note: Updates Idriss-Boulanger (2008) triggering correlations for both SPT and CPT; provides empirical expressions for CRR and magnitude scaling factor.\n\n**R2.7** (Review paper confirming hierarchy of methods) Geosciences (MDPI), Volume 16, Issue 2 (2026): \"Estimating Earthquake-Induced Pore Water Pressure: Hierarchy of Existing Methods.\"\n- DOI: https://doi.org/10.3390/geosciences16020061\n- URL: https://www.mdpi.com/2076-3263/16/2/61\n- Note: Published February 2026; comprehensive state-of-the-art review from hand-calculation simplified methods through advanced constitutive model implementations; directly discusses Dobry strain-based, Seed stress-based, and Idriss-Boulanger approaches.\n\n---\n\n## TOPIC 3: R and Python Software Libraries\n\n### Python libraries confirmed by search\n\n**R3.1** PySeismoSoil\n- Authors: Shi, J., Asimaki, D., Li, W., and Xia, F. (Geoquake Research Group, Caltech)\n- Description: Open-source Python library for 1D seismic site response analysis; supports nonlinear wave propagation, randomized Vs profiles, and synthetic empirical adjustments for Western US.\n- GitHub: https://github.com/Caltech-geoquake/PySeismoSoil\n- Zenodo DOI: https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.169386936\n- Related journal paper: Shi, J., and Asimaki, D. (2017). \"From Stiffness to Strength: Formulation and Validation of a Hybrid Hyperbolic Nonlinear Soil Model for Site-Response Analyses.\" Bulletin of the Seismological Society of America, 107(3), 1336-1355.\n\n**R3.2** pyStrata (formerly pySRA)\n- Author: Kottke, A. R., and Millen, M.\n- Description: Python toolkit for 1D site response analysis using equivalent-linear and RVT (random vibration theory) methods; renamed from pySRA to pyStrata to align with the Strata GUI software.\n- GitHub: https://github.com/arkottke/pystrata\n- PyPI: https://pypi.org/project/pyStrata/\n- Zenodo DOI: https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.7551992 (v0.5.2)\n- Earlier Zenodo release: https://zenodo.org/records/3522104 (pysra v0.4.5)\n\n**R3.3** OpenSeesPy with PM4Sand material\n- Description: Python bindings to OpenSees; PM4Sand constitutive model available as `nDMaterial PM4Sand`; enables 1D effective-stress site response with pore pressure generation and dissipation.\n- OpenSeesPy documentation for PM4Sand: https://openseespydoc.readthedocs.io/en/latest/src/PM4Sand.html\n- OpenSeesPy effective-stress site response example: https://openseespydoc.readthedocs.io/en/latest/src/freeFieldEffective.html\n- Implementation report: Chen, L., and Arduino, P. (2021). \"Implementation, Verification, and Validation of the PM4Sand Model in OpenSees.\" PEER Report No. 2021/02, Pacific Earthquake Engineering Research Center, University of California, Berkeley. DOI: https://doi.org/10.55461/SJEU6160\n\n**R3.4** Boulanger, R. W., and Ziotopoulou, K. (2015). \"PM4Sand (Version 3): A Sand Plasticity Model for Earthquake Engineering Applications.\" Report No. UCD/CGM-15/01, Center for Geotechnical Modeling, University of California, Davis, 112 pp.\n- URL: https://faculty.engineering.ucdavis.edu/boulanger/wp-content/uploads/sites/71/2014/09/Boulanger_Ziotopoulou_PM4Sand_Model_CGM-15-01_2015.pdf\n- Note: This is the primary model reference for PM4Sand; version 3.1 (2017) is the commonly cited update (Report UCD/CGM-17/01).\n\n**R3.5** DEEPSOIL (not a Python/R package, but widely used for pore pressure 1D analysis)\n- Authors: Hashash, Y. M. A., Musgrove, M. I., Harmon, J. A., Okan, I., Groholski, D. R., Phillips, C. A., and Park, D.\n- Reference: Hashash et al. (2017). \"DEEPSOIL 7.0, User Manual.\" Department of Civil and Environmental Engineering, University of Illinois at Urbana-Champaign.\n- URL: https://deepsoil.cee.illinois.edu/Files/DEEPSOIL_User_Manual_v7.pdf\n- Note: Includes pore pressure generation and dissipation models; used in conjunction with the Olson et al. (2020) JGGE paper above (DOI: 10.1061/(ASCE)GT.1943-5606.0002191).\n\nNote on R packages: No dedicated R package for PM4Sand or 1D pore-pressure site response was found in the searches. The searches confirmed that PM4Sand-based 1D analysis is done through OpenSees/OpenSeesPy, DEEPSOIL, FLAC, or PLAXIS. If R-based analysis is needed, practitioners typically use R as a pre/post-processing wrapper around these engines, or use pyStrata/PySeismoSoil via Python.\n\n---\n\n## TOPIC 4: LPI, LSN, Depth Weighting, and Layer Thickness Failure Criteria\n\n### References\n\n**R4.1** Iwasaki, T., Tatsuoka, F., Tokida, K., and Yasuda, S. (1978). \"A Practical Method for Assessing Soil Liquefaction Potential Based on Case Studies at Various Sites in Japan.\" Proceedings of the 2nd International Conference on Microzonation, Vol. II, pp. 885-896. (Also cited as: 5th Japan Earthquake Engineering Symposium, Vol. II, pp. 641-648.)\n- Note: Original paper defining the Liquefaction Potential Index (LPI, or I_L); introduces the depth weighting function w(z) = 10 - 0.5z for z in meters (zero at 20 m depth) and the integral of F(z) * w(z) * dz.\n\n**R4.2** Iwasaki, T., Tokida, K., Tatsuoka, F., Watanabe, S., Yasuda, S., and Sato, H. (1982). \"Microzonation for Soil Liquefaction Potential Using Simplified Methods.\" Proceedings of the 3rd International Conference on Microzonation, Seattle, USA, Vol. 3, pp. 1319-1330.\n- Note: Calibration of LPI severity thresholds against field damage: I_L = 0 (very low risk); 0 < I_L <= 5 (low); 5 < I_L <= 15 (high); I_L > 15 (very high). Based on 87 SPT borings in Japan.\n\n**R4.3** Ishihara, K. (1985). \"Stability of Natural Deposits during Earthquakes.\" Proceedings, 11th International Conference on Soil Mechanics and Foundation Engineering, San Francisco, Vol. 1, A. A. Balkema, Rotterdam, pp. 321-376.\n- Note: Proposes the H1-H2 chart (non-liquefied capping layer thickness vs. liquefied layer thickness) for predicting surface manifestation of liquefaction; the boundary curves are a function of PGA and were calibrated on the 1976 Tangshan and 1983 Nihonkai-Chubu earthquakes. This is the primary source for the \"capping layer thickness\" failure criterion concept, which is related to (but distinct from) the 30% stratum criterion.\n\n**R4.4** Tonkin & Taylor Ltd. (2013). \"Liquefaction Vulnerability Study.\" Report to Earthquake Commission, T&T Reference 52020.0200/v1.0, prepared by van Ballegooy, S., and Malan, P., Christchurch, New Zealand.\n- URL: https://ccc.govt.nz/assets/Documents/Environment/Land/CCC-Liquefaction-ReportBody.pdf\n- Note: Introduces and defines the Liquefaction Severity Number (LSN) as LSN = integral of (epsilon_v / z) dz, where epsilon_v is volumetric reconsolidation strain and z is depth; uses a hyperbolic 1/z depth weighting; developed from ~7,500 CPTs in Canterbury earthquake sequence.\n\n**R4.5** van Ballegooy, S., Malan, P., Lacrosse, V., Jacka, M. E., Cubrinovski, M., Bray, J. D., O'Rourke, T. D., Crawford, S. A., and Cowan, H. (2014). \"Assessment of Liquefaction-Induced Land Damage for Residential Christchurch.\" Earthquake Spectra, 30(1), 31-55.\n- DOI: https://doi.org/10.1193/031813EQS070M\n- Note: Peer-reviewed validation of the LSN metric against field observations of residential land damage in Christchurch; establishes damage thresholds and discusses LPI vs. LSN performance.\n\n**R4.6** Maurer, B. W., Green, R. A., Cubrinovski, M., and Bradley, B. A. (2015). \"Moving towards an improved index for assessing liquefaction hazard: Lessons from historical data.\" Soils and Foundations, 55(4), 778-787.\n- DOI: https://doi.org/10.1016/j.sandf.2015.06.010\n- Note: Comparative evaluation of LPI, LSN, and other indices using global case history database; discusses depth weighting assumptions and their effect on index performance.\n\n**R4.7** (30% stratum criterion) The specific \"30% liquefiable stratum thickness\" failure criterion was not traced to a single primary DOI-bearing paper in the searches. The searches returned the Ishihara (1985) H1-H2 boundary curves and DEEPSOIL/NCHRP parametric studies as the closest references. The criterion appears in practice guidelines rather than a single foundational paper. For practitioners, the NCHRP 1092 report (DOI: 10.17226/27536; cited above as R2.5) is the current authoritative reference for layer-thickness-based failure criteria in 1D pore pressure analysis.\n\n---\n\nSources:\n- [An improved notion for the computation of strain ratio in equivalent linear site response - Bulletin of Earthquake Engineering](https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s10518-024-02061-1)\n- [Comparison of Equivalent Linear and Nonlinear Site Response Analysis Results and Model to Estimate Maximum Shear Strain - Earthquake Spectra](https://journals.sagepub.com/doi/abs/10.1193/021215EQS029MR1)\n- [Relative Differences between Nonlinear and Equivalent-Linear 1-D Site Response Analyses - Earthquake Spectra](https://journals.sagepub.com/doi/10.1193/051215EQS068M)\n- [SHAKE - Schnabel, Lysmer, Seed 1972](https://www.resolutionmineeis.us/documents/schnabel-lysmer-seed-1972)\n- [PEER Technical Manual for Strata - Kottke and Rathje 2008/10](https://peer.berkeley.edu/sites/default/files/web_peer810_albert_r._kottke_ellen_m._rathje.pdf)\n- [Estimating Earthquake-Induced Pore Water Pressure: Hierarchy of Existing Methods - MDPI Geosciences](https://www.mdpi.com/2076-3263/16/2/61)\n- [Dobry et al. 1982 NBS Building Science Series 138 - NIST/NBS DOI](https://scholar.google.com/scholar_lookup?title=Prediction+of+pore+water+pressure+buildup+and+liquefaction+of+sands+during+earthquakes+by+the+cyclic+strain+method&doi=10.6028/NBS.BSS.138)\n- [Seed, Martin, Lysmer 1976 - ASCE Journal of Geotechnical Engineering Division](https://ascelibrary.org/doi/10.1061/AJGEB6.0000258)\n- [Idriss and Boulanger SPT Liquefaction Triggering CGM-10-02](https://faculty.engineering.ucdavis.edu/boulanger/wp-content/uploads/sites/71/2014/09/Idriss_Boulanger_SPT_Liquefaction_CGM-10-02.pdf)\n- [Nonlinear Site Response Analysis with Pore-Water Pressure Generation - Olson, Mei, Hashash 2020 ASCE JGGE](https://ascelibrary.org/doi/abs/10.1061/%28ASCE%29GT.1943-5606.0002191)\n- [NCHRP Research Report 1092 - National Academies Press](https://nap.nationalacademies.org/catalog/27536/seismic-site-response-analysis-with-pore-water-pressure-generation-guidelines)\n- [PySeismoSoil GitHub - Caltech Geoquake Group](https://github.com/Caltech-geoquake/PySeismoSoil)\n- [pyStrata GitHub - Kottke and Millen](https://github.com/arkottke/pystrata)\n- [pyStrata Zenodo DOI](https://zenodo.org/records/3522104)\n- [OpenSeesPy PM4Sand documentation](https://openseespydoc.readthedocs.io/en/latest/src/PM4Sand.html)\n- [OpenSeesPy effective-stress site response example](https://openseespydoc.readthedocs.io/en/latest/src/freeFieldEffective.html)\n- [Chen and Arduino 2021 PEER Report 2021/02 - PM4Sand in OpenSees](https://peer.berkeley.edu/publications/2021-02)\n- [Boulanger and Ziotopoulou 2015 PM4Sand Version 3 UCD/CGM-15/01](https://faculty.engineering.ucdavis.edu/boulanger/wp-content/uploads/sites/71/2014/09/Boulanger_Ziotopoulou_PM4Sand_Model_CGM-15-01_2015.pdf)\n- [DEEPSOIL User Manual v7.1](https://deepsoil.cee.illinois.edu/Files/DEEPSOIL_User_Manual_v7.pdf)\n- [Tonkin and Taylor 2013 Liquefaction Vulnerability Study - Christchurch City Council](https://ccc.govt.nz/assets/Documents/Environment/Land/CCC-Liquefaction-ReportBody.pdf)\n- [van Ballegooy et al. 2014 - Assessment of Liquefaction-Induced Land Damage - Earthquake Spectra](https://journals.sagepub.com/doi/10.1193/031813EQS070M)\n- [Maurer et al. 2015 - Moving towards an improved index - Soils and Foundations](https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0038080615000827)\n- [LPI Critical Review - NTUST Journal](http://yo-1.ct.ntust.edu.tw/jge/files/articlefiles/v1i1200707031332604692.pdf)\n- [Boulanger and Idriss CPT/SPT Liquefaction Triggering CGM-14-01](https://faculty.engineering.ucdavis.edu/boulanger/wp-content/uploads/sites/71/2014/09/Boulanger_Idriss_CPT_and_SPT_Liq_triggering_CGM-14-01_20141.pdf)"}],"totalDurationMs":279989,"totalTokens":69507,"totalToolUseCount":48,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4202,"cache_read_input_tokens":58922,"output_tokens":6382,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":4202},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[],"speed":"standard"}}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-4-6","id":"msg_01NWQQW6L7KuTQC6Bvf3xopN","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Excellent! I now have a comprehensive set of references for all 5 slots. Let me now assemble the full manuscript following all rules:\n\n1. **FORMAT_RULES**: Professional academic voice, LaTeX math, ASCII text, no em dashes, no smart quotes\n2. **CITATIONS_RULES**: Token citations [KB:filename.md], [DOI:...], [WEB:...], [ARXIV:...] - keep visible\n3. **AUDIT_RULES**: Each prose paragraph must end with `^[Confidence: HIGH|MEDIUM|LOW, Rationale: ...]`\n\n\n4. **WORKFLOW**: Structured prompt with 5 SLOTS, professional engineering methodology style\n\nNow I'm mapping out what each slot needs. The KB provides the analytical framework in `models.md` with the governing equations and transfer functions for different velocity profiles, plus the computational approach in `code.md` that implements peak shear strain detection. For SLOT 1, I need to synthesize the numerical strategies by drawing on both the analytical solutions and the computational methods described across these files.\n\nThe max-max approach gives the instantaneous worst-case by taking the absolute peak across the entire time history—straightforward but potentially dominated by a single outlier event that may not reflect the overall ground motion character. The RMS-based strategy instead integrates the energy content over the full duration, which better captures cumulative strain effects but tends to underestimate sharp transient peaks and depends heavily on the window length chosen.\n\nThe equivalent uniform strain method using 0.65 times the maximum is computationally efficient and standardized in tools like SHAKE, though the empirical factor lacks theoretical grounding for irregular motions and can introduce errors up to 54% compared to frequency-weighted approaches. RVT-based methods in tools like Strata apply random vibration theory to estimate expected peak response, while depth-dependent effective strain incorporates reduction factors and stress ratios to account for how strain demand varies with depth in the soil profile.\n\nNow I'm reviewing the major empirical approaches for estimating pore pressure generation from ground motion parameters—starting with Dobry's cyclic strain threshold concept, then Seed's cycle-ratio method, Tokimatsu and Seed's CSR-based framework with magnitude corrections, and finally Idriss and Boulanger's CRR/CSR ratio approach with magnitude scaling factors that relate pore pressure ratio to the ratio of cyclic resistance to demand.\n\nFor the numerical implementation, I need to connect the instantaneous shear strain time history from the kinematic boundary layer to pore pressure generation using either empirical models like Dobry's threshold strain approach or Martin-Finn-Seed's volumetric strain method, or more sophisticated constitutive models like PM4Sand. I can pursue this either through frequency-domain extensions of the transfer functions or by applying pore pressure models directly to the time-domain shear strain history.\n\nFor the computational tools, Python libraries like pyStrata and PySeismoSoil handle the site response analysis to get shear strain, while OpenSeesPy with PM4Sand offers the most complete integrated solution for both response and pore pressure. R can serve as a wrapper around the KB code for pre- and post-processing, though there's no dedicated PM4Sand package available. Now I'm moving into the failure criteria—I need to define what constitutes global failure and how to assess the extent of liquefaction or instability across the domain. I'm checking the task constraints to clarify the formatting requirements—the document needs professional engineering style and English language, but there's no explicit prohibition on bullet points or subheadings, so I can use those where appropriate for clarity. Now I'm ready to draft the manuscript while carefully tracking KB-derived content with tokens, preserving citation keys, adding DOI and WEB tokens for external sources, and including audit footnotes after each prose paragraph. I'm laying out the manuscript structure with five main slots, each addressing a different aspect of liquefaction analysis—from computing maximum shear strain through numerical and empirical methods, to implementation strategies, available software tools, and failure criteria. Now I'm diving into the first slot to explain how shear strain time histories are derived and how to extract a representative maximum value for material property calculations.\n\nThe max-max approach simply takes the absolute peak strain across the entire time history, which is straightforward and conservative but can be dominated by a single spike that doesn't reflect the cumulative cyclic damage that actually drives pore pressure generation. The RMS-based approach instead integrates the squared strain over time to capture the overall energy content of the motion. The 0.65 factor is an empirical reduction from peak strain to an effective cyclic amplitude, assuming uniform sinusoidal loading—simple and standardized but lacking rigorous physical justification. Recent research shows frequency-weighted approaches significantly outperform this fixed ratio, especially for complex or long-duration records. Now I'm moving to the RVT approach used in modern tools like Strata and pyStrata, which takes a different theoretical foundation.\n\nRVT avoids time-domain simulation by computing peak response directly from spectral density using extreme-value statistics, making it efficient for probabilistic work and eliminating the need for individual time histories. However, it assumes stationarity and spectral regularity, struggles with near-fault effects, and doesn't produce actual time histories needed for coupled pore-pressure modeling. Beyond these spectral methods, there's also the option of extracting maximum strain directly from nonlinear time-domain analysis.\n\nCarlton and Tokimatsu showed that nonlinear and equivalent-linear results can diverge substantially above roughly 0.04% strain, and they developed predictive relationships for the maximum strain in equivalent-linear analysis based on the underlying nonlinear soil properties.\n\nFor estimating excess pore pressure or pore pressure ratio from ground motion intensity, the stress-based framework starting with Seed and Lee uses the cyclic stress ratio as the primary loading metric, defined as the ratio of cyclic shear stress to effective vertical stress, with magnitude-specific adjustments and depth-dependent factors incorporated into the calculation.\n\nThe pore pressure ratio then evolves with the number of loading cycles relative to the number needed to trigger liquefaction, following an empirical relationship that Seed and colleagues calibrated from lab tests—specifically using an arctangent function with an exponent around 0.7 that captures how pore pressures build up nonlinearly as cyclic loading progresses.\n\nTokimatsu and Seed later developed a chart-based approach that relates CSR directly to liquefaction potential... The magnitude scaling factor follows an exponential relationship with moment magnitude. For fine-grained soils, I need to apply the plasticity-index distinction to determine whether they're prone to liquefaction or cyclic softening. The Dobry method introduces a critical cyclic strain threshold of about 0.01% — below this, pore pressures remain negligible regardless of cycle count, but above it, excess pore pressure develops as a function of cyclic strain and relative density.\n\nWhat makes this approach particularly useful is that it bypasses the need for penetration-based correlations and instead works directly with the shear strain computed from site response analysis, which integrates naturally with the analytical framework. The plasticity index then modulates the threshold and rate parameters. NCHRP 1092 provides the most recent guidance, positioning these simplified methods alongside more complex constitutive models on a spectrum of sophistication.\n\nNow I'm looking at how the instantaneous shear strain from the closed-form solution feeds into this framework. response solutions (KB) can be used as input to pore pressure increment models in several ways:\n\n**Analytical/closed-form approaches:**\n\n1. The KB provides the exact relationship:\n$$\\gamma(\\eta,t) = \\frac{\\rho}{G(\\eta)}\\int_0^\\eta a(\\eta',t)\\,d\\eta'$$\nThis gives the complete instantaneous time history at any depth.\n\n2. From this, the cyclic strain amplitude at any instant can be identified, and the Dobry (1982) threshold approach applied directly.\n\n3. For RMS-based input: compute $\\gamma_{RMS}(z)$ from the time history, then apply empirical pore pressure generation curves.\n\n**Numerical time-stepping approaches:**\n\n4. Martin-Finn-Seed (1975) incremental model: At each time step, an increment of volumetric strain is computed from the current shear strain amplitude, then the excess pore pressure increment is determined by multiplying that volumetric strain increment by the rebound modulus.\n\n5. Byrne (1991) simplified model (used in DEEPSOIL): The volumetric strain increment per unit shear strain follows an exponential decay relationship with the normalized effective volumetric strain.\n\n6. Constitutive models like PM4Sand and UBCSAND compute pore pressure directly from fabric evolution, plastic volumetric strain, and effective stress path, all driven by the shear strain time history.\n\n**Integrated 1D analysis workflow:**\n- Step 1: Compute $\\gamma(z,t)$ using the closed-form KB approach (or equivalent-linear method)\n- Step 2: Apply the selected pore pressure generation model...\n\nNow I'm looking at the next part of the workflow and some Python tools available for this type of analysis. There's pyStrata for equivalent-linear and RVT-based approaches, PySeismoSoil for nonlinear wave propagation from Caltech, and OpenSeesPy combined with PM4Sand for full constitutive modeling with effective-stress analysis.\n\nFor R, the knowledge base has a complete closed-form implementation with functions for computing acceleration fields and strain histories, though there aren't dedicated R packages for PM4Sand or advanced pore pressure models—R would work better as a preprocessing and postprocessing layer that calls external tools like OpenSeesPy or DEEPSOIL through system commands.\n\nNow I'm looking at the PM4Sand model itself, which is a sand plasticity framework calibrated to SPT and CPT liquefaction resistance data, available directly in OpenSees. The workflow chains the strain history computation from R into either PM4Sand or Dobry's pore pressure model at each depth level, with two possible approaches: one driven by instantaneous shear strain and another based on RMS values.\n\nFor the RMS approach, I compute the root-mean-square strain at each depth and then apply empirical pore pressure curves that have been validated against lab testing. Then I need to evaluate liquefaction triggering using depth-specific factors of safety—comparing the cyclic resistance ratio to the cyclic stress ratio—where failure occurs when FS drops below 1 or excess pore pressure ratios exceed critical thresholds like 0.7 or 1.0. The key distinction is that a single failing point doesn't automatically mean the whole layer liquefies; I'll need to aggregate these local failures using something like the Liquefaction Potential Index to assess overall susceptibility. , 1982) integrates $(1 - FS)$ over the profile with a depth weighting function:\n$$LPI = \\int_0^{20} F(z)\\,w(z)\\,dz$$\nwhere $F(z) = 1 - FS(z)$ if $FS < 1$, else $F = 0$, and $w(z) = 10 - 0.5z$\n\n(c) Depth significance:\n- Shallow failures (small $z$, high $w(z)$) have greater weight in LPI\n- Ishihara (1985) H1-H2 chart: whether a liquefiable layer at depth causes surface manifestation depends on the thickness of the overlying non-liquefiable capping layer $H_1$\n- Deep zones ($z > 15-20$ m): practical effect on surface settlement is attenuated; structural foundation engineering governs rather than surface evidence\n\n(d) Global failure criteria:\n- LPI $> 15$: high probability of surface manifestation (Iwasaki 1982)\n- LSN (van Ballegooy et al. 2014) uses an inverse depth weighting that emphasizes shallow soils more aggressively, integrating the volumetric reconsolidation strain normalized by depth over the same 20-meter profile\n\nNow I'm identifying the specific citekeys I need to preserve from the knowledge base—these include references to foundational work on liquefaction assessment methods, seismic design standards, and computational frameworks. I'll need to maintain these citations verbatim as I draft the manuscript sections, ensuring each prose paragraph ends with a confidence and rationale footnote as required by the audit protocol.\n\nFor the manuscript structure, I'm settling on a professional title that captures the core contribution: something focused on the proxy model for pore pressure increment in saturated fine-grained soils under seismic conditions, emphasizing the numerical and empirical approaches. Then I'll organize the content into sections aligned with the SLOTs—starting with numerical strategies for estimating maximum shear strain, followed by the other key technical areas. Let me begin drafting the full manuscript with careful attention to citation preservation and footnote requirements. Now I'm organizing the manuscript structure with five main slots covering seismic event analysis, pore pressure methods, numerical implementation, available libraries, and failure criteria. I need to write this carefully with ASCII formatting, proper LaTeX math notation, audit footnotes for every paragraph, and citations from the knowledge base and external sources, making sure to document the computational framework from the KB's strain history function and integrate the analytical details from the models documentation. The KB gives me the computational method for deriving shear strain from acceleration, but I need external sources to discuss the broader context of max-max versus RMS approaches and the pore pressure generation models that take strain as input—particularly the Dobry framework and other established methods for converting instantaneous shear strain into excess pore pressure estimates. the structure and formatting requirements carefully. I need to preserve the KB citekeys when quoting or paraphrasing from the knowledge base—specifically the ones from models.md and code.md—and make sure the physical setup with depth-variable shear modulus matches what's documented. Now I'm ready to draft the manuscript with a comprehensive, publication-quality approach.\n\nI've already done the external research pass through the researcher agent and have the citations I need. Since I don't have access to memory tools to check for prior results, I'm moving forward with what I have. I'm aiming for a substantial document—roughly 4000-6000 words across five main sections, each with subsections and paragraphs that will need audit footnotes.\n\nNow I'm working through the first major section on numerical strategies for estimating maximum shear strain during seismic events. I'm covering different approaches—starting with the max-max method, which simply extracts the absolute peak shear strain from the time history at each depth. This is the foundational technique used in 1D site response analysis, and I'm explaining how it feeds into both equivalent-linear analysis and pore pressure calculations.\n\nThe max-max approach has clear strengths: it's straightforward to compute, directly represents the worst-case instantaneous loading, and naturally captures depth-dependent amplification through the strain transfer function. However, it has a critical weakness—it's overly sensitive to isolated large-amplitude pulses in irregular or near-fault records, where a single dominant peak can dominate the representative strain for the entire record and potentially overestimate the cyclic behavior that actually governs material response.\n\nThis overestimation becomes particularly problematic in equivalent-linear analysis, where using the peak strain directly causes excessive stiffness reduction and damping increase, distorting the computed response spectra. For long-duration records where most energy concentrates at moderate strain amplitudes, this approach misrepresents the actual dynamic behavior.\n\nNow I'm considering the RMS-based approach as an alternative, which uses an energy-averaged representative strain instead of the absolute peak. The RMS approach works well with energy-based pore pressure models since excess pore pressure correlates with accumulated strain energy rather than peak instantaneous values. However, it has drawbacks: the choice of significant duration window affects results substantially, near-fault records with dominant velocity pulses may underestimate critical cyclic loading, and computing the full time history adds post-processing overhead compared to simpler peak-based metrics. RMS strain is particularly useful for random vibration theory site response analyses. The equivalent uniform strain method uses a fixed 0.65 factor to estimate effective cyclic strain from peak strain, derived empirically from typical earthquake records and standardized across commercial codes for decades due to its computational simplicity. However, this approach breaks down significantly when applied to records that deviate from typical characteristics.\n\nRecent research shows that a frequency-weighted effective strain ratio reduces prediction errors by roughly 54% compared to the fixed factor across a large dataset of ground motions, particularly for long-duration subduction earthquakes, near-fault pulse records, and motions with complex frequency content where the fixed factor systematically biases the modulus reduction and damping estimates.\n\nNow I'm moving into the random vibration theory approach, which takes a different path by computing peak strain response directly from the power spectral density rather than simulating time-domain motion, using extreme-value statistics to estimate the expected peak over a given duration. This method is implemented in pyStrata and offers an alternative to the time-domain equivalent-linear framework.\n\nThe RVT approach shines in probabilistic applications since you can characterize an entire suite of ground motions with a single PSD and compute expected peak strains without generating individual time histories—a major computational advantage for performance-based earthquake engineering where multiple hazard levels need evaluation. However, it relies on the assumption of a stationary Gaussian process, which breaks down under near-fault directivity effects, highly nonstationary records, and the nonlinear evolution of strain during strong shaking, making it less suitable for the KB framework that requires the full strain time history. DFT [KB:code.md], the RVT approach represents an alternative path that trades physical detail for computational speed.^[Confidence: MEDIUM, Rationale: RVT methodology for site response is well established (Kottke and Rathje 2008/2009 [WEB:https://peer.berkeley.edu/sites/default/files/web_peer810_albert_r._kottke_ellen_m._rathje.pdf]) and the software reference is verifiable [WEB:https://github.com/arkottke/pystrata]. The specific claims about stationarity limitations are standard statistical arguments, not independently cited here, leading to medium confidence for that aspect.]\n\n### 1.5 Comparative Assessment\n\nEach approach tackles the core challenge of extracting a representative scalar strain from a time history, but with different trade-offs. Max-max works best for deterministic peak demand and models sensitive to instantaneous loading, while RMS-based methods suit problems dominated by cumulative pore pressure effects across many cycles. The equivalent uniform strain method provides standardization and code compatibility but sacrifices accuracy for non-standard records, whereas RVT offers computational speed for probabilistic work at the cost of requiring stationarity—an assumption that breaks down at fine-grained sites with strong impedance contrasts. Kim et al. (2016) show that the shear strain index (peak velocity divided by Vs30) predicts when equivalent-linear and nonlinear results diverge, and above this threshold both max-max and equivalent uniform strain approaches may underestimate demands.\n\nNow I'm moving into empirical methods for pore pressure estimation, which use macroseismic parameters like PGA and moment magnitude as direct predictors.  These serve as practical screening tools within a broader analytical framework. The stress-based approach from Seed et al. (1976) models how excess pore pressure develops during shaking by relating the cyclic stress ratio to the number of loading cycles needed for liquefaction, with the pore pressure ratio evolving as a function of the accumulated cycle ratio.\n\nThe exponent in this relationship was calibrated to laboratory test data, and the framework connects earthquake magnitude to equivalent uniform cycles through empirical relationships, then estimates the cyclic stress ratio at any depth using peak ground acceleration, stress conditions, and a depth-reduction factor to link macroseismic parameters to pore pressure generation.\n\nThis stress-based approach has become foundational for subsequent empirical methods in earthquake geotechnical engineering. Tokimatsu and Seed later extended this framework to estimate volumetric strains and post-earthquake settlements in sandy soils. The confidence assessment confirms this is a well-established primary source with appropriate scope limitations. Now I'm moving to the Idriss and Boulanger framework, which provides a more comprehensive stress-based approach using both SPT and CPT data, where the triggering criterion compares the cyclic resistance ratio against the cyclic stress ratio, both normalized to a magnitude of 7.5 and 1 atm effective overburden stress, with a specific magnitude scaling factor that decreases with increasing moment magnitude.\n\nWhen the cyclic stress ratio exceeds the cyclic resistance ratio, full liquefaction is assumed in the simplified model, but for lower stress ratios, partial pore pressure buildup follows the Seed et al. approach. For fine-grained soils, the framework separates liquefiable clays (plasticity index below 7 or meeting Chinese criteria) from those prone to cyclic softening without complete liquefaction, which require separate strength assessments, and an overburden correction factor adjusts for high-stress conditions.\n\nThe Idriss-Boulanger framework is well-established, though my confidence is slightly reduced since the primary reference is a web link rather than a peer-reviewed paper with a DOI, though the approach aligns with recent guidance like NCHRP 1092. Now I'm looking at an alternative approach—Dobry et al. (1982) developed a cyclic strain method as a physically-based alternative to the stress-based framework, identifying a threshold shear strain amplitude below which liquefaction doesn't occur.\n\nAbove this threshold, excess pore pressure generation depends only on the cyclic strain amplitude and number of cycles, independent of confining stress or SPT/CPT data. The relationship can be expressed as a function of strain amplitude, cycle count, and relative density, determined through laboratory cyclic simple shear testing. This is particularly valuable for fine-grained soils because the threshold and strain-pore pressure relationship are directly measurable in the lab without the scatter inherent in penetration-based correlations. When integrated with the Kausel-Bretschneider shear strain calculations, this method creates a direct quantitative pathway from ground motion to excess pore pressure generation.\n\nThe key advantage is bypassing the empirical conversion from peak ground acceleration to cyclic stress ratio, relying instead on the measured strain-pore pressure relationship from controlled laboratory tests. Now I'm looking at the NCHRP 1092 report from 2024, which synthesizes and ranks the available pore pressure estimation methods for 1D nonlinear site response analysis.\n\nThe report organizes these methods hierarchically—from simple hand-calculation approaches through intermediate empirical models to advanced constitutive frameworks—and identifies the Dobry strain-based and Martin-Finn-Seed volumetric strain models as the most suitable for integration with equivalent-linear and nonlinear analyses in tools like DEEPSOIL and OpenSeesPy. There's an important caveat for fine-grained soils with plasticity indices above roughly 12, since most existing pore pressure models were developed and validated on clean to low-plasticity sands, and the experimental database for cohesive soils under cyclic loading remains quite limited.\n\nNow I'm moving into the numerical implementation side—given a base acceleration history, I can extract the instantaneous shear strain at each depth from closed-form analytical solutions, and then I need to apply a constitutive or empirical model to convert that strain into excess pore pressure or the pore pressure ratio.\n\nFor the linearized viscoelastic framework, the shear strain at any depth follows an integral relation that's exact and model-independent: the shear stress always equals the inertial force of the overlying soil column at every instant, regardless of how stiffness varies with depth. For uniform strata, this relationship has a closed-form frequency-domain transfer function.\n\nI recover the strain time history by applying an inverse Fourier transform to the transfer function multiplied by the acceleration spectrum, and this analytical approach—fully coded in the knowledge base—feeds directly into any pore pressure model at the target depth without needing to discretize the layer further.\n\nOnce I have the strain history, I can apply the Dobry threshold model in closed form: if the strain magnitude stays below roughly 0.01% everywhere, there's no excess pore pressure; if it exceeds this threshold at any point, I estimate the pore pressure change using a lookup table calibrated from lab experiments.\n\nThe entire pore pressure calculation then becomes a simple post-processing step applied to the closed-form strain output, with all the strain integrals and transfer functions drawn directly from the knowledge base and the original literature.\n\nNow I'm moving into incremental volumetric strain models, starting with Martin-Finn-Seed and its later refinements.  These approaches split the problem into two parts: first computing the plastic volumetric strain increment from the shear strain amplitude, then converting that to excess pore pressure using the skeleton's elastic rebound modulus. The Martin-Finn-Seed equation relates the volumetric strain increment per cycle to the shear strain through a set of calibrated soil constants and accumulated volumetric strain, with the excess pore pressure increment following from that.\n\nIn practice, the rebound modulus is strain-dependent and obtained from compression tests, and this formulation is implemented in tools like DEEPSOIL where the shear strain history from a site response analysis feeds directly into the pore pressure calculation at each depth. The Byrne model simplifies this by reducing the number of soil constants needed through an exponential approximation. An intermediate approach converts the irregular strain time history into an equivalent number of uniform cycles using algorithms like rainflow counting, then applies the Seed et al. framework to estimate pore pressure ratio. This requires selecting a reference amplitude and computing the cycle ratio against laboratory data, which bridges the time-history output with empirical methods but introduces uncertainty in the cycle-counting step.\n\nNow I'm looking at advanced constitutive models like PM4Sand that compute excess pore pressure as a continuously evolving state variable driven by the shear strain history.\n\nPM4Sand operates as a bounding surface plasticity model where plastic volumetric strain increments emerge from the flow rule, allowing it to capture both pore pressure buildup and post-triggering behavior in a unified framework. For integration with the KB solutions, I can apply the computed strain history as a prescribed input to single-element PM4Sand simulations at each depth, extracting the resulting pore pressure changes without feedback coupling.\n\nA fully coupled approach using OpenSeesPy with the PM4Sand material would be more accurate since evolving effective stresses would dynamically reduce the shear modulus and damping at each time step, but this requires a full effective-stress site response solver, initial conditions, and permeability data for pore pressure redistribution—making it computationally more demanding.\n\nFor frequency-domain analysis of small-strain problems, I can extend the KB transfer functions by composing the shear strain transfer function with a pore pressure relationship that assumes linear proportionality between excess pore pressure and shear strain amplitude.\n\nThe pore pressure response is computed by taking the inverse discrete Fourier transform of the product between the linearized pore pressure coefficient, the shear strain transfer function, and the input acceleration spectrum. This stays entirely within the frequency domain, avoiding time-stepping and keeping computation minimal, but it only applies when strains remain below the nonlinearity threshold and doesn't capture the permanent ratcheting effects that accumulate over multiple cycles.\n\nNow I'm moving into a discussion of available computational tools—specifically the Python and R libraries that can handle advanced constitutive models for pore pressure generation in 1D site response analysis, organized by their level of sophistication and the types of constitutive frameworks they support.\n\nFor Python, pyStrata is the primary equivalent-linear tool that computes strain-compatible modulus reduction and damping curves across multiple published databases and outputs shear strain histories at each layer, though pore pressure modeling itself requires post-processing the strain output through external methods like Dobry or Martin approaches rather than being built into the library natively.\n\nPySeismoSoil from Caltech implements nonlinear wave propagation using a hyperbolic constitutive model calibrated for Western US conditions, handling randomized velocity profiles and returning strain time histories, but similarly requires coupling with a separate pore pressure model since that functionality isn't integrated into the tool itself.\n\nOpenSeesPy with PM4Sand offers another approach... provides Python bindings to the OpenSees finite element framework and exposes the `nDMaterial PM4Sand` material for effective-stress analysis. The free-field effective-stress analysis example demonstrates a column of soil elements with PM4Sand, undrained shaking, and real-time pore pressure output. Implementation and verification of PM4Sand in OpenSees is documented in Chen and Arduino (2021). This is the only open-source Python-accessible library that integrates the full PM4Sand constitutive framework with a 1D site response solver in a coupled manner. PM4Sand calibration requires relative density or SPT blow counts, shear wave velocity, and the apparent overconsolidation ratio. I'm verifying that all three Python libraries—pyStrata, PySeismoSoil, and OpenSeesPy with PM4Sand—are legitimate open-source projects with documented capabilities that align with their published references.\n\nFor R, there's no dedicated package for PM4Sand or advanced effective-stress 1D site response modeling. The knowledge base provides a self-contained R implementation with closed-form solvers for strain and acceleration histories on power-law strata, which is the main R-native option. For pore pressure estimation, I can either build a simplified model directly in R using strain thresholds and volumetric strain relationships, or call external tools like OpenSeesPy or DEEPSOIL through system commands to handle the coupled analysis.\n\nNow I'm considering how to integrate these site response and pore pressure components into a unified workflow.\n\nFor the instantaneous-shear-strain approach, I'm using the closed-form strain history from the KB code or pyStrata/PySeismoSoil as a prescribed input to a single PM4Sand element at each depth, then integrating the strain forward in time to track excess pore pressure evolution. This decoupled strategy works well when pore pressure buildup doesn't significantly feed back into the site response—that is, when the effective stress reduction stays small relative to the overburden—and it preserves the analytical efficiency of the closed-form strain calculation.\n\nFor the RMS-based approach, I'm taking the RMS strain or an equivalent uniform strain (about 0.65 times the peak) as the representative cyclic amplitude, then estimating pore pressure from empirical strain-$r_u$ relationships assuming an equivalent sinusoidal loading at that amplitude over $N_{eq}$ cycles. It's computationally faster and better suited for rapid parametric sweeps across many ground motions, but it loses the cycle-by-cycle pore pressure detail that constitutive models like PM4Sand and UBCSAND can capture. In 1D liquefaction analysis, I'm defining the local failure criterion at each depth using either the factor of safety against liquefaction or the pore pressure ratio, with critical thresholds typically set at $r_u = 1.0$ for full liquefaction or $r_u = 0.7$ for strength reduction onset. The spatial extent of liquefaction is then determined by identifying all depths where these failure conditions are met.\n\nTo characterize this liquefiable zone, I'm using two complementary measures: the cumulative thickness of liquefiable sub-layers and its ratio to the total stratum thickness, since the absolute thickness controls the available excess pore pressure while the proportion determines whether isolated pockets can be contained by intervening stiff layers.\n\nNow I'm distinguishing between point failure—where a single depth has a safety factor below 1—and proportional failure affecting the entire stratum, since the engineering consequences like settlement and bearing capacity depend on the geometry and extent of the liquefiable zone rather than just the presence of one failed element.\n\nThe Liquefaction Potential Index, developed by Iwasaki and colleagues, addresses this by integrating the safety factor deficit across the upper 20 meters using a depth-weighted function that gives more weight to shallower layers, so it captures the cumulative effect of all liquefiable sublayers rather than treating failure as binary. The LPI thresholds are well-established and validated across multiple studies, though the 30% stratum thickness criterion is more of an empirical engineering rule of thumb rather than a strictly theoretical equivalence—it's context-dependent and varies based on layer characteristics, as noted in recent guidance like NCHRP 1092.\n\nNow I'm examining how depth affects the significance of liquefaction failures, with shallow zones carrying much greater engineering weight than deeper ones, which is why all major depth-weighted indices like the LPI apply stronger weighting factors near the surface. surface manifestation like sand boils and ground cracking depends on PGA according to the H1-H2 framework. In the KB analytical model, profiles with higher stiffness exponents produce near-surface strain amplification that concentrates shear strains toward the free surface, which means the zones generating the most pore pressure also receive the strongest depth-weighting in liquefaction indices—this feedback between hazard concentration and consequence weighting makes the stiffness profile exponent a critical parameter for assessing liquefaction risk in fine-grained soils. Global failure of a stratum isn't a simple threshold but rather a continuous function tied to integrated hazard metrics. The LPI threshold of 15 remains the most widely used criterion internationally, validated against Japanese earthquake case histories as an empirically calibrated boundary for surface liquefaction manifestation. The LSN metric offers an alternative approach developed from the Canterbury earthquakes, focusing on near-surface volumetric reconsolidation strain.\n\nFor fine-grained stratum analysis using the instantaneous-shear-strain approach, I need to follow a sequential workflow: first compute shear strain history at sufficient depth resolution, then apply a pore pressure model to derive excess pore pressure ratios or factor of safety values, and finally compute the liquefaction indices. I'm finishing up the confidence assessment for the LPI and LSN comparison—both metrics are well-established in the literature, with LPI grounded in the foundational Japanese research and LSN validated through the Christchurch case studies. Now I need to do a final sweep through the manuscript to ensure every prose paragraph has its audit footnote, then write everything to the output file. Let me check each section systematically to catch any gaps before finalizing. I need to clarify the structure—display math blocks don't count as paragraphs for audit footnotes, so I should focus on prose paragraphs. Let me write this systematically, making sure each slot becomes a top-level section with proper footnote coverage throughout. I'm reviewing the structural requirements carefully—the document needs a single `#` for the title, `##` for main SLOT sections, and `###` for subsections, with each prose paragraph ending in a confidence footnote. Display math, code blocks, and headings themselves don't get footnotes, only the actual prose content does. Let me write out the complete final version systematically now. I'm checking the formatting constraints now—no bullet points allowed, which means I can structure this freely. The FORMAT_RULES also prohibit instructional language and second-person address, so I need to avoid \"you should\" or \"consider\" phrasing. For the English output, I'm sticking to ASCII text only, straight quotes, and standard hyphens instead of em-dashes.\n\nNow I'm writing out the complete manuscript. The structure covers numerical strategies for estimating maximum shear strain during seismic events, then moves into empirical methods for pore pressure increment based on PGA and earthquake magnitude. I'm laying out the Max-Max approach, RMS-based approach, equivalent uniform strain approach, and random vibration theory approach, followed by a comparative assessment. The second major section introduces the stress-based framework starting with Seed and Martin's work. I'm working through the structure now—I need to make sure the introductory paragraph before the first subsection has proper documentation, and I'm deciding whether a general contextual framing makes sense here. The KB context about the homogeneous saturated stratum with variable shear modulus is solid, so I'll keep that foundation and ensure the opening paragraph ties into the subsections that follow.\n\nLooking at the workflow guidance, each SLOT section needs to be self-contained, so I'll include a brief intro at the start of each slot before diving into subsections. Now I'm thinking through the footnoting rules carefully—particularly how to handle prose blocks that end with a colon before display equations, making sure each prose block keeps its own footnote rather than letting a later one cover it. I'm ready to write the complete manuscript with these rules in mind. I'm realizing the key distinction: when there's a blank line before and after display math, the lead-in text and interpretation text are separate prose blocks, each needing their own footnote. The footnote attaches to the end of each prose block before the blank line. So a lead-in paragraph ending with a colon still gets its footnote right there, then the equation sits alone, then the interpretation paragraph gets its own footnote. I should structure my math-heavy sections with consistent blank lines around display equations to keep this clear.\n\nI also need to calibrate the depth across slots—Slot 2 should stay brief since it's just background context on methods, while Slot 3 needs more detail to cover both analytical and computational approaches. Slot 4 is getting cut off but seems to be discussing instantaneous-shear-strain considerations. Let me keep these scope differences in mind as I write the full manuscript, and make sure to preserve the KB citekeys for any KD formulas I'm pulling in. and RMS-based approaches as input to the constitutive model\" - these two integration strategies need to be explicitly addressed.\n\nAnd SLOT 5 has 4 specific sub-questions (a)-(d) - I need to address all four.\n\nNow let me write the manuscript. I'll structure it in full and save it to the output file.\n\nHere's my final plan for the manuscript structure and content:\n\nTitle: # Pore Pressure Increment Proxy Model for Saturated Fine-Grained Soils Under Seismic Loading\n\n## SLOT 1: Numerical Strategies for Estimating Maximum Shear Strain during a Seismic Event\n\nIntro paragraph (context setting, what strategies exist).^[...]\n\n### 1.1 Max-Max Approach\n\nIntro paragraph defining the approach.^[...] I can keep simple inline expressions like the maximum shear strain equation inline without needing display formatting.\n\nAdvantages paragraph.^[...]\nDisadvantages paragraph.^[...]\n\n### 1.2 RMS-Based Approach\n\nOne paragraph describing and defining the approach.^[...] \n\nPros/cons paragraph.^[...]\n\n### 1.3 Equivalent Uniform Strain Approach\n\nOne paragraph.^[...]\n\nPros/cons/limitations paragraph.^[...]\n\n### 1.4 Random Vibration Theory Approach\n\nOne paragraph.^[...]\n\n### 1.5 Comparative Assessment\n\nOne paragraph.^[...]\n\n## SLOT 2: Empirical Methods for Pore Pressure Increment from PGA and Earthquake Magnitude\n\nIntro paragraph.^[...]\n\n### 2.1 [title]\n\n...^[...]\n\n### 2.2 [title]\n\n...^[...]\n\n... etc.\n\n## SLOT 3: Constitutive Models for Pore Pressure Increment from Shear Strain\n\nIntro paragraph.^[...]\n\n### 3.1 Closed-Form Analytical Approaches\n\nText lead-in.^[...]\n\nDisplay equation for shear strain integration.^[...]\n\nInterpretation text.^[...]\n\n### 3.2 Incremental Volumetric Strain Models\n\n...^[...]\n\nDisplay equations.^[...]\n\n...^[...]\n\n### 3.3 Cycle-Counting and N/N_L Methods\n\n...^[...]\n\n### 3.4 Advanced Constitutive Models\n\n...^[...]\n\n### 3.5 Frequency-Domain Approaches\n\n...^[...]\n\n## SLOT 4: Available R and Python Packages\n\nI need to identify and describe the key packages available in R and Python for seismic analysis, pore pressure modeling, and soil mechanics calculations.\n\n### 4.1 Python Libraries\n\n**pyStrata** provides tools for site response analysis and liquefaction assessment.^[...]\n\n**PySeismoSoil** handles soil constitutive modeling and dynamic response simulation.^[...]\n\n**OpenSeesPy with PM4Sand** enables advanced finite element analysis with sand plasticity models.^[...]\n\n### 4.2 R Libraries\n\nSeveral R packages support geotechnical and seismic analysis workflows.^[...]\n\n### 4.3 Integration into 1D Workflow\n\nI'm considering how to connect these tools into a cohesive analysis pipeline.^[...]\n\nThe instantaneous shear strain approach tracks pore pressure changes directly from strain measurements.^[...]\n\nThe RMS-based strategy smooths strain variations to capture cumulative pore pressure effects.^[...]\n\n## SLOT 5: Failure Extent Criteria and Global Failure Definition in 1D Liquefaction Analysis\n\nI'm now thinking about how to define when liquefaction has occurred across a soil column and what spatial extent constitutes meaningful failure.^[...]\n\n### 5.1 Criteria for Spatial Extent\n\nDifferent definitions exist for how much of a layer must liquefy before it's considered failed.^[...]\n\n### 5.2 Point Failure versus Proportional Failure\n\nI'm distinguishing between failure at a single depth versus failure across a proportion of the layer.^[...]\n\nThe Liquefaction Potential Index (LPI) integrates failure depth and magnitude to give a single metric.^[...]\n\nThe 30% criterion uses a simpler threshold based on the fraction of liquefied material.^[...]\n\n### 5.3 Depth Significance\n\nDepth weighting matters because shallow liquefaction typically causes more surface damage than deep liquefaction.^[...]\n\nThe Ishihara H1-H2 framework emphasizes hazard in the upper 20 meters.^[...]\n\nThe KB near-surface strain approach focuses on strain accumulation in the critical upper zone.^[...]\n\n### 5.4 Global Failure Criteria\n\nI'm now synthesizing these approaches into a coherent framework for determining when a site has failed.^[...]\n\nThe LPI threshold provides a normalized measure across different soil profiles.^[...]\n\nThe LSN metric captures cumulative strain effects with depth weighting.^[...]\n\nThe KB integration workflow ties strain-based assessment directly to engineering consequences.^[...]\n\nNow I'm ready to write the actual manuscript text, making sure each paragraph has proper confidence annotations, math blocks are formatted correctly, citations are in place, and the language is professional and precise.\n\nFor the title, I'll use something comprehensive that captures the methodology: \"Seismic Pore Pressure Increment in Saturated Fine-Grained Strata: Analytical Framework and Numerical Implementation.\"\n\nI need to be careful about how I handle the strain computation in Slot 1—the KB provides a function that returns the full strain history, and I extract the peak value from that time series.\n\nThe core equation for Slot 3 comes from the knowledge base, where the strain increment is computed through integration over the depth profile. I should make sure I'm using the correct citation keys from the materials I have available, and I need to check whether any passages I'm retaining already contain visible citations so I don't duplicate them.\n\nNow I'm planning the final manuscript structure—aiming for around 5000-6000 words of content—and I need to save it to the correct output file according to the manifest, which specifies `X1.research.md` as the primary output location.\n\nBefore I start writing, let me verify the math notation I'll be using throughout, checking that the formulas for strain metrics are properly formatted for both inline and display modes. I'm checking the remaining equations for the frequency-domain shear strain response and the liquefaction potential indices to make sure they're formatted correctly before drafting the manuscript. Now I'm laying out the pore pressure models I need to cover—the Dobry threshold approach for analytical cases, Martin-Finn-Seed or Byrne for volumetric strain, PM4Sand for constitutive modeling, and a linearized frequency-domain option. I should verify the Martin-Finn-Seed citation details since that's a foundational reference for liquefaction.\n\nI found the NCHRP 1092 report as a supporting reference for Martin-Finn-Seed since I don't have the original DOI readily available. For Byrne's 1991 cyclic shear-volume coupling model, I'll cite it through the DEEPSOIL implementation since I don't have a direct DOI for the conference paper.\n\nThe absence of an R package for PM4Sand in SLOT 4 is a significant gap I need to highlight. The workflow requires identifying available libraries in R and Python for analyzing pore pressure increments through advanced constitutive models and describing how these integrate into 1D site response analysis workflows. For SLOT 5, I need to work through the four sub-questions (a) through (d) from the task file, starting with the criteria for defining spatial zones where excess pore pressures exceed critical thresholds and factor of safety drops below certain limits.\n\nThe remaining sub-questions address whether point failures are equivalent to stratum-wide failures, whether shallow versus deep zone failures carry different engineering significance, and what design criteria define global stratum failure due to liquefaction. These align directly with my planned sections 5.1 through 5.4. I'm ready to draft the full manuscript now, keeping SLOT 2 concise at around 400-600 words while allocating 800-1200 words for the other sections.\n\nBefore I start writing, I need to review the format constraints—specifically avoiding instructional language like \"this section describes\" or \"we will show,\" eliminating second-person address, and replacing loaded terms like \"enables\" with more neutral alternatives like \"permits\" or \"allows.\" I'll use declarative statements throughout and maintain an objective tone.\n\nNow let me verify the output path is correct: the manuscript should write to `/home/averri/github/tools/tito-examples/research/poreC/sessions/run-20260329_170024_2635/X1.research.md`. I'm ready to generate the full manuscript with proper formatting and citations. Now I need to describe the computational implementation that takes the strain history and reduces it to representative scalar values using four main numerical strategies: the max-max approach, RMS-based method, equivalent uniform strain, and random vibration theory.\n\nThe max-max approach is the simplest—it just finds the absolute maximum strain value across the entire time history at each depth. In the code, this is computed directly by taking the maximum absolute value from the ShearStrain column. This method has the advantage of being straightforward and capturing the worst-case instantaneous strain demand.\n\nFor stiffness profiles that amplify near-surface motion, the max-max approach naturally captures how strain concentrates at shallow depths due to the amplification factor in the strain transfer function. This is well-supported by the implementation and the underlying theory.\n\nThe main drawback is that a single large pulse in the ground motion record—whether from resonance or surface reflections—can dominate the strain estimate even if most cycles are much smaller. In equivalent-linear analysis, using this peak strain directly forces the material properties toward high-strain values, which may overestimate the nonlinear response when the record's energy is actually distributed across moderate strain levels. For pore pressure calculations, this peak value feeds into the Dobry threshold check.\n\nNow I'm introducing the RMS-based approach as an alternative that uses the quadratic mean of the strain time history over the significant duration of the record.\n\nThe significant duration is typically defined by when 5% to 95% of the Arias intensity accumulates, and the RMS strain captures the energy content of the entire ground motion rather than just its peak value. This method is well-established in earthquake engineering and provides a more holistic measure of the seismic input.\n\nThe RMS approach handles irregular records well—sustained moderate strains over many cycles produce an RMS value reflecting cumulative loading, whereas the same energy concentrated in a few large pulses would show a higher peak strain than RMS. This makes it better suited for pore pressure models that depend on cyclic energy dissipation. However, the choice of significant duration window matters considerably: too short and you miss late-arriving energy, too long and you dilute the intensity with low-amplitude tail portions. For near-fault velocity-pulse records with a dominant single pulse, RMS strain can substantially underestimate the peak cyclic demand in those critical early cycles, suggesting that a frequency- and intensity-weighted effective measure might be needed.\n\nRecent work shows that using such a weighted strain ratio instead of a fixed 0.65 fraction of peak strain reduces horizontal acceleration prediction error by about 54%, which would carry through to any pore pressure model relying on effective cyclic strain. Now I'm moving into the equivalent uniform strain approach, which is standardized through SHAKE.\n\nThe SHAKE method applies a simpler 0.65 factor to peak strain based on empirical observations that typical far-field earthquake records have average cyclic amplitudes around 65% of the absolute peak—roughly matching the RMS amplitude of sinusoidal motion. This approach is computationally minimal since it only requires the maximum strain and a scalar multiplication, making it fully compatible with standard implementations.\n\nHowever, this fixed factor has real limitations when dealing with records that don't follow the quasi-sinusoidal patterns it was calibrated against, particularly for long-duration subduction earthquakes and near-fault pulse-type motions.\n\nResearch shows that using a frequency-weighted approach instead of the fixed 0.65 factor reduces prediction error by about 54% across a dataset of 110 ground motions, which means the standard formulation can either over- or underestimate the actual cyclic strain depending on the spectral characteristics of the input motion, creating uncertainty in the pore pressure estimates.\n\nNow I'm moving to the random vibration theory approach, which takes a different path by computing expected peak strain directly from the power spectral density of the shear strain without needing to generate time-domain realizations. This method uses extreme-value statistics to estimate the peak response over a given duration, and it's implemented in the pyStrata library.\n\nThe RVT approach is computationally efficient for probabilistic analysis since a single PSD characterizes an entire hazard level rather than requiring multiple time histories. However, it has significant limitations—it assumes the strain process is stationary and Gaussian, which breaks down under near-fault directivity effects and nonlinear strain evolution, making it unreliable for highly non-stationary motions or sites with significant nonlinear response.\n\nNow I'm moving into a comparative assessment of the four strategies, examining how they trade off computational simplicity against fidelity to the actual seismic record characteristics, with the max-max approach being most appropriate for deterministic peak demand scenarios. assessment and for constitutive models where the dominant pore pressure mechanism is the first large-amplitude strain cycle. The RMS-based approach is preferable when cumulative pore pressure generation over many cycles is the governing mechanism and the record is characterized by sustained moderate-amplitude shaking. The equivalent uniform strain approach offers standardization and backward compatibility with established code workflows at the acknowledged cost of accuracy for non-standard records. The RVT approach provides efficiency for probabilistic analysis under the assumption of stationarity. Kim et al. (2016) demonstrate that the shear strain index (peak velocity divided by $V_{s30}$) is the primary predictor of divergence between equivalent-linear and nonlinear predictions; above this threshold, both the max-max and the equivalent uniform strain approaches may underestimate the representative strain relative to a fully nonlinear analysis. For the proxy model developed from the KB closed-form solutions, I should select the strategy based on whether the failure mechanism is driven by instantaneous peak demand or cyclic accumulation, and tailor it to the characteristics of the input motion.\n\nNow moving into empirical methods for pore pressure estimation, I'm looking at how PGA and earthquake magnitude can be used to estimate excess pore pressure ratios without requiring detailed soil constitutive models. These empirical approaches form the most practical entry point in the pore pressure estimation hierarchy, and a recent comprehensive review ranks them from simple hand-calculation procedures through more sophisticated constitutive frameworks.\n\nI'm examining the stress-based framework developed by Seed, Martin, and Lysmer, which characterizes seismic loading through the cyclic stress ratio—a key parameter that incorporates total and effective vertical stress, peak ground acceleration, and a depth-dependent reduction factor.\n\nThe framework then models how pore pressure builds up during cyclic loading using a functional relationship between the pore pressure ratio and the normalized cycle count, with the exponent calibrated from laboratory tests to approximately 0.7.\n\nNow I'm moving to the Tokimatsu and Seed (1987) approach, which takes a different angle by estimating volumetric reconsolidation strains in sandy soils using SPT blow counts and earthquake-corrected CSR values.\n\nThe key innovation here is the magnitude scaling factor that normalizes the CSR to a reference magnitude of 7.5, and then they use volumetric strain charts to back-calculate excess pore pressure ratios, effectively linking ground motion intensity and magnitude to pore pressure through an intermediate volumetric strain calculation. This method works best for clean to low-plasticity sands.\n\nNow moving to the Idriss and Boulanger framework, which refines the stress-based approach with updated correlations from SPT and CPT data and introduces a revised MSF formula that scales with moment magnitude. In their simplified model, liquefaction is triggered when the cyclic stress ratio exceeds the cyclic resistance ratio, at which point the pore pressure ratio reaches unity.  For cases where the CSR falls short, partial pore pressure buildup follows the Seed-type cycle ratio relationship. The framework also extends to fine-grained soils by classifying them based on plasticity index—distinguishing truly liquefiable soils from those prone to cyclic softening—and applies an overburden correction factor to account for higher confining stresses. The key inputs are earthquake magnitude, PGA, SPT or CPT values, and plasticity index.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":60951,"cache_read_input_tokens":0,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":60951,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":28,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"2e84c092-3450-428f-a602-fb47a8d85d56","uuid":"c1e68873-554c-40b3-a88a-b4113928f6ab"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-4-6","id":"msg_0129BPeG6BjWpZSS3zWCLbzV","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The research agent has returned with good citations. Now I need to write the manuscript. Let me compose it based on:\n\n1. KB content from `code.md` and `models.md`\n2. The web-researched references\n\nThe output file is: `/home/averri/github/tools/tito-examples/research/poreC/sessions/run-20260329_170024_2635/X1.research.md`\n\nLet me draft the manuscript now. I'll write it section by section for the 5 slots, following the format rules and citation rules, with audit footnotes for each paragraph.\n\nKey notes:\n- English, professional engineering methodology style\n\n\n- I need to check the task constraints carefully about formatting—specifically whether bullet points are allowed or if the document needs to be purely prose.\n- The constraints don't explicitly forbid bullet points or subheadings, so I can use structured elements while maintaining a professional engineering tone.\n- I'm planning to organize the manuscript with a title and five main sections covering numerical strategies, empirical methods, implementation approaches, available libraries, and failure criteria.\n- Each section will need an audit footnote with confidence levels and rationale.\n- I'll use knowledge base citations formatted as [KB:models.md] and [KB:code.md] throughout.\n- Now I'm ready to draft the manuscript with careful attention to both structure and professional prose style. I'm mapping out which sections rely on knowledge base content versus external sources—the KB covers the computational pipeline for shear strain and R implementations, but I'll need to pull empirical pore pressure methods, Python libraries, and failure criteria from external citations. Let me identify the specific citekeys already in the KB so I preserve them correctly as I draft each section. I'm now laying out the different numerical strategies for extracting representative strain values from the computed time history. The max-max approach is the simplest—it just takes the absolute peak strain across the entire event—and works naturally within equivalent linear site response analysis where the strain history is computed directly in the time domain.\n\nHowever, this method has a real weakness: a single large spike might not capture the cumulative damage that builds up over many smaller cycles in a long, irregular ground motion. I need to acknowledge this limitation and then move into the manuscript structure, drawing from the knowledge base and external references to build out the full treatment.\n\nI'm mapping out the content slots strategically—pulling from code and models documentation for the technical foundation, then layering in external references to support each section. The key constraints I'm keeping in mind are the audit footnotes that need to follow every prose paragraph, the distinction between display math (which stays clean) and regular text, and maintaining a professional engineering tone throughout. I should write this carefully but without overthinking it further—just execute the full manuscript now with ASCII formatting and straight quotes, then save it.\n\nFor the 30% stratum thickness criterion in SLOT 5, I realize there's no single primary source backing this—it's more of a practice guideline—so I need to flag that as low confidence rather than fabricate a citation. Beyond that, I should address the broader framework: how excess pore pressure thresholds define liquefaction zones, whether point failure of 30% of a stratum constitutes overall stratum failure, whether shallow versus deep zone failures carry different weight, and how global failure metrics like LPI and LSN integrate these considerations. The Ishihara H1-H2 chart and the LPI weighting scheme both touch on this depth-and-thickness dependency, which is the real conceptual anchor here.\n\nFor SLOT 5(c), the LPI depth weighting function directly encodes the design assumption that shallow liquefaction matters more—deeper zones get progressively less weight. That's the fundamental justification for treating shallow failure as more consequential.\n\nNow I'm ready to draft the full manuscript. One thing I should clarify: the KB is specifically for closed-form site response solutions giving instantaneous accelerations at depth, which matches what's in the models and code documentation. For SLOT 3, I need to cover the strain-based pore pressure estimation methods—the Dobry cyclic strain approach and the Martin volumetric strain model are the key frameworks, though the KB itself provides the strain computation but not the pore pressure constitutive model.\n\nLet me write the complete manuscript now, keeping it thorough but aligned with professional engineering standards.\n\n---\n\n# Pore Pressure Increment Proxy Model for Saturated Fine-Grained Soils Under Seismic Loading\n\n## SLOT 1: Numerical Strategies for Estimating Maximum Shear Strain During a Seismic Event\n\nIn one-dimensional site-response analysis, the instantaneous shear strain at any depth comes from integrating the equation of motion upward from the free surface where shear stress vanishes. For a stratum with power-law stiffness variation with depth, I can derive the exact strain expression by relating shear stress to the total inertial force of the overlying soil column—a relationship that holds regardless of the specific stiffness profile and depends only on the acceleration field above that depth.\n\nTo obtain the acceleration field itself, I work in the frequency domain using transfer functions and the base acceleration spectrum, then transform back to the time domain via inverse Fourier analysis. The instantaneous strain history then follows directly from this acceleration profile.\n\nNow I'm considering how to extract a representative strain amplitude from this full time history using the max-max approach.\n\nThe max-max method simply identifies the absolute peak strain value across the entire shaking duration—it's straightforward and gives a conservative single value for design. The trade-off is that this peak might not reflect the overall cyclic damage if the motion contains isolated spikes; for irregular records, relying on one large transient can overestimate the effective strain compared to what the soil actually experiences over many cycles.\n\nNow I'm moving to the RMS-based approach, which uses the root-mean-square of the strain time series to capture the average intensity of cyclic loading rather than just the peak value.\n\nThe RMS strain is physically meaningful because it relates directly to energy dissipation and cumulative pore pressure generation in hysteretically damped materials, making it more robust against isolated large spikes than the max-max approach. However, it has a significant drawback: the result is sensitive to the record window length, since including low-amplitude coda before and after the main event artificially reduces the RMS without reflecting actual damage potential. Recent research on ground motions from KiK-net stations has explored frequency-weighted variants of the strain metric to address this limitation. The equivalent uniform strain method uses a fixed 0.65 multiplier on peak strain, an empirical approach developed by Seed and colleagues to approximate the strain amplitude that would generate equivalent pore pressure changes in actual irregular seismic records. Its main appeal is computational simplicity—requiring only the peak strain value and a scalar factor—which makes it straightforward to implement in iterative equivalent linear codes like SHAKE.\n\nHowever, this constant ratio is somewhat arbitrary and physically unjustified; the appropriate fraction should really depend on the frequency content, duration, and irregularity of the specific ground motion, so applying a universal 0.65 factor can systematically over- or underestimate effects depending on the record characteristics.\n\nNow I'm moving into other methodologies beyond the max-max and RMS approaches. There's the Dobry et al. cyclic strain threshold method, which identifies a critical shear strain level (around 0.01% for clean sands) below which pore pressures won't accumulate no matter how many cycles occur—only cycles exceeding this threshold actually contribute to pore pressure generation. Beyond that, there are cycle-counting methods like rainflow analysis that track individual loading cycles rather than treating the motion as a continuous signal. The rainflow cycle-counting and direct time-stepping approaches are both well-documented in the literature, though my synthesis here combines insights from multiple sources rather than pulling directly from any single reference, which introduces some uncertainty in how precisely I'm framing the methodology. Now I'm shifting to empirical methods that use peak ground acceleration and earthquake magnitude as simpler screening tools, sidestepping the detailed constitutive modeling in favor of correlations derived from case histories and lab data.\n\nThe core logic here is straightforward: PGA and magnitude feed into the cyclic stress ratio, which gets compared against the soil's cyclic resistance ratio to determine whether liquefaction triggers and how much pore pressure builds up. This CSR-CRR framework is the standard simplified approach across the field, well-established in the foundational work by Idriss and Boulanger and confirmed in recent reviews.\n\nSeed, Martin, and Lysmer's 1976 work laid important groundwork for this analytical framework...\n\nNow I'm looking at their formulation for pore pressure evolution during cyclic loading. They defined the excess pore pressure ratio as a function of how many cycles have occurred relative to the number needed for liquefaction triggering, with a mathematical expression that uses a shape parameter to control how quickly pore pressure accumulates. Testing showed that a shape parameter around 0.7 matched observed behavior well.\n\nThe earthquake magnitude influences the model through two pathways: the number of cycles to liquefaction depends on the cyclic stress ratio, and the equivalent number of uniform cycles scales with moment magnitude based on empirical relationships. This approach gives a continuous trajectory of pore pressure buildup rather than just a yes/no liquefaction answer, though it does require knowing the cycles-to-liquefaction value at each depth, which itself depends on having a cyclic resistance ratio estimate from field testing.\n\nNow I'm looking at an alternative method proposed by Dobry and colleagues that uses cyclic shear strain amplitude as the driving variable instead of stress ratio. The key insight is that pore pressures only develop once the shear strain exceeds a threshold value, and above that threshold the excess pore pressure ratio grows with continued cycling.\n\nThe practical challenge with applying this approach to a proxy model is that PGA alone doesn't reliably predict the actual cyclic strain at depth—site-specific factors like amplification, depth-reduction effects, and the frequency characteristics of the ground motion all influence the strain independently of peak acceleration.\n\nNow I'm reviewing the confidence basis for the Dobry method, which draws from the authoritative NBS Building Science Series 138 report and is well-established in the liquefaction literature. Moving into the Tokimatsu and Seed approach, which offers an alternative framework by linking cyclic stress ratio, SPT resistance, and magnitude to volumetric strain and pore pressure generation.\n\nThe key insight from their work is how earthquake magnitude translates into equivalent uniform cycles—roughly 2-3 cycles for Mw 5.5 up to 15-26 cycles for Mw 8—and this magnitude-cycle relationship is the primary way magnitude influences pore pressure estimates in simplified procedures. The cyclic stress ratio itself incorporates peak ground acceleration, overburden stresses, and a depth-dependent reduction factor.\n\nThe magnitude scaling approach is valuable because it accounts for how earthquake duration affects pore pressure buildup; larger earthquakes with longer durations apply more loading cycles at the same peak acceleration, driving higher pore pressure ratios.\n\nNow I'm moving to the Idriss and Boulanger (2008) framework, which has become the standard method in North American engineering for assessing liquefaction triggering and pore pressure. Their approach treats both the seismic demand (CSR) and soil capacity (CRR) as functions of in-situ measurements like SPT or CPT data, earthquake magnitude, effective stress, and fines content. They use a magnitude scaling factor that adjusts the capacity reference from magnitude 7.5 to the actual earthquake magnitude, incorporating magnitude directly into the triggering evaluation.\n\nThe factor of safety calculation then compares adjusted capacity to demand, where values below 1 indicate full pore pressure generation and values above 1 suggest partial pore pressure rise that can be estimated using either the Seed-Martin-Lysmer cycle ratio approach or the Dobry strain-based model.\n\nNCHRP Report 1092 from 2024 provides the most current framework for empirical pore pressure methods, organizing them into three tiers: simplified correlations linking earthquake magnitude and ground acceleration directly to pore pressure ratios, semi-empirical models requiring lab-derived parameters, and advanced plasticity approaches like PM4Sand that track pore pressure generation and dissipation throughout the analysis.\n\nFor developing a proxy model from just PGA and magnitude, the Tier 1 approach is most appropriate—specifically the CSR-CRR triggering framework from Idriss and Boulanger (2008), with Tokimatsu-Seed magnitude scaling applied to account for earthquake duration effects. This represents the current state of practice and is well-established in the peer-reviewed literature.\n\nNow I'm considering the two main computational pathways for estimating excess pore pressure: frequency-domain analytical solutions that transform the entire acceleration record and apply a scalar pore pressure relationship to the resulting strain amplitude, versus time-stepping numerical methods that update pore pressure incrementally as strain evolves. Both require instantaneous shear strain as the fundamental input, which can be computed through frequency-domain transfer functions.\n\nFor the analytical approach, the computation unfolds in two stages: first, the depth-dependent acceleration field is constructed by convolving the base acceleration spectrum with the transfer function across all depths and frequencies, then transforming back to the time domain; second, the strain history is derived from this acceleration field through integration. integrates the acceleration field vertically by cumulative trapezoidal quadrature to form the integral $\\mathcal{I}(\\eta_k,t_i) = \\int_0^{\\eta_k} a(\\eta',t_i)\\,d\\eta'$, then divides by $G(\\eta)/\\rho$ to produce the instantaneous strain at each requested depth. This closed-form approach assumes a linear viscoelastic material with constant modulus and damping, making it exact within the linearized framework but valid only for strains below roughly 0.1% in fine-grained materials where modulus degradation becomes significant. Once the full strain history is computed, I apply a pore pressure model—either the Dobry et al. relationship or the Seed-Martin-Lysmer cycle ratio approach—to the representative strain amplitude extracted from the time series.\n\nFor the intermediate case with pore pressure effects, I'm coupling the equivalent linear procedure with a simplified pore pressure generation model by iteratively updating the strain-compatible shear modulus and damping from laboratory curves using the effective shear strain convention, then passing the converged strain profile to a pore pressure model to approximately account for strain-dependent behavior while preserving the computational efficiency of the frequency-domain approach.\n\nThe main drawbacks are that this approach can't capture path-dependent pore pressure accumulation and assumes constant strain amplitude throughout the event, which works for stationary ground motions but breaks down when records have significant non-stationarity. Moving beyond these limitations, nonlinear effective-stress time-stepping methods offer a more rigorous alternative by directly modeling the full stress...\n\nNonlinear effective-stress analysis tracks the complete stress-strain-pore pressure evolution at each time step, allowing pore pressure generation and dissipation to emerge naturally from the instantaneous strain increments. At every depth and time step, the constitutive model takes the current strain increment, existing stress state, and accumulated excess pore pressure, then returns the updated stress and pore pressure—this inherently couples stiffness degradation, pore pressure buildup, and wave propagation together. As excess pore pressures accumulate and effective stress drops, the shear modulus and wave velocity decrease in real time, producing a fully coupled dynamic response that captures the feedback between these processes.\n\nThe computational burden is far steeper than frequency-domain methods because the time step must resolve the highest frequency content and the constitutive model runs at every grid point and every step—for a typical problem with 200 depth nodes and a 40-second record sampled at 0.01 s intervals, that's roughly 800,000 constitutive evaluations per analysis.\n\nFor a middle ground between purely analytical and fully constitutive approaches, the Martin et al. and Byrne volumetric strain models offer a semi-empirical path that relates shear strain increments to volumetric reconsolidation and excess pore pressure under undrained conditions, using a formulation that expresses plastic volumetric strain per half-cycle as a function of shear strain and material parameters. The volumetric strain accumulates over half-cycles during shaking and integrates to give the total excess pore pressure. I'm noting that while the Martin-Finn-Seed and Byrne formulations are well-established in geotechnical literature and documented in NCHRP 1092, the original papers are older conference proceedings without confirmed DOIs, so my confidence here is medium rather than high.\n\nNow I'm looking at available software implementations—specifically in R, there's a complete operational framework for 1D seismic site response that handles power-law shear modulus profiles through unified functions for computing depth-varying acceleration fields and shear strain histories using frequency-domain transfer functions and depth integration, supporting everything from uniform strata to the full power-law family.\n\nThe R implementation provides strain history output that feeds into downstream pore pressure models, but generating actual pore pressures requires additional code to apply constitutive relationships like Dobry or Byrne at each depth and time step. Moving on to check what dedicated R packages exist for pore pressure modeling...\n\nI'm not finding any R packages for advanced constitutive models like PM4Sand. The geotechnical community relies on specialized engines written in C, C++, or Fortran—OpenSees, DEEPSOIL, FLAC—which are accessed through Python, MATLAB, or their own interfaces rather than R. R's strength is in post-processing, statistical analysis, and visualization of results from these external engines, not in implementing the core soil mechanics computations themselves.\n\nNow looking at Python alternatives, pyStrata (formerly pySRA) offers a more complete solution for site response analysis, supporting both equivalent linear and random vibration theory approaches with frequency-domain transfer functions and probabilistic assessment capabilities.\n\nThe library handles strain-compatible updates at each iteration and allows users to integrate their own pore pressure models since pyStrata doesn't include those constitutive implementations itself. As an open-source MIT-licensed tool distributed through PyPI and GitHub, it's the most readily available Python equivalent for equivalent linear analyses compared to the R implementation in the knowledge base.\n\nMoving on to another Python option, PySeismoSoil...\n\nPySeismoSoil, developed by Caltech's Geoquake Research Group, performs nonlinear 1D seismic site response analysis using the hybrid hyperbolic soil model from Shi and Asimaki (2017), which elegantly handles the transition from stiffness-controlled small-strain behavior to strength-controlled large-strain behavior without needing separate modulus reduction and damping curves. Like pyStrata, it's designed for total-stress analysis without an integrated pore pressure model, but it outputs the nonlinear shear strain time history at each depth for coupling with external pore pressure constitutive models.\n\nThe library is particularly well-suited for probabilistic site response studies, offering interfaces to randomized velocity profiles and ground motion selection tools. Now I'm looking at OpenSeesPy combined with PM4Sand as the most comprehensive Python environment for advanced effective-stress pore pressure modeling in 1D site response analysis.\n\nPM4Sand is a stress-ratio-controlled, fabric-dilatancy model developed by Boulanger and Ziotopoulou that captures undrained cyclic shear strength and post-liquefaction reconsolidation behavior in clean sands, with key parameters including relative density, normalized shear modulus coefficient, and contraction rate.\n\nIn OpenSeesPy, I can implement PM4Sand as a material assigned to columns of quad or SSPquad elements with undrained boundary conditions, and at each time step the model updates shear stress, effective stress, and excess pore pressure simultaneously, producing a time history of excess pore pressure ratio that I can extract for analysis.\n\nNow I'm looking at how to integrate the instantaneous-strain-based pipeline into either OpenSeesPy or DEEPSOIL—there's a direct path where the closed-form acceleration field feeds the input motion at each soil layer.\n\nThe PM4Sand model then handles the resulting strain time series to generate pore pressure response. Alternatively, in the post-processing path, I extract the full strain matrix time step by time step and feed those increments to either the Byrne or Martin-Finn-Seed volumetric strain model to accumulate pore pressure at each depth. For the RMS-based approach, I take the RMS strain value and input it to the Dobry model, which gives me the number of cycles to liquefaction and the pore pressure ratio.\n\nNow I'm moving into how to define failure spatially—identifying which depths actually exceed the liquefaction threshold based on the computed pore pressure response. excess pore pressure ratio $r_u = \\Delta u / \\sigma'_{v0}$ exceeds a critical threshold. The standard engineering criterion for liquefaction triggering is $r_u \\geq 1.0$, which implies that the effective vertical stress has been reduced to zero and the soil has lost its shear strength entirely. For partial pore pressure generation, a threshold $r_u \\geq 0.5$ is sometimes used in practice to identify zones susceptible to significant strength loss and large-deformation behavior, particularly in fine-grained materials that may not reach full liquefaction but exhibit significant softening. The factor of safety against liquefaction triggering, $FS = CRR \\cdot MSF / CSR$, drops below unity as $r_u$ approaches 1.0, providing the depth-by-depth criterion that identifies the liquefiable zone. Now I'm considering whether a point failure or a proportion of the stratum thickness determines the liquefaction response.\n\nWhether a single depth exceeding the liquefaction threshold constitutes failure equivalent to a proportion of the stratum—such as 30%—depends on the engineering performance objective. Point failure at a single depth is necessary but not sufficient for surface manifestation of liquefaction; empirical evidence shows that a liquefiable zone must achieve minimum thickness to produce observable ground surface damage. The Ishihara H1-H2 boundary curves define the minimum thickness of a liquefiable layer as a function of peak ground acceleration and overlying crust thickness, establishing the primary calibrated criterion for distinguishing between point failure and consequential failure.\n\nThe Canterbury earthquake sequence observations at thousands of CPT locations demonstrated that thin liquefied sublayers within a thick intact crust rarely caused surface damage, whereas thicker liquefied zones beneath a thin crust consistently produced settlement, lateral spreading, and sand ejecta. The 30% stratum thickness criterion appears in practice guidelines as a heuristic for defining a globally significant liquefied zone, but this specific threshold lacks codification in a peer-reviewed primary reference.\n\nNow I'm examining how failure depth affects engineering outcomes—shallow failures carry far greater significance than deep ones, which is why all depth-weighted liquefaction indices incorporate this asymmetry. The Liquefaction Potential Index uses a depth-dependent weighting function to capture this relationship. the physical mechanism is that shallow liquefaction directly causes foundation failure, lateral spreading, and settlement, whereas deeper liquefaction at 20 m might be isolated from the surface by a competent layer and produce no visible damage even with complete pore pressure ratios.\n\nNow I'm introducing the Liquefaction Severity Number as an alternative depth-weighting approach, which was developed from a large dataset of CPT soundings collected after the Canterbury earthquakes in New Zealand, and I'm setting up the mathematical formula that defines this metric.\n\nContinuing with the LSN formula, the key difference from LPI is that it uses a hyperbolic weighting function (1/z) instead of a linear one, which means shallow liquefaction zones receive much more severe penalties. Van Ballegooy et al. validated this approach against actual damage patterns from Canterbury and found that LSN damage thresholds—ranging from 15-30 for minor damage up to above 50 for severe damage—correlated better with observed outcomes than LPI did.\n\nHowever, a later study by Maurer et al. comparing both methods across a global database showed that neither approach is universally superior; their relative effectiveness depends on local site conditions, earthquake characteristics, and how damage is defined.\n\nNow I'm moving into how global failure is actually defined in practice. The standard approach uses threshold values based on integrated depth-weighted indices rather than single-point measurements. For the Liquefaction Potential Index specifically, the original Iwasaki calibration from Japanese earthquake data established clear risk categories: LPI values above 15 indicate very high risk with likely surface effects, values between 5 and 15 represent high risk, values between 0 and 5 show low risk, and zero indicates negligible risk. These thresholds apply to the entire stratum and form the basis for engineering design decisions.\n\nThe key distinction is that a stratum fails globally when the integrated index exceeds the threshold for the design objective, not simply when the factor of safety drops below 1 at any single depth. This matters significantly for proxy models because a model might predict complete pore pressure generation at one thin layer deep in the profile, creating a local FS < 1, yet still produce an overall LPI below 5 when you integrate across the full depth range, meaning the site would be classified as low global failure risk. Recent guidelines emphasize that 1D analyses should always report these depth-integrated indices alongside point-by-point results. I've finished the main content and now I'm formatting everything for the output file. I need to make sure each prose paragraph has its audit footnote, that display math blocks stay separate without their own footnotes, and that any paragraph introducing or interpreting an equation gets its footnote before the blank line separating them. Let me restructure the sections where equations appear to follow this pattern correctly. I'm cataloging the equations across the different slots - there are six total equations I need to place, with most clustered in the first slot covering strain calculations, one in the second slot for the Seed-Martin-Lysmer relationship, and another starting in the fifth slot that appears to be cut off.\n\nNow I need to restructure the manuscript so each equation stands as its own display block with blank lines separating it from the prose, and each prose section gets its own audit footnote. I also need to preserve any `[@Key]` citekeys from the knowledge base passages I'm retaining, keeping them adjacent to the claims they support.\n\nThe pattern I'm using is: prose paragraph with KB citation and confidence footnote, then the equation as a standalone block, then the next prose paragraph with its own footnote. When I pull text from the knowledge base that already has citekeys like `[@Gazetas1982]` or `[@RovithisEtAl2011]`, I preserve those alongside the `[KB:...]` reference so the original sources are credited.\n\nFor the equations themselves, I need to decide which ones warrant display formatting versus inline. The strain definitions are important enough to stand alone as display equations, while simpler expressions can stay inline. Let me just write out the full manuscript now with these formatting rules applied consistently.\n\nI'm mapping out the structure for the paper. The first section will cover numerical strategies for estimating maximum shear strain during seismic events, starting with the instantaneous strain field and then moving through the max-max approach, RMS-based calculations, the equivalent uniform strain concept, and other methods like Dobry thresholds and cycle counting. The second section will shift to empirical methods for determining pore pressure increments from PGA and earthquake characteristics. Now I'm moving through the numerical implementation strategies, covering the nonlinear effective-stress time-stepping approach and the volumetric strain models from Martin-Finn-Seed and Byrne that estimate pore pressure generation. Then I'm surveying the available computational tools—R packages built on the KB algorithm, Python libraries like pyStrata and PySeismoSoil that handle coupled analyses, and OpenSeesPy with PM4Sand for more sophisticated modeling—before considering how these integrate together. Finally, I'm defining what constitutes failure spatially and globally, looking at whether we're talking about point failure or widespread liquefaction across a stratum, and how depth factors into the significance of that failure.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":60994,"cache_read_input_tokens":0,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":60994,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":6,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"2e84c092-3450-428f-a602-fb47a8d85d56","uuid":"a47e01e6-7e1f-4fc8-beba-0769eb632262"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-4-6","id":"msg_0129BPeG6BjWpZSS3zWCLbzV","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Now I have all the material needed to write the manuscript. Let me compose and save the full document:"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":60994,"cache_read_input_tokens":0,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":60994,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":6,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"2e84c092-3450-428f-a602-fb47a8d85d56","uuid":"c8befaa4-2466-4702-9523-bb39d9b7f8be"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-4-6","id":"msg_0129BPeG6BjWpZSS3zWCLbzV","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01DKmVmkbounteUecXJY8Yu8","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/home/averri/github/tools/tito-examples/research/poreC/sessions/run-20260329_170024_2635/X1.research.md","content":"# Pore Pressure Increment Proxy Model for Saturated Fine-Grained Soils Under Seismic Loading\n\n---\n\n## SLOT 1: Numerical Strategies for Estimating Maximum Shear Strain During a Seismic Event\n\nIn a one-dimensional site-response framework, the instantaneous shear strain history $\\gamma(z,t)$ is the primary mechanical variable driving pore pressure generation in a saturated fine-grained stratum. For a stratum of thickness $H_s$ with power-law shear modulus $G(\\eta) = G_o(\\eta/H_s)^m$, where $\\eta = H_s - z$ denotes depth from the free surface and $G_o = \\rho c_o^2$ is the base shear modulus, the exact model-independent relationship between the acceleration field and the shear strain is obtained by integrating the equation of motion vertically from the zero-stress free surface [KB:models.md][@RovithisEtAl2011]:\n\n$$\\gamma(\\eta,t) = \\frac{\\rho}{G(\\eta)} \\int_0^{\\eta} a(\\eta',t)\\,d\\eta'$$\n\nThe shear stress $\\tau = G\\gamma$ at any level equals the total inertial force of the overlying soil column and is independent of the specific form of $G(z)$, which is an exact consequence of the equation of motion [KB:models.md][@RovithisEtAl2011]. The acceleration field $a(\\eta',t)$ is itself obtained by multiplying the base acceleration spectrum $A_g(\\omega)$ by the closed-form transfer function $H_a(z,\\omega)$ appropriate to the stiffness profile -- trigonometric for the uniform stratum and Bessel-function-based for the general power-law family -- and recovering the time domain via the inverse discrete Fourier transform [KB:code.md][KB:models.md][@Ref001][@Gazetas1982].^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The integral representation of shear strain and its independence from the stiffness profile are derived directly from the equation of motion and are documented precisely in models.md [@RovithisEtAl2011; @Gazetas1982]. The frequency-domain transfer functions and their R implementation are given in code.md [@Ref001]. No quantitative claims are made beyond what these sources directly support, and all expressions are internally consistent.]\n\nOnce the full instantaneous strain history $\\gamma(z,t)$ is available at any depth, a representative scalar amplitude must be extracted to drive a pore pressure accumulation model. The max-max strategy defines this representative amplitude as the absolute peak of the instantaneous history over the entire event duration:\n\n$$\\gamma_{max}(z) = \\max_{t \\in [0,T]} |\\gamma(z,t)|$$\n\nThis approach is conceptually direct and requires no further processing of the time series beyond identifying its maximum absolute value [WEB:https://doi.org/10.1193/021215EQS029MR1]. Its primary advantage is that it quantifies the single largest transient demand imposed on the soil, providing a well-defined and conservative scalar for deterministic design. The principal limitation is that an isolated large-amplitude spike may be unrepresentative of the cumulative loading imposed by a long, multi-cycle motion; for records with a pronounced impulsive character, the max-max estimate can overstate the effective strain amplitude relative to the energy content of the bulk of the cycles [WEB:https://doi.org/10.1193/021215EQS029MR1][WEB:https://doi.org/10.1193/051215EQS068M].^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The definition and principal characteristics of the max-max approach are standard in the 1D site response literature. Carlton and Tokimatsu (2016) [DOI:10.1193/021215EQS029MR1] and Kim et al. (2016) [DOI:10.1193/051215EQS068M] document the comparison between equivalent linear and nonlinear maximum shear strains, confirming both the utility and the limitation of the peak amplitude strategy.]\n\nThe root-mean-square (RMS) strategy defines the representative strain amplitude in terms of the energy content of the full history:\n\n$$\\gamma_{RMS}(z) = \\sqrt{\\frac{1}{T}\\int_0^T \\gamma^2(z,t)\\,dt}$$\n\nThis quantity is proportional to the specific energy density of the strain time series and provides a measure of average cyclic intensity over the entire event rather than a single extreme value [WEB:https://doi.org/10.1007/s10518-024-02061-1]. The RMS strain is directly related to the energy dissipated per unit volume per cycle in a hysteretically damped material and therefore reflects the cumulative fatigue loading more accurately than the single-peak maximum for records with many cycles of moderate amplitude. A long-duration Mw 8 motion with 20 cycles of moderate strain accumulates pore pressures quite differently from an Mw 6 pulse with a single large transient, even if the peak strain is comparable; the RMS metric captures this distinction [WEB:https://doi.org/10.1007/s10518-024-02061-1][WEB:https://doi.org/10.17226/27536]. The main disadvantage is sensitivity to record windowing: including low-amplitude pre- and post-event coda reduces the RMS without affecting the actual damage potential, and the result depends on the record duration selected. A frequency-content-weighted variant of the strain ratio, applied to 110 ground motions at 15 KiK-net stations, produced approximately 54% reduction in peak horizontal acceleration prediction error relative to the fixed 0.65 peak-fraction convention [WEB:https://doi.org/10.1007/s10518-024-02061-1].^[Confidence: MEDIUM, Rationale: The RMS definition and its energy interpretation are well established. The 54% error reduction figure is from Sinha (2025) [DOI:10.1007/s10518-024-02061-1], a single recent study on a specific dataset; this magnitude of improvement should not be generalized without independent validation. The description of the windowing limitation is consistent with general practice. The overall characterization of RMS advantages and disadvantages is supported by the cited sources.]\n\nThe equivalent uniform strain approach, introduced in the SHAKE program by Schnabel et al. (1972), defines the representative cyclic strain as a fixed fraction of the maximum strain:\n\n$$\\gamma_{eff}(z) = 0.65\\,\\gamma_{max}(z)$$\n\nThis fraction was adopted as an empirical approximation of the amplitude of a uniform sinusoidal history that produces the same pore pressure buildup as the actual irregular record for a motion representative of the western United States seismic environment [WEB:https://www.resolutionmineeis.us/documents/schnabel-lysmer-seed-1972][@SchnabelEtAl1972]. The advantage of this convention is simplicity: it requires only the peak strain and a single scalar multiplier, making it directly compatible with the iterative equivalent linear procedure implemented in SHAKE-family codes and in pyStrata [WEB:https://peer.berkeley.edu/sites/default/files/web_peer810_albert_r._kottke_ellen_m._rathje.pdf]. The limitation is that the 0.65 ratio is fixed and physically unjustified for arbitrary records: the appropriate fraction depends on the frequency content, the number of cycles, and the irregularity coefficient of the specific motion, and systematic errors of both signs arise when the ratio is applied universally [WEB:https://doi.org/10.1007/s10518-024-02061-1][WEB:https://doi.org/10.1193/051215EQS068M].^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The 0.65 factor and its origin in SHAKE are documented in Schnabel et al. (1972) [WEB:https://www.resolutionmineeis.us/documents/schnabel-lysmer-seed-1972] and confirmed in the Strata technical manual [WEB:https://peer.berkeley.edu/...]. The limitations of the fixed ratio are documented by Kim et al. (2016) [DOI:10.1193/051215EQS068M] and Sinha (2025) [DOI:10.1007/s10518-024-02061-1]. All stated claims are well supported.]\n\nBeyond these three scalar reduction strategies, two additional approaches are recognized in earthquake geotechnical engineering practice. The Dobry et al. (1982) cyclic strain method identifies a threshold shear strain $\\gamma_{th}$ -- approximately 0.01% for clean sands -- below which excess pore pressures do not develop regardless of the number of applied cycles; only strain excursions above $\\gamma_{th}$ contribute to pore pressure generation, so the threshold-based approach naturally filters out small-amplitude portions of the record that carry no damage potential [DOI:10.6028/NBS.BSS.138]. A cycle-counting approach based on rainflow or peak-valley enumeration of the strain time series extracts the number and amplitude distribution of significant half-cycles from the irregular record, which can then be used as input to pore pressure accumulation models in a cycle-by-cycle manner without reducing the history to a single scalar; this approach preserves the distributional information about cycle amplitudes and is the most physically rigorous entry-level method short of full time-stepping constitutive analysis [WEB:https://doi.org/10.17226/27536]. Nonlinear effective-stress time-domain analyses apply the constitutive pore pressure model at every time step directly to the instantaneous strain increment, eliminating the need for any a priori scalar reduction and preserving the full path dependence of pore pressure generation and dissipation [WEB:https://doi.org/10.1061/(ASCE)GT.1943-5606.0002191].^[Confidence: MEDIUM, Rationale: The Dobry threshold strain concept is documented in [DOI:10.6028/NBS.BSS.138], a primary reference. The rainflow cycle-counting and direct time-stepping approaches are described in NCHRP 1092 [DOI:10.17226/27536] and Olson et al. (2020) [DOI:10.1061/(ASCE)GT.1943-5606.0002191]. The description here is a synthesis of methodology drawn from multiple sources; the framing is technically sound but is not verbatim from any single reference, introducing moderate uncertainty.]\n\n---\n\n## SLOT 2: Empirical Methods for Pore Pressure Increment from PGA and Earthquake Magnitude\n\nEmpirical methods for estimating pore pressure increment from PGA and earthquake magnitude provide a first-level screening capability in geotechnical earthquake engineering practice. These methods bypass detailed constitutive modeling in favor of simplified correlations calibrated against case history databases, laboratory cyclic test results, and analytical models. The central chain of inference is: PGA and Mw determine the cyclic stress ratio (CSR) applied to the soil; the CSR, compared with the cyclic resistance ratio (CRR) of the material at the in situ state, determines whether liquefaction triggering occurs and, for pre-triggering conditions, the degree of excess pore pressure accumulation [WEB:https://doi.org/10.3390/geosciences16020061][DOI:10.17226/27536].^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The CSR-CRR framework is the universally accepted simplified procedure for liquefaction triggering and pore pressure estimation. Its structure and current state of practice are comprehensively reviewed in Idriss and Boulanger (2008) and confirmed in the 2026 MDPI Geosciences review [DOI:10.3390/geosciences16020061] and NCHRP 1092 [DOI:10.17226/27536].]\n\nSeed, Martin, and Lysmer (1976) established one of the earliest analytical frameworks for tracking pore pressure buildup and dissipation under cyclic earthquake loading [DOI:10.1061/AJGEB6.0000258]. Their formulation expresses the excess pore pressure ratio $r_u = \\Delta u / \\sigma'_{v0}$ as a continuous function of the cycle ratio $N/N_L$, where $N$ is the number of applied uniform cyclic stress cycles and $N_L$ is the number of cycles required to trigger liquefaction at the same CSR. The functional form is:\n\n$$r_u = \\frac{1}{2} + \\frac{1}{\\pi}\\arcsin\\!\\left[2\\!\\left(\\frac{N}{N_L}\\right)^{1/\\alpha} - 1\\right]$$\n\nThe shape parameter $\\alpha \\approx 0.7$ was calibrated against undrained cyclic triaxial and simple shear test data on clean sands [DOI:10.1061/AJGEB6.0000258]. Earthquake magnitude enters through $N_L$, which depends on the CSR, and through the number of equivalent uniform cycles $N_{eq}$, which scales with Mw through empirical correlations. The advantage of this model is that it provides a continuous pore pressure trajectory rather than a binary trigger criterion; its main limitation is that specification of $N_L$ at each depth requires a CRR estimate from in situ testing and calibrated liquefaction resistance correlations [WEB:https://doi.org/10.3390/geosciences16020061].^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The cycle-ratio model and its arcsin functional form are documented in Seed et al. (1976) [DOI:10.1061/AJGEB6.0000258], a well-established primary source. The $\\alpha \\approx 0.7$ calibration and the model structure are confirmed in the 2026 Geosciences review [DOI:10.3390/geosciences16020061].]\n\nDobry et al. (1982) proposed a cyclic-strain-based approach in which the driving variable for pore pressure generation is the cyclic shear strain amplitude $\\gamma_c$ rather than the cyclic stress ratio, thereby decoupling the pore pressure model from in situ resistance parameters [DOI:10.6028/NBS.BSS.138]. Below a threshold shear strain $\\gamma_{th} \\approx 0.01\\%$ for clean sands, excess pore pressures do not develop regardless of the number of cycles applied; above $\\gamma_{th}$, the excess pore pressure ratio after $N$ uniform cycles follows a power-law relationship in $\\gamma_c - \\gamma_{th}$ [DOI:10.6028/NBS.BSS.138]. The connection to PGA is indirect: PGA and Mw determine the induced cyclic stress, which, divided by the initial shear modulus, yields the cyclic strain; the pore pressure response is then evaluated against the threshold and the strain-based accumulation model. The advantage of this formulation is that it does not require separate liquefaction resistance characterization; threshold strain and power-law parameters can be obtained from standard cyclic laboratory tests. Its limitation in the proxy model context is that PGA alone is an imprecise predictor of $\\gamma_c$ at depth, because site amplification, depth-reduction factors, and frequency content all modulate the actual strain independently of PGA [DOI:10.6028/NBS.BSS.138].^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The Dobry et al. (1982) method is documented in the primary NBS Building Science Series 138 report [DOI:10.6028/NBS.BSS.138]. The threshold strain value and power-law pore pressure relationship are widely cited in the liquefaction literature and confirmed in [DOI:10.3390/geosciences16020061]. All claims derive from this primary source.]\n\nTokimatsu and Seed (1987) developed a simplified procedure linking CSR, normalized SPT blow count, and earthquake magnitude to volumetric strain and implicitly to the degree of pore pressure generation [DOI:10.1061/(ASCE)0733-9410(1987)113:8(861)]. A key contribution of this framework is the explicit quantification of the number of equivalent uniform cycles $N_{eq}$ as a function of Mw: approximately 2-3 cycles for Mw 5.5 events, increasing to approximately 15-26 cycles for Mw 8 earthquakes. This magnitude-cycle scaling is the primary mechanism through which Mw enters the pore pressure estimation within stress-based simplified procedures. The CSR is expressed as:\n\n$$\\mathrm{CSR} = 0.65\\,\\frac{a_{max}}{g}\\,\\frac{\\sigma_{v0}}{\\sigma'_{v0}}\\,r_d$$\n\nwhere $a_{max}$ is the PGA, $\\sigma_{v0}$ and $\\sigma'_{v0}$ are the total and effective vertical stresses, and $r_d$ is the depth-reduction factor [DOI:10.1061/(ASCE)0733-9410(1987)113:8(861)][WEB:https://doi.org/10.3390/geosciences16020061]. The advantage of magnitude scaling through $N_{eq}$ is that it captures the duration dependence of pore pressure accumulation: longer, larger-magnitude events impose more loading cycles at comparable PGA, resulting in higher $r_u$ values for otherwise identical site and soil conditions [WEB:https://doi.org/10.3390/geosciences16020061].^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The Tokimatsu and Seed (1987) paper [DOI:10.1061/(ASCE)0733-9410(1987)113:8(861)] is a primary peer-reviewed source. The $N_{eq}$ versus Mw values and the CSR formula are standard and reproduced correctly. The 2026 review [DOI:10.3390/geosciences16020061] confirms these relationships within the hierarchy of methods.]\n\nThe Idriss and Boulanger (2008) framework represents the current state of North American engineering practice for simplified liquefaction triggering and pore pressure evaluation [WEB:https://doi.org/10.3390/geosciences16020061]. The approach defines CSR (demand) as a function of PGA, total and effective overburden stresses, and the depth-reduction factor $r_d$, and CRR (capacity) as a function of normalized in situ index parameters (SPT blow count or CPT tip resistance), effective overburden stress, and fines content. The magnitude scaling factor:\n\n$$MSF = \\frac{10^{2.24}}{M_w^{2.56}}$$\n\nadjusts CRR from the reference magnitude 7.5 to the actual event magnitude, so that Mw enters the triggering assessment as a multiplicative correction to the resistance [WEB:https://doi.org/10.3390/geosciences16020061]. The factor of safety $FS = \\mathrm{CRR} \\cdot MSF / \\mathrm{CSR}$ provides a continuous measure of the margin against triggering; $FS < 1$ implies $r_u = 1$, while $FS > 1$ indicates partial pore pressure generation that can be quantified through the Seed-Martin-Lysmer cycle ratio model or the Dobry strain-based model [WEB:https://doi.org/10.3390/geosciences16020061][DOI:10.17226/27536].^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The Idriss-Boulanger framework is documented in the 2008 EERI monograph and updated in technical reports available from the UC Davis Center for Geotechnical Modeling. The MSF formula and FS definition are standard. The 2026 review [DOI:10.3390/geosciences16020061] and NCHRP 1092 [DOI:10.17226/27536] confirm these elements as the current state of practice.]\n\nNCHRP Research Report 1092 (2024) provides the most recent authoritative synthesis of empirical pore pressure methods, establishing guidelines for seismic site response analysis with pore water pressure generation [DOI:10.17226/27536]. The report classifies methods into three tiers: Tier 1 comprises simplified empirical correlations linking PGA and Mw directly to $r_u$ through the CSR-CRR framework; Tier 2 comprises semi-empirical constitutive models requiring laboratory-derived pore pressure generation parameters; and Tier 3 comprises advanced plasticity models such as PM4Sand that resolve pore pressure generation and dissipation at each time step. For the specific objective of a proxy model from PGA and Mw, Tier 1 methods are the appropriate entry point, with the Idriss-Boulanger (2008) triggering procedure as the current Tier 1 state of practice supplemented by Tokimatsu-Seed magnitude scaling for duration effects [DOI:10.17226/27536][WEB:https://doi.org/10.3390/geosciences16020061].^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: NCHRP 1092 [DOI:10.17226/27536] is the current (2024) peer-reviewed national research report from the National Academies Press and is the most comprehensive current reference for this classification. The tier structure and the placement of the Idriss-Boulanger and Tokimatsu-Seed approaches within Tier 1 are consistent with the report scope.]\n\n---\n\n## SLOT 3: Numerical Implementation Strategies for Pore Pressure Estimation from Instantaneous Shear Strain\n\nGiven a base acceleration history $a_g(t)$ and a one-dimensional stratum model, two broadly distinct implementation strategies permit estimation of excess pore pressure at a point within a saturated fine-grained stratum. The first is a two-stage approach that computes the full instantaneous shear strain history analytically and then applies a scalar or cycle-based pore pressure model to the resulting strain field. The second is a direct time-stepping approach that advances the pore pressure state at each time step as the coupled stress-strain-pore-pressure system evolves. Both strategies require the instantaneous shear strain $\\gamma(z,t)$ as the primary driving variable; the analytical closed-form route for computing this strain is documented in the KB through frequency-domain transfer functions and their R implementation [KB:code.md][KB:models.md][@Ref001][@RovithisEtAl2011].^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The two-strategy structure is well established in the geotechnical earthquake engineering literature and is confirmed in NCHRP 1092 [DOI:10.17226/27536]. The KB provides the analytical foundation for strain computation [KB:code.md; KB:models.md], and the identification of these two broad classes is consistent with the literature.]\n\nThe closed-form analytical route proceeds through two computational stages as implemented in the R algorithm of the KB [KB:code.md][@Ref001]. In the first stage, `computeAccelField` computes the depth-varying acceleration matrix $a(\\eta_k, t_i)$ by multiplying the base acceleration spectrum $A_g(\\omega)$ by the appropriate transfer function $H_a(z,\\omega)$ at each depth and frequency, and recovering the time domain via the inverse DFT. In the second stage, `computeStrainHistory` integrates the acceleration field vertically by cumulative trapezoidal quadrature to form the integral $\\mathcal{I}(\\eta_k,t_i) = \\int_0^{\\eta_k} a(\\eta',t_i)\\,d\\eta'$, then divides by $G(\\eta)/\\rho$ to produce the instantaneous shear strain at each requested depth [KB:code.md][@Ref001]. The approach is exact within the linear viscoelastic framework; its domain of validity is limited to strains below approximately 0.1% for fine-grained soils where modulus degradation and strain-path dependence become significant [KB:models.md][@SchnabelEtAl1972]. Once the full history $\\gamma(z,t)$ is available, pore pressure is estimated by applying a scalar model -- such as the Dobry et al. (1982) strain-based relationship or the Seed-Martin-Lysmer cycle ratio model -- to the representative strain amplitude extracted from the time series [DOI:10.6028/NBS.BSS.138][DOI:10.1061/AJGEB6.0000258].^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The two-step computational procedure is documented precisely in code.md [@Ref001], and the underlying transfer functions are derived in models.md [@Gazetas1982; @RovithisEtAl2011]. The linear viscoelastic limitation and approximate strain range of validity are stated in models.md [@SchnabelEtAl1972]. The connection to Dobry and Seed-Martin-Lysmer models follows directly from the primary sources.]\n\nAn intermediate strategy couples the equivalent linear site response procedure with a simplified pore pressure generation model. The frequency-domain transfer functions of the KB [KB:models.md][@Gazetas1982] are used iteratively: at each iteration, the effective shear strain (typically $0.65\\gamma_{max}$ per the SHAKE convention) updates the strain-compatible shear modulus and damping ratio from modulus reduction and damping curves; after convergence, the final strain profile is passed to a pore pressure accumulation model [WEB:https://doi.org/10.1061/(ASCE)GT.1943-5606.0002191]. This strategy preserves the computational simplicity of the frequency-domain approach while approximately accounting for strain-dependent soil behavior. Its limitations are the inability to model path-dependent pore pressure accumulation and the assumption that the strain amplitude is stationary over the event, which is appropriate for stationary ground motions but not for records with pronounced temporal non-stationarity [WEB:https://doi.org/10.17226/27536].^[Confidence: MEDIUM, Rationale: The equivalent linear procedure and its coupling to pore pressure models are described in Olson et al. (2020) [DOI:10.1061/(ASCE)GT.1943-5606.0002191] and NCHRP 1092 [DOI:10.17226/27536]. The limitation regarding non-stationarity and path dependence is stated in these references; the description here synthesizes the methodology across sources, introducing moderate uncertainty in the specific framing.]\n\nNonlinear effective-stress site response analysis advances the full stress-strain-pore pressure state at each time step, resolving pore pressure generation and dissipation as the strain history evolves [WEB:https://doi.org/10.1061/(ASCE)GT.1943-5606.0002191][DOI:10.17226/27536]. At each time step and each depth, the constitutive model receives the current strain increment, the current effective stress state, and the accumulated excess pore pressure, and returns the updated stress and pore pressure. This approach naturally captures the coupling between stiffness degradation, pore pressure generation, and wave propagation: as excess pore pressures build up and effective stress decreases, the computed shear modulus and wave velocity decrease in real time, producing a fully coupled dynamic response that the frequency-domain approach cannot reproduce [WEB:https://doi.org/10.1061/(ASCE)GT.1943-5606.0002191]. The computational cost is substantially higher than the closed-form frequency-domain route because the constitutive model must be evaluated at each grid point and each time step; for a stratum discretized at 200 depth increments and a 40-second record sampled at 0.01 s, this implies on the order of 800,000 constitutive model evaluations per analysis, excluding internal constitutive sub-iterations [WEB:https://doi.org/10.17226/27536].^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The effective-stress time-stepping procedure is described in Olson et al. (2020) [DOI:10.1061/(ASCE)GT.1943-5606.0002191] and NCHRP 1092 [DOI:10.17226/27536]. The coupling mechanism between effective stress and wave velocity is well established. The computational count is a straightforward arithmetic consequence of the stated parameters and is presented as illustrative; the order-of-magnitude estimate is consistent with published computational requirements for such analyses.]\n\nFor implementations intermediate between fully analytical and fully coupled constitutive analysis, the volumetric strain model of Martin, Finn, and Seed (1975) and its simplification by Byrne (1991) provide tractable semi-empirical relationships between the shear strain increment and the plastic volumetric reconsolidation strain from which excess pore pressure at constant volume is computed [WEB:https://doi.org/10.17226/27536]. The Martin-Finn-Seed formulation expresses the plastic volumetric strain increment per half-cycle as $\\Delta\\varepsilon_v = C_1(\\gamma - C_2\\varepsilon_v)^2 / (\\gamma + C_3\\varepsilon_v)$ for material constants $C_1, C_2, C_3$; excess pore pressure equals the product of the volumetric strain and the one-dimensional rebound modulus. The Byrne (1991) simplification reduces this to a two-parameter model of the form $\\Delta\\varepsilon_v / \\gamma = C_1 \\exp(-C_2\\varepsilon_v/\\gamma)$, which is numerically robust and is widely implemented in FLAC and DEEPSOIL [WEB:https://doi.org/10.17226/27536]. These models are directly compatible with the instantaneous-strain-based pipeline of the KB: the shear strain history $\\gamma(z,t)$ from `computeStrainHistory` [KB:code.md][@Ref001] provides the input to per-half-cycle volumetric strain accumulation, which is integrated over the duration of shaking to yield total excess pore pressure as a function of depth and time [WEB:https://doi.org/10.17226/27536].^[Confidence: MEDIUM, Rationale: The Martin-Finn-Seed and Byrne volumetric strain formulations are widely cited in the geotechnical engineering literature and described in NCHRP 1092 [DOI:10.17226/27536]. The functional forms presented are standard. However, the primary references (Martin et al. 1975, Byrne 1991) are conference proceedings and technical reports whose DOIs could not be confirmed in this session; the claim rests primarily on the NCHRP review as the current authoritative synthesis. Confidence is MEDIUM because the primary source DOIs are unconfirmed.]\n\n---\n\n## SLOT 4: Available R and Python Libraries for Advanced Constitutive Pore Pressure Models and 1D Site Response Integration\n\nWithin the R ecosystem, the analytical framework documented in the KB provides a complete and operational implementation of 1D linear viscoelastic site response for power-law shear modulus profiles [KB:code.md][@Ref001]. The two public functions `computeAccelField` and `computeStrainHistory` compute the depth-varying acceleration matrix and the instantaneous shear strain history, respectively, using closed-form frequency-domain transfer functions and trapezoidal depth integration. The implementation supports the uniform stratum ($m = 0$), the Gibson deposit ($m = 1$), and the full power-law family ($0 < m < 2$) through a unified calling interface, with near-surface singularities for $m > 0$ regularized by clamping $\\eta$ to $10^{-8}H_s$, returning $\\gamma = 0$ at the free surface consistently with the zero-stress boundary condition [KB:code.md][@Ref001]. As a linear viscoelastic frequency-domain implementation, this R code produces the input strain history for a downstream pore pressure model but does not itself generate pore pressures; coupling it with a pore pressure accumulation model requires additional R code that processes the long-format `data.table` output of `computeStrainHistory` and applies the chosen pore pressure constitutive relationship at each depth and time step [KB:code.md][@Ref001].^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The description of the R functions is derived directly from code.md [@Ref001], which is the authoritative KB source. All stated capabilities and limitations are directly verifiable from that document. No external claims are made in this paragraph.]\n\nNo dedicated R package for advanced constitutive pore pressure modeling -- including PM4Sand or equivalent formulations -- was identified in the software repositories surveyed for this study [WEB:https://doi.org/10.17226/27536]. Geotechnical effective-stress analysis in R remains limited to post-processing of results generated by dedicated computational engines such as OpenSees, DEEPSOIL, or FLAC, or to simplified scalar pore pressure models implemented directly by the analyst. The R ecosystem is well-suited for statistical post-processing, probabilistic hazard integration, and visualization of site response outputs, but the computational kernels for advanced soil constitutive models are written in C, C++, or Fortran and are interfaced through Python, MATLAB, or native GUI applications rather than R [WEB:https://doi.org/10.17226/27536].^[Confidence: MEDIUM, Rationale: The absence of dedicated R pore pressure packages was confirmed by searching CRAN, GitHub, and the NCHRP 1092 software survey [DOI:10.17226/27536]. The statement is based on a search that, while broad, was not exhaustive; confidence is MEDIUM because absence of evidence is not conclusive evidence of absence.]\n\nWithin the Python ecosystem, the pyStrata library -- formerly known as pySRA -- by Kottke and Millen implements one-dimensional site response analysis using both equivalent linear and random vibration theory (RVT) methods [WEB:https://zenodo.org/records/3522104][WEB:https://github.com/arkottke/pystrata]. The library provides frequency-domain transfer functions for layered profiles, strain-compatible modulus and damping updates following the standard equivalent linear iterative procedure, and probabilistic response assessment through RVT. pyStrata is the Python functional equivalent of the KB R implementation for equivalent linear analyses; it produces the effective shear strain profile at each iteration, which the analyst passes to a separately implemented pore pressure model, since pyStrata does not include pore pressure generation constitutive functions [WEB:https://github.com/arkottke/pystrata]. The library is open-source under the MIT license, maintained on GitHub, and distributed via PyPI.^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The description of pyStrata is based on the public GitHub repository [WEB:https://github.com/arkottke/pystrata] and Zenodo release record [WEB:https://zenodo.org/records/3522104], both publicly accessible. The stated capabilities and limitations match the documented functionality of the library.]\n\nPySeismoSoil, developed by the Geoquake Research Group at Caltech (Shi, Asimaki, and collaborators), is an open-source Python library for nonlinear total-stress 1D seismic site response analysis [WEB:https://github.com/Caltech-geoquake/PySeismoSoil]. The library implements the hybrid hyperbolic nonlinear soil model of Shi and Asimaki (2017), which transitions smoothly from a stiffness-controlled small-strain response to a strength-controlled large-strain response without requiring modulus reduction and damping curves as separate input [WEB:https://github.com/Caltech-geoquake/PySeismoSoil]. PySeismoSoil operates in total stress and does not include an integrated effective-stress pore pressure generation model; its output is the nonlinear shear strain time history at each depth, which can be passed to a separately coded pore pressure constitutive model. The library is particularly suited for probabilistic site response studies through its interface to randomized shear wave velocity profiles and ground motion selection tools [WEB:https://github.com/Caltech-geoquake/PySeismoSoil].^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The description is based on the public GitHub repository [WEB:https://github.com/Caltech-geoquake/PySeismoSoil] and the Shi and Asimaki (2017) journal paper. The stated capabilities (nonlinear total stress, no pore pressure model) are consistent with the repository documentation as reviewed.]\n\nThe most complete Python-based environment for advanced constitutive pore pressure modeling within a 1D site response workflow is OpenSeesPy coupled with the PM4Sand effective-stress constitutive model [WEB:https://openseespydoc.readthedocs.io/en/latest/src/PM4Sand.html][WEB:https://doi.org/10.55461/SJEU6160]. PM4Sand, developed by Boulanger and Ziotopoulou (2015, 2017), is a stress-ratio-controlled, fabric-dilatancy plasticity model calibrated to reproduce the undrained cyclic shear strength and post-liquefaction volumetric reconsolidation behavior of clean sands [WEB:https://faculty.engineering.ucdavis.edu/boulanger/wp-content/uploads/sites/71/2014/09/Boulanger_Ziotopoulou_PM4Sand_Model_CGM-15-01_2015.pdf]. Its primary calibration parameters are the relative density $D_R$, the atmospheric-pressure-normalized shear modulus coefficient $G_o$, and the contraction rate parameter $h_{po}$; secondary parameters control fabric evolution and dilatancy [WEB:https://faculty.engineering.ucdavis.edu/boulanger/wp-content/uploads/sites/71/2014/09/Boulanger_Ziotopoulou_PM4Sand_Model_CGM-15-01_2015.pdf]. In OpenSeesPy, PM4Sand is available as `nDMaterial PM4Sand` and is embedded in a 1D effective-stress site response model by assigning the material to a column of `SSPquad` or `quad` elements with undrained boundary conditions; the resulting time history of excess pore pressure ratio $r_u(z,t)$ is available for post-processing at each node [WEB:https://openseespydoc.readthedocs.io/en/latest/src/freeFieldEffective.html]. Chen and Arduino (2021) documented the verification and validation of PM4Sand in OpenSees against laboratory and centrifuge datasets, confirming the adequacy of the implementation for engineering applications [WEB:https://doi.org/10.55461/SJEU6160].^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: OpenSeesPy PM4Sand is documented in the OpenSeesPy documentation [WEB:https://openseespydoc.readthedocs.io/...], the PM4Sand model report by Boulanger and Ziotopoulou (2015) [WEB:https://faculty.engineering.ucdavis.edu/boulanger/...], and the PEER verification report by Chen and Arduino (2021) [DOI:10.55461/SJEU6160]. All stated capabilities are verifiable from these sources.]\n\nIntegration of the closed-form strain pipeline from the KB [KB:code.md][@Ref001] into the Python-based pore pressure workflow can proceed along two paths. In the direct input path, the analytical acceleration field from `computeAccelField` defines the input motion at each soil node in the OpenSeesPy model, and PM4Sand processes the resulting strain increments to produce the pore pressure response at each depth. In the post-processing path, `computeStrainHistory` produces the full $\\gamma(z,t)$ matrix; the instantaneous strain increments are extracted time step by time step and supplied to the Byrne or Martin-Finn-Seed volumetric strain model implemented in Python, accumulating excess pore pressure at each depth. For the RMS-based approach, the scalar $\\gamma_{RMS}(z)$ extracted from the history provides input to the Dobry (1982) strain-based pore pressure model, returning the cycle count to liquefaction and the pore pressure ratio for a specified cycle count [DOI:10.6028/NBS.BSS.138][WEB:https://doi.org/10.17226/27536].^[Confidence: MEDIUM, Rationale: The integration pathways described are consistent with standard practice documented in NCHRP 1092 [DOI:10.17226/27536] and the OpenSeesPy documentation [WEB:https://openseespydoc.readthedocs.io/...]. The specific two-path framing is a synthesis by the analyst; while technically sound and internally consistent with the cited sources, it is not reproduced verbatim from any single reference.]\n\n---\n\n## SLOT 5: Failure Extent Criteria and Global Failure Definition in 1D Liquefaction Analysis\n\n### (a) Criteria for the Spatial Extent of Zones Where Excess Pore Pressures Exceed Critical Thresholds\n\nIn a one-dimensional liquefaction analysis driven by instantaneous shear strains, the spatial extent of failure is identified by computing the excess pore pressure ratio $r_u = \\Delta u / \\sigma'_{v0}$ at each depth and comparing it against a critical threshold. The standard engineering criterion for liquefaction triggering is $r_u \\geq 1.0$, corresponding to the condition in which effective vertical stress has been reduced to zero and the soil has lost its shear strength entirely [WEB:https://doi.org/10.3390/geosciences16020061][DOI:10.17226/27536]. For partial pore pressure generation prior to triggering, the factor of safety against liquefaction $FS = \\mathrm{CRR} \\cdot MSF / \\mathrm{CSR}$ provides the depth-by-depth criterion: $FS < 1$ identifies the liquefiable zone, and the thickness of the zone where $FS < 1$ is a primary descriptor of the spatial extent of failure [WEB:https://doi.org/10.3390/geosciences16020061]. For fine-grained materials susceptible to cyclic softening rather than full liquefaction, a lower threshold such as $r_u \\geq 0.5$ is sometimes applied in practice to identify zones of significant strength loss, though the specific threshold value varies by jurisdiction and project performance objective [WEB:https://doi.org/10.3390/geosciences16020061][DOI:10.17226/27536].^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The $r_u \\geq 1$ criterion and the $FS < 1$ equivalence are universally documented in the geotechnical earthquake engineering literature. NCHRP 1092 [DOI:10.17226/27536] and the 2026 Geosciences review [DOI:10.3390/geosciences16020061] both confirm these criteria. The $r_u \\geq 0.5$ threshold for cyclic softening is also consistent with common practice, though the specific value is jurisdiction-dependent.]\n\n### (b) Point Failure versus Failure of a Proportion of the Stratum Thickness\n\nThe question of whether failure at a single depth is equivalent to failure across a proportion of the stratum depends on the engineering performance objective and on empirical evidence regarding the conditions required for surface manifestation of liquefaction. Point failure ($FS < 1$ at a single depth) is a necessary but not sufficient condition for observable ground surface damage; theoretical and field evidence establishes that a liquefiable zone must achieve a minimum thickness to propagate its effects to the surface [WEB:https://doi.org/10.17226/27536]. Ishihara (1985) formalized this concept through the H1-H2 boundary curves, which define the minimum thickness $H_2$ of a liquefiable layer, as a function of PGA and the overlying non-liquefiable crust thickness $H_1$, required to produce surface manifestations; layers thinner than the H1-H2 boundary for the applicable PGA level may liquefy internally without surface expression [WEB:https://doi.org/10.1193/031813EQS070M]. Field observations from the Canterbury earthquake sequence (2010-2011), compiled from approximately 7,500 CPT investigations, confirmed that thin liquefied sublayers within a competent crust rarely produced surface damage, while thicker zones beneath a thin crust consistently caused settlement, lateral spreading, and sand ejecta [WEB:https://doi.org/10.1193/031813EQS070M]. The specific threshold of 30% of stratum thickness as a failure criterion does not correspond to a single universally cited primary standard; it appears in practice guidelines as a heuristic for defining a globally significant liquefied zone relative to the total stratum but has not been codified in a DOI-bearing primary reference identified in this review [WEB:https://doi.org/10.17226/27536].^[Confidence: MEDIUM, Rationale: The Ishihara (1985) H1-H2 framework and the Canterbury observations [DOI:10.1193/031813EQS070M] are well documented. The 30% threshold claim is assessed against NCHRP 1092 [DOI:10.17226/27536] as the closest current authoritative reference, but the specific 30% figure was not traced to a primary DOI-bearing paper in the sources reviewed; confidence is MEDIUM accordingly.]\n\n### (c) Engineering Significance of Deep versus Shallow Failure Zones\n\nFailure at depth does not carry the same engineering significance as failure at shallow depths; this asymmetry is a foundational design principle embedded in all depth-weighted liquefaction assessment indices. The physical rationale is that liquefied zones close to the surface directly control bearing capacity failure of shallow foundations, the driving mechanism for lateral spreading, and the volumetric reconsolidation that produces settlement [WEB:https://doi.org/10.1193/031813EQS070M][DOI:10.1016/j.sandf.2015.06.010]. A liquefied zone at 20 m depth may be hydraulically isolated from the surface by an overlying competent crust; under these conditions surface damage may not occur even if $r_u = 1$ at depth, provided the crust thickness $H_1$ exceeds the Ishihara H1-H2 limit for the applicable PGA [WEB:https://doi.org/10.1193/031813EQS070M]. The Liquefaction Potential Index (LPI) introduced by Iwasaki et al. (1978, 1982) quantifies this depth dependence through the linear weighting function $w(z) = 10 - 0.5z$ for $z$ in meters (zero at 20 m depth), integrated over the top 20 m of the profile [WEB:https://doi.org/10.3390/geosciences16020061][DOI:10.1016/j.sandf.2015.06.010]:\n\n$$LPI = \\int_0^{20\\,\\mathrm{m}} F(z)\\,w(z)\\,dz$$\n\nwhere $F(z) = 1 - FS(z)$ when $FS(z) < 1$ and $F(z) = 0$ otherwise. This formulation assigns ten times more weight to the near-surface meter than to the zone at 18 m depth, capturing the empirically observed primacy of shallow failure in producing surface damage [WEB:https://doi.org/10.3390/geosciences16020061].^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The LPI formula and the Iwasaki et al. (1978, 1982) depth weighting function are well-established primary contributions, confirmed consistently across multiple secondary references including Maurer et al. (2015) [DOI:10.1016/j.sandf.2015.06.010] and the 2026 Geosciences review [DOI:10.3390/geosciences16020061]. The physical explanation of why shallow liquefaction is more consequential is supported by the van Ballegooy et al. (2014) Canterbury field observations [DOI:10.1193/031813EQS070M].]\n\n### (d) Design and Analysis Criteria Defining Global Failure\n\nAn alternative depth-weighting scheme with hyperbolic rather than linear decay is provided by the Liquefaction Severity Number (LSN), developed from the Canterbury earthquake sequence field database [WEB:https://doi.org/10.1193/031813EQS070M][WEB:https://ccc.govt.nz/assets/Documents/Environment/Land/CCC-Liquefaction-ReportBody.pdf]:\n\n$$LSN = \\int_0^{z_{max}} \\frac{\\varepsilon_v(z)}{z}\\,dz$$\n\nwhere $\\varepsilon_v(z)$ is the volumetric reconsolidation strain at depth $z$ and the $1/z$ weighting is hyperbolic [WEB:https://doi.org/10.1193/031813EQS070M]. This weighting assigns more aggressive penalization to shallow failure zones than LPI; the hyperbolic kernel approaches infinity as $z \\to 0$, producing an index that is highly sensitive to near-surface pore pressure generation. Van Ballegooy et al. (2014) validated LSN against observed residential land damage from the Canterbury events and found LSN thresholds of approximately 15-30 corresponding to minor damage, 30-50 to moderate damage, and above 50 to severe damage [DOI:10.1193/031813EQS070M]. A comparative evaluation by Maurer et al. (2015) using a global case history database found that neither LPI nor LSN is universally superior, with relative performance depending on site depth profiles, earthquake characteristics, and the specific damage reference level [DOI:10.1016/j.sandf.2015.06.010].^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The LSN definition and the Canterbury validation are documented in primary sources: the Tonkin and Taylor (2013) report [WEB:https://ccc.govt.nz/...] and van Ballegooy et al. (2014) [DOI:10.1193/031813EQS070M]. The LSN thresholds and the Maurer et al. (2015) comparison [DOI:10.1016/j.sandf.2015.06.010] are from peer-reviewed journal papers. The description is accurate and well supported.]\n\nGlobal failure of a stratum is conventionally defined through threshold values of the depth-integrated index rather than through any single depth criterion. For LPI, the Iwasaki et al. (1982) calibration against Japanese field damage established the following severity thresholds: $LPI > 15$ corresponds to very high liquefaction risk with a high probability of surface manifestation; $5 < LPI \\leq 15$ to high risk; $0 < LPI \\leq 5$ to low risk; and $LPI = 0$ to negligible risk [WEB:https://doi.org/10.3390/geosciences16020061][DOI:10.1016/j.sandf.2015.06.010]. These thresholds apply to the stratum as a whole and constitute the primary engineering design criteria for global liquefaction potential assessment. In design practice a stratum is considered globally failed when the applicable index exceeds the threshold for the performance objective, not merely when $FS < 1$ at any isolated depth. A proxy model that predicts $r_u = 1$ at a single thin sublayer at 18 m depth may produce $LPI < 5$ when integrated over the full profile with the Iwasaki weighting, indicating low global failure risk despite local triggering. The NCHRP 1092 (2024) guidelines recommend that site-specific 1D effective-stress analyses always report depth-integrated indices in addition to point-by-point $r_u$ profiles, providing a globally consistent failure metric for comparison with performance objectives [DOI:10.17226/27536].^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The LPI threshold values from Iwasaki et al. (1982) and their interpretation are consistently documented across multiple peer-reviewed sources including Maurer et al. (2015) [DOI:10.1016/j.sandf.2015.06.010] and the 2026 review [DOI:10.3390/geosciences16020061]. The NCHRP 1092 recommendation for depth-integrated reporting is stated explicitly in that report [DOI:10.17226/27536]. The illustrative example (LPI < 5 for a thin deep sublayer) is a direct arithmetic consequence of the LPI weighting function stated above and is presented as illustrative only.]\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":60994,"cache_read_input_tokens":0,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":60994,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":6,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"2e84c092-3450-428f-a602-fb47a8d85d56","uuid":"fd89b0f0-2ae5-4002-ba6a-f7eb2a750e59"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01DKmVmkbounteUecXJY8Yu8","type":"tool_result","content":"File created successfully at: /home/averri/github/tools/tito-examples/research/poreC/sessions/run-20260329_170024_2635/X1.research.md"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"2e84c092-3450-428f-a602-fb47a8d85d56","uuid":"bed91eff-3429-4b8b-8b7b-55efefc33803","timestamp":"2026-03-29T20:26:25.554Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"create","filePath":"/home/averri/github/tools/tito-examples/research/poreC/sessions/run-20260329_170024_2635/X1.research.md","content":"# Pore Pressure Increment Proxy Model for Saturated Fine-Grained Soils Under Seismic Loading\n\n---\n\n## SLOT 1: Numerical Strategies for Estimating Maximum Shear Strain During a Seismic Event\n\nIn a one-dimensional site-response framework, the instantaneous shear strain history $\\gamma(z,t)$ is the primary mechanical variable driving pore pressure generation in a saturated fine-grained stratum. For a stratum of thickness $H_s$ with power-law shear modulus $G(\\eta) = G_o(\\eta/H_s)^m$, where $\\eta = H_s - z$ denotes depth from the free surface and $G_o = \\rho c_o^2$ is the base shear modulus, the exact model-independent relationship between the acceleration field and the shear strain is obtained by integrating the equation of motion vertically from the zero-stress free surface [KB:models.md][@RovithisEtAl2011]:\n\n$$\\gamma(\\eta,t) = \\frac{\\rho}{G(\\eta)} \\int_0^{\\eta} a(\\eta',t)\\,d\\eta'$$\n\nThe shear stress $\\tau = G\\gamma$ at any level equals the total inertial force of the overlying soil column and is independent of the specific form of $G(z)$, which is an exact consequence of the equation of motion [KB:models.md][@RovithisEtAl2011]. The acceleration field $a(\\eta',t)$ is itself obtained by multiplying the base acceleration spectrum $A_g(\\omega)$ by the closed-form transfer function $H_a(z,\\omega)$ appropriate to the stiffness profile -- trigonometric for the uniform stratum and Bessel-function-based for the general power-law family -- and recovering the time domain via the inverse discrete Fourier transform [KB:code.md][KB:models.md][@Ref001][@Gazetas1982].^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The integral representation of shear strain and its independence from the stiffness profile are derived directly from the equation of motion and are documented precisely in models.md [@RovithisEtAl2011; @Gazetas1982]. The frequency-domain transfer functions and their R implementation are given in code.md [@Ref001]. No quantitative claims are made beyond what these sources directly support, and all expressions are internally consistent.]\n\nOnce the full instantaneous strain history $\\gamma(z,t)$ is available at any depth, a representative scalar amplitude must be extracted to drive a pore pressure accumulation model. The max-max strategy defines this representative amplitude as the absolute peak of the instantaneous history over the entire event duration:\n\n$$\\gamma_{max}(z) = \\max_{t \\in [0,T]} |\\gamma(z,t)|$$\n\nThis approach is conceptually direct and requires no further processing of the time series beyond identifying its maximum absolute value [WEB:https://doi.org/10.1193/021215EQS029MR1]. Its primary advantage is that it quantifies the single largest transient demand imposed on the soil, providing a well-defined and conservative scalar for deterministic design. The principal limitation is that an isolated large-amplitude spike may be unrepresentative of the cumulative loading imposed by a long, multi-cycle motion; for records with a pronounced impulsive character, the max-max estimate can overstate the effective strain amplitude relative to the energy content of the bulk of the cycles [WEB:https://doi.org/10.1193/021215EQS029MR1][WEB:https://doi.org/10.1193/051215EQS068M].^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The definition and principal characteristics of the max-max approach are standard in the 1D site response literature. Carlton and Tokimatsu (2016) [DOI:10.1193/021215EQS029MR1] and Kim et al. (2016) [DOI:10.1193/051215EQS068M] document the comparison between equivalent linear and nonlinear maximum shear strains, confirming both the utility and the limitation of the peak amplitude strategy.]\n\nThe root-mean-square (RMS) strategy defines the representative strain amplitude in terms of the energy content of the full history:\n\n$$\\gamma_{RMS}(z) = \\sqrt{\\frac{1}{T}\\int_0^T \\gamma^2(z,t)\\,dt}$$\n\nThis quantity is proportional to the specific energy density of the strain time series and provides a measure of average cyclic intensity over the entire event rather than a single extreme value [WEB:https://doi.org/10.1007/s10518-024-02061-1]. The RMS strain is directly related to the energy dissipated per unit volume per cycle in a hysteretically damped material and therefore reflects the cumulative fatigue loading more accurately than the single-peak maximum for records with many cycles of moderate amplitude. A long-duration Mw 8 motion with 20 cycles of moderate strain accumulates pore pressures quite differently from an Mw 6 pulse with a single large transient, even if the peak strain is comparable; the RMS metric captures this distinction [WEB:https://doi.org/10.1007/s10518-024-02061-1][WEB:https://doi.org/10.17226/27536]. The main disadvantage is sensitivity to record windowing: including low-amplitude pre- and post-event coda reduces the RMS without affecting the actual damage potential, and the result depends on the record duration selected. A frequency-content-weighted variant of the strain ratio, applied to 110 ground motions at 15 KiK-net stations, produced approximately 54% reduction in peak horizontal acceleration prediction error relative to the fixed 0.65 peak-fraction convention [WEB:https://doi.org/10.1007/s10518-024-02061-1].^[Confidence: MEDIUM, Rationale: The RMS definition and its energy interpretation are well established. The 54% error reduction figure is from Sinha (2025) [DOI:10.1007/s10518-024-02061-1], a single recent study on a specific dataset; this magnitude of improvement should not be generalized without independent validation. The description of the windowing limitation is consistent with general practice. The overall characterization of RMS advantages and disadvantages is supported by the cited sources.]\n\nThe equivalent uniform strain approach, introduced in the SHAKE program by Schnabel et al. (1972), defines the representative cyclic strain as a fixed fraction of the maximum strain:\n\n$$\\gamma_{eff}(z) = 0.65\\,\\gamma_{max}(z)$$\n\nThis fraction was adopted as an empirical approximation of the amplitude of a uniform sinusoidal history that produces the same pore pressure buildup as the actual irregular record for a motion representative of the western United States seismic environment [WEB:https://www.resolutionmineeis.us/documents/schnabel-lysmer-seed-1972][@SchnabelEtAl1972]. The advantage of this convention is simplicity: it requires only the peak strain and a single scalar multiplier, making it directly compatible with the iterative equivalent linear procedure implemented in SHAKE-family codes and in pyStrata [WEB:https://peer.berkeley.edu/sites/default/files/web_peer810_albert_r._kottke_ellen_m._rathje.pdf]. The limitation is that the 0.65 ratio is fixed and physically unjustified for arbitrary records: the appropriate fraction depends on the frequency content, the number of cycles, and the irregularity coefficient of the specific motion, and systematic errors of both signs arise when the ratio is applied universally [WEB:https://doi.org/10.1007/s10518-024-02061-1][WEB:https://doi.org/10.1193/051215EQS068M].^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The 0.65 factor and its origin in SHAKE are documented in Schnabel et al. (1972) [WEB:https://www.resolutionmineeis.us/documents/schnabel-lysmer-seed-1972] and confirmed in the Strata technical manual [WEB:https://peer.berkeley.edu/...]. The limitations of the fixed ratio are documented by Kim et al. (2016) [DOI:10.1193/051215EQS068M] and Sinha (2025) [DOI:10.1007/s10518-024-02061-1]. All stated claims are well supported.]\n\nBeyond these three scalar reduction strategies, two additional approaches are recognized in earthquake geotechnical engineering practice. The Dobry et al. (1982) cyclic strain method identifies a threshold shear strain $\\gamma_{th}$ -- approximately 0.01% for clean sands -- below which excess pore pressures do not develop regardless of the number of applied cycles; only strain excursions above $\\gamma_{th}$ contribute to pore pressure generation, so the threshold-based approach naturally filters out small-amplitude portions of the record that carry no damage potential [DOI:10.6028/NBS.BSS.138]. A cycle-counting approach based on rainflow or peak-valley enumeration of the strain time series extracts the number and amplitude distribution of significant half-cycles from the irregular record, which can then be used as input to pore pressure accumulation models in a cycle-by-cycle manner without reducing the history to a single scalar; this approach preserves the distributional information about cycle amplitudes and is the most physically rigorous entry-level method short of full time-stepping constitutive analysis [WEB:https://doi.org/10.17226/27536]. Nonlinear effective-stress time-domain analyses apply the constitutive pore pressure model at every time step directly to the instantaneous strain increment, eliminating the need for any a priori scalar reduction and preserving the full path dependence of pore pressure generation and dissipation [WEB:https://doi.org/10.1061/(ASCE)GT.1943-5606.0002191].^[Confidence: MEDIUM, Rationale: The Dobry threshold strain concept is documented in [DOI:10.6028/NBS.BSS.138], a primary reference. The rainflow cycle-counting and direct time-stepping approaches are described in NCHRP 1092 [DOI:10.17226/27536] and Olson et al. (2020) [DOI:10.1061/(ASCE)GT.1943-5606.0002191]. The description here is a synthesis of methodology drawn from multiple sources; the framing is technically sound but is not verbatim from any single reference, introducing moderate uncertainty.]\n\n---\n\n## SLOT 2: Empirical Methods for Pore Pressure Increment from PGA and Earthquake Magnitude\n\nEmpirical methods for estimating pore pressure increment from PGA and earthquake magnitude provide a first-level screening capability in geotechnical earthquake engineering practice. These methods bypass detailed constitutive modeling in favor of simplified correlations calibrated against case history databases, laboratory cyclic test results, and analytical models. The central chain of inference is: PGA and Mw determine the cyclic stress ratio (CSR) applied to the soil; the CSR, compared with the cyclic resistance ratio (CRR) of the material at the in situ state, determines whether liquefaction triggering occurs and, for pre-triggering conditions, the degree of excess pore pressure accumulation [WEB:https://doi.org/10.3390/geosciences16020061][DOI:10.17226/27536].^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The CSR-CRR framework is the universally accepted simplified procedure for liquefaction triggering and pore pressure estimation. Its structure and current state of practice are comprehensively reviewed in Idriss and Boulanger (2008) and confirmed in the 2026 MDPI Geosciences review [DOI:10.3390/geosciences16020061] and NCHRP 1092 [DOI:10.17226/27536].]\n\nSeed, Martin, and Lysmer (1976) established one of the earliest analytical frameworks for tracking pore pressure buildup and dissipation under cyclic earthquake loading [DOI:10.1061/AJGEB6.0000258]. Their formulation expresses the excess pore pressure ratio $r_u = \\Delta u / \\sigma'_{v0}$ as a continuous function of the cycle ratio $N/N_L$, where $N$ is the number of applied uniform cyclic stress cycles and $N_L$ is the number of cycles required to trigger liquefaction at the same CSR. The functional form is:\n\n$$r_u = \\frac{1}{2} + \\frac{1}{\\pi}\\arcsin\\!\\left[2\\!\\left(\\frac{N}{N_L}\\right)^{1/\\alpha} - 1\\right]$$\n\nThe shape parameter $\\alpha \\approx 0.7$ was calibrated against undrained cyclic triaxial and simple shear test data on clean sands [DOI:10.1061/AJGEB6.0000258]. Earthquake magnitude enters through $N_L$, which depends on the CSR, and through the number of equivalent uniform cycles $N_{eq}$, which scales with Mw through empirical correlations. The advantage of this model is that it provides a continuous pore pressure trajectory rather than a binary trigger criterion; its main limitation is that specification of $N_L$ at each depth requires a CRR estimate from in situ testing and calibrated liquefaction resistance correlations [WEB:https://doi.org/10.3390/geosciences16020061].^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The cycle-ratio model and its arcsin functional form are documented in Seed et al. (1976) [DOI:10.1061/AJGEB6.0000258], a well-established primary source. The $\\alpha \\approx 0.7$ calibration and the model structure are confirmed in the 2026 Geosciences review [DOI:10.3390/geosciences16020061].]\n\nDobry et al. (1982) proposed a cyclic-strain-based approach in which the driving variable for pore pressure generation is the cyclic shear strain amplitude $\\gamma_c$ rather than the cyclic stress ratio, thereby decoupling the pore pressure model from in situ resistance parameters [DOI:10.6028/NBS.BSS.138]. Below a threshold shear strain $\\gamma_{th} \\approx 0.01\\%$ for clean sands, excess pore pressures do not develop regardless of the number of cycles applied; above $\\gamma_{th}$, the excess pore pressure ratio after $N$ uniform cycles follows a power-law relationship in $\\gamma_c - \\gamma_{th}$ [DOI:10.6028/NBS.BSS.138]. The connection to PGA is indirect: PGA and Mw determine the induced cyclic stress, which, divided by the initial shear modulus, yields the cyclic strain; the pore pressure response is then evaluated against the threshold and the strain-based accumulation model. The advantage of this formulation is that it does not require separate liquefaction resistance characterization; threshold strain and power-law parameters can be obtained from standard cyclic laboratory tests. Its limitation in the proxy model context is that PGA alone is an imprecise predictor of $\\gamma_c$ at depth, because site amplification, depth-reduction factors, and frequency content all modulate the actual strain independently of PGA [DOI:10.6028/NBS.BSS.138].^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The Dobry et al. (1982) method is documented in the primary NBS Building Science Series 138 report [DOI:10.6028/NBS.BSS.138]. The threshold strain value and power-law pore pressure relationship are widely cited in the liquefaction literature and confirmed in [DOI:10.3390/geosciences16020061]. All claims derive from this primary source.]\n\nTokimatsu and Seed (1987) developed a simplified procedure linking CSR, normalized SPT blow count, and earthquake magnitude to volumetric strain and implicitly to the degree of pore pressure generation [DOI:10.1061/(ASCE)0733-9410(1987)113:8(861)]. A key contribution of this framework is the explicit quantification of the number of equivalent uniform cycles $N_{eq}$ as a function of Mw: approximately 2-3 cycles for Mw 5.5 events, increasing to approximately 15-26 cycles for Mw 8 earthquakes. This magnitude-cycle scaling is the primary mechanism through which Mw enters the pore pressure estimation within stress-based simplified procedures. The CSR is expressed as:\n\n$$\\mathrm{CSR} = 0.65\\,\\frac{a_{max}}{g}\\,\\frac{\\sigma_{v0}}{\\sigma'_{v0}}\\,r_d$$\n\nwhere $a_{max}$ is the PGA, $\\sigma_{v0}$ and $\\sigma'_{v0}$ are the total and effective vertical stresses, and $r_d$ is the depth-reduction factor [DOI:10.1061/(ASCE)0733-9410(1987)113:8(861)][WEB:https://doi.org/10.3390/geosciences16020061]. The advantage of magnitude scaling through $N_{eq}$ is that it captures the duration dependence of pore pressure accumulation: longer, larger-magnitude events impose more loading cycles at comparable PGA, resulting in higher $r_u$ values for otherwise identical site and soil conditions [WEB:https://doi.org/10.3390/geosciences16020061].^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The Tokimatsu and Seed (1987) paper [DOI:10.1061/(ASCE)0733-9410(1987)113:8(861)] is a primary peer-reviewed source. The $N_{eq}$ versus Mw values and the CSR formula are standard and reproduced correctly. The 2026 review [DOI:10.3390/geosciences16020061] confirms these relationships within the hierarchy of methods.]\n\nThe Idriss and Boulanger (2008) framework represents the current state of North American engineering practice for simplified liquefaction triggering and pore pressure evaluation [WEB:https://doi.org/10.3390/geosciences16020061]. The approach defines CSR (demand) as a function of PGA, total and effective overburden stresses, and the depth-reduction factor $r_d$, and CRR (capacity) as a function of normalized in situ index parameters (SPT blow count or CPT tip resistance), effective overburden stress, and fines content. The magnitude scaling factor:\n\n$$MSF = \\frac{10^{2.24}}{M_w^{2.56}}$$\n\nadjusts CRR from the reference magnitude 7.5 to the actual event magnitude, so that Mw enters the triggering assessment as a multiplicative correction to the resistance [WEB:https://doi.org/10.3390/geosciences16020061]. The factor of safety $FS = \\mathrm{CRR} \\cdot MSF / \\mathrm{CSR}$ provides a continuous measure of the margin against triggering; $FS < 1$ implies $r_u = 1$, while $FS > 1$ indicates partial pore pressure generation that can be quantified through the Seed-Martin-Lysmer cycle ratio model or the Dobry strain-based model [WEB:https://doi.org/10.3390/geosciences16020061][DOI:10.17226/27536].^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The Idriss-Boulanger framework is documented in the 2008 EERI monograph and updated in technical reports available from the UC Davis Center for Geotechnical Modeling. The MSF formula and FS definition are standard. The 2026 review [DOI:10.3390/geosciences16020061] and NCHRP 1092 [DOI:10.17226/27536] confirm these elements as the current state of practice.]\n\nNCHRP Research Report 1092 (2024) provides the most recent authoritative synthesis of empirical pore pressure methods, establishing guidelines for seismic site response analysis with pore water pressure generation [DOI:10.17226/27536]. The report classifies methods into three tiers: Tier 1 comprises simplified empirical correlations linking PGA and Mw directly to $r_u$ through the CSR-CRR framework; Tier 2 comprises semi-empirical constitutive models requiring laboratory-derived pore pressure generation parameters; and Tier 3 comprises advanced plasticity models such as PM4Sand that resolve pore pressure generation and dissipation at each time step. For the specific objective of a proxy model from PGA and Mw, Tier 1 methods are the appropriate entry point, with the Idriss-Boulanger (2008) triggering procedure as the current Tier 1 state of practice supplemented by Tokimatsu-Seed magnitude scaling for duration effects [DOI:10.17226/27536][WEB:https://doi.org/10.3390/geosciences16020061].^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: NCHRP 1092 [DOI:10.17226/27536] is the current (2024) peer-reviewed national research report from the National Academies Press and is the most comprehensive current reference for this classification. The tier structure and the placement of the Idriss-Boulanger and Tokimatsu-Seed approaches within Tier 1 are consistent with the report scope.]\n\n---\n\n## SLOT 3: Numerical Implementation Strategies for Pore Pressure Estimation from Instantaneous Shear Strain\n\nGiven a base acceleration history $a_g(t)$ and a one-dimensional stratum model, two broadly distinct implementation strategies permit estimation of excess pore pressure at a point within a saturated fine-grained stratum. The first is a two-stage approach that computes the full instantaneous shear strain history analytically and then applies a scalar or cycle-based pore pressure model to the resulting strain field. The second is a direct time-stepping approach that advances the pore pressure state at each time step as the coupled stress-strain-pore-pressure system evolves. Both strategies require the instantaneous shear strain $\\gamma(z,t)$ as the primary driving variable; the analytical closed-form route for computing this strain is documented in the KB through frequency-domain transfer functions and their R implementation [KB:code.md][KB:models.md][@Ref001][@RovithisEtAl2011].^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The two-strategy structure is well established in the geotechnical earthquake engineering literature and is confirmed in NCHRP 1092 [DOI:10.17226/27536]. The KB provides the analytical foundation for strain computation [KB:code.md; KB:models.md], and the identification of these two broad classes is consistent with the literature.]\n\nThe closed-form analytical route proceeds through two computational stages as implemented in the R algorithm of the KB [KB:code.md][@Ref001]. In the first stage, `computeAccelField` computes the depth-varying acceleration matrix $a(\\eta_k, t_i)$ by multiplying the base acceleration spectrum $A_g(\\omega)$ by the appropriate transfer function $H_a(z,\\omega)$ at each depth and frequency, and recovering the time domain via the inverse DFT. In the second stage, `computeStrainHistory` integrates the acceleration field vertically by cumulative trapezoidal quadrature to form the integral $\\mathcal{I}(\\eta_k,t_i) = \\int_0^{\\eta_k} a(\\eta',t_i)\\,d\\eta'$, then divides by $G(\\eta)/\\rho$ to produce the instantaneous shear strain at each requested depth [KB:code.md][@Ref001]. The approach is exact within the linear viscoelastic framework; its domain of validity is limited to strains below approximately 0.1% for fine-grained soils where modulus degradation and strain-path dependence become significant [KB:models.md][@SchnabelEtAl1972]. Once the full history $\\gamma(z,t)$ is available, pore pressure is estimated by applying a scalar model -- such as the Dobry et al. (1982) strain-based relationship or the Seed-Martin-Lysmer cycle ratio model -- to the representative strain amplitude extracted from the time series [DOI:10.6028/NBS.BSS.138][DOI:10.1061/AJGEB6.0000258].^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The two-step computational procedure is documented precisely in code.md [@Ref001], and the underlying transfer functions are derived in models.md [@Gazetas1982; @RovithisEtAl2011]. The linear viscoelastic limitation and approximate strain range of validity are stated in models.md [@SchnabelEtAl1972]. The connection to Dobry and Seed-Martin-Lysmer models follows directly from the primary sources.]\n\nAn intermediate strategy couples the equivalent linear site response procedure with a simplified pore pressure generation model. The frequency-domain transfer functions of the KB [KB:models.md][@Gazetas1982] are used iteratively: at each iteration, the effective shear strain (typically $0.65\\gamma_{max}$ per the SHAKE convention) updates the strain-compatible shear modulus and damping ratio from modulus reduction and damping curves; after convergence, the final strain profile is passed to a pore pressure accumulation model [WEB:https://doi.org/10.1061/(ASCE)GT.1943-5606.0002191]. This strategy preserves the computational simplicity of the frequency-domain approach while approximately accounting for strain-dependent soil behavior. Its limitations are the inability to model path-dependent pore pressure accumulation and the assumption that the strain amplitude is stationary over the event, which is appropriate for stationary ground motions but not for records with pronounced temporal non-stationarity [WEB:https://doi.org/10.17226/27536].^[Confidence: MEDIUM, Rationale: The equivalent linear procedure and its coupling to pore pressure models are described in Olson et al. (2020) [DOI:10.1061/(ASCE)GT.1943-5606.0002191] and NCHRP 1092 [DOI:10.17226/27536]. The limitation regarding non-stationarity and path dependence is stated in these references; the description here synthesizes the methodology across sources, introducing moderate uncertainty in the specific framing.]\n\nNonlinear effective-stress site response analysis advances the full stress-strain-pore pressure state at each time step, resolving pore pressure generation and dissipation as the strain history evolves [WEB:https://doi.org/10.1061/(ASCE)GT.1943-5606.0002191][DOI:10.17226/27536]. At each time step and each depth, the constitutive model receives the current strain increment, the current effective stress state, and the accumulated excess pore pressure, and returns the updated stress and pore pressure. This approach naturally captures the coupling between stiffness degradation, pore pressure generation, and wave propagation: as excess pore pressures build up and effective stress decreases, the computed shear modulus and wave velocity decrease in real time, producing a fully coupled dynamic response that the frequency-domain approach cannot reproduce [WEB:https://doi.org/10.1061/(ASCE)GT.1943-5606.0002191]. The computational cost is substantially higher than the closed-form frequency-domain route because the constitutive model must be evaluated at each grid point and each time step; for a stratum discretized at 200 depth increments and a 40-second record sampled at 0.01 s, this implies on the order of 800,000 constitutive model evaluations per analysis, excluding internal constitutive sub-iterations [WEB:https://doi.org/10.17226/27536].^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The effective-stress time-stepping procedure is described in Olson et al. (2020) [DOI:10.1061/(ASCE)GT.1943-5606.0002191] and NCHRP 1092 [DOI:10.17226/27536]. The coupling mechanism between effective stress and wave velocity is well established. The computational count is a straightforward arithmetic consequence of the stated parameters and is presented as illustrative; the order-of-magnitude estimate is consistent with published computational requirements for such analyses.]\n\nFor implementations intermediate between fully analytical and fully coupled constitutive analysis, the volumetric strain model of Martin, Finn, and Seed (1975) and its simplification by Byrne (1991) provide tractable semi-empirical relationships between the shear strain increment and the plastic volumetric reconsolidation strain from which excess pore pressure at constant volume is computed [WEB:https://doi.org/10.17226/27536]. The Martin-Finn-Seed formulation expresses the plastic volumetric strain increment per half-cycle as $\\Delta\\varepsilon_v = C_1(\\gamma - C_2\\varepsilon_v)^2 / (\\gamma + C_3\\varepsilon_v)$ for material constants $C_1, C_2, C_3$; excess pore pressure equals the product of the volumetric strain and the one-dimensional rebound modulus. The Byrne (1991) simplification reduces this to a two-parameter model of the form $\\Delta\\varepsilon_v / \\gamma = C_1 \\exp(-C_2\\varepsilon_v/\\gamma)$, which is numerically robust and is widely implemented in FLAC and DEEPSOIL [WEB:https://doi.org/10.17226/27536]. These models are directly compatible with the instantaneous-strain-based pipeline of the KB: the shear strain history $\\gamma(z,t)$ from `computeStrainHistory` [KB:code.md][@Ref001] provides the input to per-half-cycle volumetric strain accumulation, which is integrated over the duration of shaking to yield total excess pore pressure as a function of depth and time [WEB:https://doi.org/10.17226/27536].^[Confidence: MEDIUM, Rationale: The Martin-Finn-Seed and Byrne volumetric strain formulations are widely cited in the geotechnical engineering literature and described in NCHRP 1092 [DOI:10.17226/27536]. The functional forms presented are standard. However, the primary references (Martin et al. 1975, Byrne 1991) are conference proceedings and technical reports whose DOIs could not be confirmed in this session; the claim rests primarily on the NCHRP review as the current authoritative synthesis. Confidence is MEDIUM because the primary source DOIs are unconfirmed.]\n\n---\n\n## SLOT 4: Available R and Python Libraries for Advanced Constitutive Pore Pressure Models and 1D Site Response Integration\n\nWithin the R ecosystem, the analytical framework documented in the KB provides a complete and operational implementation of 1D linear viscoelastic site response for power-law shear modulus profiles [KB:code.md][@Ref001]. The two public functions `computeAccelField` and `computeStrainHistory` compute the depth-varying acceleration matrix and the instantaneous shear strain history, respectively, using closed-form frequency-domain transfer functions and trapezoidal depth integration. The implementation supports the uniform stratum ($m = 0$), the Gibson deposit ($m = 1$), and the full power-law family ($0 < m < 2$) through a unified calling interface, with near-surface singularities for $m > 0$ regularized by clamping $\\eta$ to $10^{-8}H_s$, returning $\\gamma = 0$ at the free surface consistently with the zero-stress boundary condition [KB:code.md][@Ref001]. As a linear viscoelastic frequency-domain implementation, this R code produces the input strain history for a downstream pore pressure model but does not itself generate pore pressures; coupling it with a pore pressure accumulation model requires additional R code that processes the long-format `data.table` output of `computeStrainHistory` and applies the chosen pore pressure constitutive relationship at each depth and time step [KB:code.md][@Ref001].^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The description of the R functions is derived directly from code.md [@Ref001], which is the authoritative KB source. All stated capabilities and limitations are directly verifiable from that document. No external claims are made in this paragraph.]\n\nNo dedicated R package for advanced constitutive pore pressure modeling -- including PM4Sand or equivalent formulations -- was identified in the software repositories surveyed for this study [WEB:https://doi.org/10.17226/27536]. Geotechnical effective-stress analysis in R remains limited to post-processing of results generated by dedicated computational engines such as OpenSees, DEEPSOIL, or FLAC, or to simplified scalar pore pressure models implemented directly by the analyst. The R ecosystem is well-suited for statistical post-processing, probabilistic hazard integration, and visualization of site response outputs, but the computational kernels for advanced soil constitutive models are written in C, C++, or Fortran and are interfaced through Python, MATLAB, or native GUI applications rather than R [WEB:https://doi.org/10.17226/27536].^[Confidence: MEDIUM, Rationale: The absence of dedicated R pore pressure packages was confirmed by searching CRAN, GitHub, and the NCHRP 1092 software survey [DOI:10.17226/27536]. The statement is based on a search that, while broad, was not exhaustive; confidence is MEDIUM because absence of evidence is not conclusive evidence of absence.]\n\nWithin the Python ecosystem, the pyStrata library -- formerly known as pySRA -- by Kottke and Millen implements one-dimensional site response analysis using both equivalent linear and random vibration theory (RVT) methods [WEB:https://zenodo.org/records/3522104][WEB:https://github.com/arkottke/pystrata]. The library provides frequency-domain transfer functions for layered profiles, strain-compatible modulus and damping updates following the standard equivalent linear iterative procedure, and probabilistic response assessment through RVT. pyStrata is the Python functional equivalent of the KB R implementation for equivalent linear analyses; it produces the effective shear strain profile at each iteration, which the analyst passes to a separately implemented pore pressure model, since pyStrata does not include pore pressure generation constitutive functions [WEB:https://github.com/arkottke/pystrata]. The library is open-source under the MIT license, maintained on GitHub, and distributed via PyPI.^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The description of pyStrata is based on the public GitHub repository [WEB:https://github.com/arkottke/pystrata] and Zenodo release record [WEB:https://zenodo.org/records/3522104], both publicly accessible. The stated capabilities and limitations match the documented functionality of the library.]\n\nPySeismoSoil, developed by the Geoquake Research Group at Caltech (Shi, Asimaki, and collaborators), is an open-source Python library for nonlinear total-stress 1D seismic site response analysis [WEB:https://github.com/Caltech-geoquake/PySeismoSoil]. The library implements the hybrid hyperbolic nonlinear soil model of Shi and Asimaki (2017), which transitions smoothly from a stiffness-controlled small-strain response to a strength-controlled large-strain response without requiring modulus reduction and damping curves as separate input [WEB:https://github.com/Caltech-geoquake/PySeismoSoil]. PySeismoSoil operates in total stress and does not include an integrated effective-stress pore pressure generation model; its output is the nonlinear shear strain time history at each depth, which can be passed to a separately coded pore pressure constitutive model. The library is particularly suited for probabilistic site response studies through its interface to randomized shear wave velocity profiles and ground motion selection tools [WEB:https://github.com/Caltech-geoquake/PySeismoSoil].^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The description is based on the public GitHub repository [WEB:https://github.com/Caltech-geoquake/PySeismoSoil] and the Shi and Asimaki (2017) journal paper. The stated capabilities (nonlinear total stress, no pore pressure model) are consistent with the repository documentation as reviewed.]\n\nThe most complete Python-based environment for advanced constitutive pore pressure modeling within a 1D site response workflow is OpenSeesPy coupled with the PM4Sand effective-stress constitutive model [WEB:https://openseespydoc.readthedocs.io/en/latest/src/PM4Sand.html][WEB:https://doi.org/10.55461/SJEU6160]. PM4Sand, developed by Boulanger and Ziotopoulou (2015, 2017), is a stress-ratio-controlled, fabric-dilatancy plasticity model calibrated to reproduce the undrained cyclic shear strength and post-liquefaction volumetric reconsolidation behavior of clean sands [WEB:https://faculty.engineering.ucdavis.edu/boulanger/wp-content/uploads/sites/71/2014/09/Boulanger_Ziotopoulou_PM4Sand_Model_CGM-15-01_2015.pdf]. Its primary calibration parameters are the relative density $D_R$, the atmospheric-pressure-normalized shear modulus coefficient $G_o$, and the contraction rate parameter $h_{po}$; secondary parameters control fabric evolution and dilatancy [WEB:https://faculty.engineering.ucdavis.edu/boulanger/wp-content/uploads/sites/71/2014/09/Boulanger_Ziotopoulou_PM4Sand_Model_CGM-15-01_2015.pdf]. In OpenSeesPy, PM4Sand is available as `nDMaterial PM4Sand` and is embedded in a 1D effective-stress site response model by assigning the material to a column of `SSPquad` or `quad` elements with undrained boundary conditions; the resulting time history of excess pore pressure ratio $r_u(z,t)$ is available for post-processing at each node [WEB:https://openseespydoc.readthedocs.io/en/latest/src/freeFieldEffective.html]. Chen and Arduino (2021) documented the verification and validation of PM4Sand in OpenSees against laboratory and centrifuge datasets, confirming the adequacy of the implementation for engineering applications [WEB:https://doi.org/10.55461/SJEU6160].^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: OpenSeesPy PM4Sand is documented in the OpenSeesPy documentation [WEB:https://openseespydoc.readthedocs.io/...], the PM4Sand model report by Boulanger and Ziotopoulou (2015) [WEB:https://faculty.engineering.ucdavis.edu/boulanger/...], and the PEER verification report by Chen and Arduino (2021) [DOI:10.55461/SJEU6160]. All stated capabilities are verifiable from these sources.]\n\nIntegration of the closed-form strain pipeline from the KB [KB:code.md][@Ref001] into the Python-based pore pressure workflow can proceed along two paths. In the direct input path, the analytical acceleration field from `computeAccelField` defines the input motion at each soil node in the OpenSeesPy model, and PM4Sand processes the resulting strain increments to produce the pore pressure response at each depth. In the post-processing path, `computeStrainHistory` produces the full $\\gamma(z,t)$ matrix; the instantaneous strain increments are extracted time step by time step and supplied to the Byrne or Martin-Finn-Seed volumetric strain model implemented in Python, accumulating excess pore pressure at each depth. For the RMS-based approach, the scalar $\\gamma_{RMS}(z)$ extracted from the history provides input to the Dobry (1982) strain-based pore pressure model, returning the cycle count to liquefaction and the pore pressure ratio for a specified cycle count [DOI:10.6028/NBS.BSS.138][WEB:https://doi.org/10.17226/27536].^[Confidence: MEDIUM, Rationale: The integration pathways described are consistent with standard practice documented in NCHRP 1092 [DOI:10.17226/27536] and the OpenSeesPy documentation [WEB:https://openseespydoc.readthedocs.io/...]. The specific two-path framing is a synthesis by the analyst; while technically sound and internally consistent with the cited sources, it is not reproduced verbatim from any single reference.]\n\n---\n\n## SLOT 5: Failure Extent Criteria and Global Failure Definition in 1D Liquefaction Analysis\n\n### (a) Criteria for the Spatial Extent of Zones Where Excess Pore Pressures Exceed Critical Thresholds\n\nIn a one-dimensional liquefaction analysis driven by instantaneous shear strains, the spatial extent of failure is identified by computing the excess pore pressure ratio $r_u = \\Delta u / \\sigma'_{v0}$ at each depth and comparing it against a critical threshold. The standard engineering criterion for liquefaction triggering is $r_u \\geq 1.0$, corresponding to the condition in which effective vertical stress has been reduced to zero and the soil has lost its shear strength entirely [WEB:https://doi.org/10.3390/geosciences16020061][DOI:10.17226/27536]. For partial pore pressure generation prior to triggering, the factor of safety against liquefaction $FS = \\mathrm{CRR} \\cdot MSF / \\mathrm{CSR}$ provides the depth-by-depth criterion: $FS < 1$ identifies the liquefiable zone, and the thickness of the zone where $FS < 1$ is a primary descriptor of the spatial extent of failure [WEB:https://doi.org/10.3390/geosciences16020061]. For fine-grained materials susceptible to cyclic softening rather than full liquefaction, a lower threshold such as $r_u \\geq 0.5$ is sometimes applied in practice to identify zones of significant strength loss, though the specific threshold value varies by jurisdiction and project performance objective [WEB:https://doi.org/10.3390/geosciences16020061][DOI:10.17226/27536].^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The $r_u \\geq 1$ criterion and the $FS < 1$ equivalence are universally documented in the geotechnical earthquake engineering literature. NCHRP 1092 [DOI:10.17226/27536] and the 2026 Geosciences review [DOI:10.3390/geosciences16020061] both confirm these criteria. The $r_u \\geq 0.5$ threshold for cyclic softening is also consistent with common practice, though the specific value is jurisdiction-dependent.]\n\n### (b) Point Failure versus Failure of a Proportion of the Stratum Thickness\n\nThe question of whether failure at a single depth is equivalent to failure across a proportion of the stratum depends on the engineering performance objective and on empirical evidence regarding the conditions required for surface manifestation of liquefaction. Point failure ($FS < 1$ at a single depth) is a necessary but not sufficient condition for observable ground surface damage; theoretical and field evidence establishes that a liquefiable zone must achieve a minimum thickness to propagate its effects to the surface [WEB:https://doi.org/10.17226/27536]. Ishihara (1985) formalized this concept through the H1-H2 boundary curves, which define the minimum thickness $H_2$ of a liquefiable layer, as a function of PGA and the overlying non-liquefiable crust thickness $H_1$, required to produce surface manifestations; layers thinner than the H1-H2 boundary for the applicable PGA level may liquefy internally without surface expression [WEB:https://doi.org/10.1193/031813EQS070M]. Field observations from the Canterbury earthquake sequence (2010-2011), compiled from approximately 7,500 CPT investigations, confirmed that thin liquefied sublayers within a competent crust rarely produced surface damage, while thicker zones beneath a thin crust consistently caused settlement, lateral spreading, and sand ejecta [WEB:https://doi.org/10.1193/031813EQS070M]. The specific threshold of 30% of stratum thickness as a failure criterion does not correspond to a single universally cited primary standard; it appears in practice guidelines as a heuristic for defining a globally significant liquefied zone relative to the total stratum but has not been codified in a DOI-bearing primary reference identified in this review [WEB:https://doi.org/10.17226/27536].^[Confidence: MEDIUM, Rationale: The Ishihara (1985) H1-H2 framework and the Canterbury observations [DOI:10.1193/031813EQS070M] are well documented. The 30% threshold claim is assessed against NCHRP 1092 [DOI:10.17226/27536] as the closest current authoritative reference, but the specific 30% figure was not traced to a primary DOI-bearing paper in the sources reviewed; confidence is MEDIUM accordingly.]\n\n### (c) Engineering Significance of Deep versus Shallow Failure Zones\n\nFailure at depth does not carry the same engineering significance as failure at shallow depths; this asymmetry is a foundational design principle embedded in all depth-weighted liquefaction assessment indices. The physical rationale is that liquefied zones close to the surface directly control bearing capacity failure of shallow foundations, the driving mechanism for lateral spreading, and the volumetric reconsolidation that produces settlement [WEB:https://doi.org/10.1193/031813EQS070M][DOI:10.1016/j.sandf.2015.06.010]. A liquefied zone at 20 m depth may be hydraulically isolated from the surface by an overlying competent crust; under these conditions surface damage may not occur even if $r_u = 1$ at depth, provided the crust thickness $H_1$ exceeds the Ishihara H1-H2 limit for the applicable PGA [WEB:https://doi.org/10.1193/031813EQS070M]. The Liquefaction Potential Index (LPI) introduced by Iwasaki et al. (1978, 1982) quantifies this depth dependence through the linear weighting function $w(z) = 10 - 0.5z$ for $z$ in meters (zero at 20 m depth), integrated over the top 20 m of the profile [WEB:https://doi.org/10.3390/geosciences16020061][DOI:10.1016/j.sandf.2015.06.010]:\n\n$$LPI = \\int_0^{20\\,\\mathrm{m}} F(z)\\,w(z)\\,dz$$\n\nwhere $F(z) = 1 - FS(z)$ when $FS(z) < 1$ and $F(z) = 0$ otherwise. This formulation assigns ten times more weight to the near-surface meter than to the zone at 18 m depth, capturing the empirically observed primacy of shallow failure in producing surface damage [WEB:https://doi.org/10.3390/geosciences16020061].^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The LPI formula and the Iwasaki et al. (1978, 1982) depth weighting function are well-established primary contributions, confirmed consistently across multiple secondary references including Maurer et al. (2015) [DOI:10.1016/j.sandf.2015.06.010] and the 2026 Geosciences review [DOI:10.3390/geosciences16020061]. The physical explanation of why shallow liquefaction is more consequential is supported by the van Ballegooy et al. (2014) Canterbury field observations [DOI:10.1193/031813EQS070M].]\n\n### (d) Design and Analysis Criteria Defining Global Failure\n\nAn alternative depth-weighting scheme with hyperbolic rather than linear decay is provided by the Liquefaction Severity Number (LSN), developed from the Canterbury earthquake sequence field database [WEB:https://doi.org/10.1193/031813EQS070M][WEB:https://ccc.govt.nz/assets/Documents/Environment/Land/CCC-Liquefaction-ReportBody.pdf]:\n\n$$LSN = \\int_0^{z_{max}} \\frac{\\varepsilon_v(z)}{z}\\,dz$$\n\nwhere $\\varepsilon_v(z)$ is the volumetric reconsolidation strain at depth $z$ and the $1/z$ weighting is hyperbolic [WEB:https://doi.org/10.1193/031813EQS070M]. This weighting assigns more aggressive penalization to shallow failure zones than LPI; the hyperbolic kernel approaches infinity as $z \\to 0$, producing an index that is highly sensitive to near-surface pore pressure generation. Van Ballegooy et al. (2014) validated LSN against observed residential land damage from the Canterbury events and found LSN thresholds of approximately 15-30 corresponding to minor damage, 30-50 to moderate damage, and above 50 to severe damage [DOI:10.1193/031813EQS070M]. A comparative evaluation by Maurer et al. (2015) using a global case history database found that neither LPI nor LSN is universally superior, with relative performance depending on site depth profiles, earthquake characteristics, and the specific damage reference level [DOI:10.1016/j.sandf.2015.06.010].^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The LSN definition and the Canterbury validation are documented in primary sources: the Tonkin and Taylor (2013) report [WEB:https://ccc.govt.nz/...] and van Ballegooy et al. (2014) [DOI:10.1193/031813EQS070M]. The LSN thresholds and the Maurer et al. (2015) comparison [DOI:10.1016/j.sandf.2015.06.010] are from peer-reviewed journal papers. The description is accurate and well supported.]\n\nGlobal failure of a stratum is conventionally defined through threshold values of the depth-integrated index rather than through any single depth criterion. For LPI, the Iwasaki et al. (1982) calibration against Japanese field damage established the following severity thresholds: $LPI > 15$ corresponds to very high liquefaction risk with a high probability of surface manifestation; $5 < LPI \\leq 15$ to high risk; $0 < LPI \\leq 5$ to low risk; and $LPI = 0$ to negligible risk [WEB:https://doi.org/10.3390/geosciences16020061][DOI:10.1016/j.sandf.2015.06.010]. These thresholds apply to the stratum as a whole and constitute the primary engineering design criteria for global liquefaction potential assessment. In design practice a stratum is considered globally failed when the applicable index exceeds the threshold for the performance objective, not merely when $FS < 1$ at any isolated depth. A proxy model that predicts $r_u = 1$ at a single thin sublayer at 18 m depth may produce $LPI < 5$ when integrated over the full profile with the Iwasaki weighting, indicating low global failure risk despite local triggering. The NCHRP 1092 (2024) guidelines recommend that site-specific 1D effective-stress analyses always report depth-integrated indices in addition to point-by-point $r_u$ profiles, providing a globally consistent failure metric for comparison with performance objectives [DOI:10.17226/27536].^[Confidence: HIGH, Rationale: The LPI threshold values from Iwasaki et al. (1982) and their interpretation are consistently documented across multiple peer-reviewed sources including Maurer et al. (2015) [DOI:10.1016/j.sandf.2015.06.010] and the 2026 review [DOI:10.3390/geosciences16020061]. The NCHRP 1092 recommendation for depth-integrated reporting is stated explicitly in that report [DOI:10.17226/27536]. The illustrative example (LPI < 5 for a thin deep sublayer) is a direct arithmetic consequence of the LPI weighting function stated above and is presented as illustrative only.]\n","structuredPatch":[],"originalFile":null}}
